      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <p>A"Ty 72Nri°N 1111 c DONAU'S PATENT SELF RMSINC FLEUR PASTRY. THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 4, 1913.</p>
        <p>children food and the shelter. That was the fourth family she had taken in. The Chairman said the magistrates appre- ciated the way in which the woman had acted and publicly thanked her for the kindness she had extended to poor people. On behalf of the society they would also like to thank her. Taylor would go to prison for six months.</p>
        <p>tolerated by the police.—The case was adjourned for a week.</p>
        <p>FRIDAY.</p>
        <p>Before the Mayor (in the chair), Aldermen Smethurst, Bolton and Ilierboint,..Messrs. G.</p>
        <p>Pilling and S. Wright. A STRANOE CONFESS/ON.--ThOMW3 Richardson, 59, King-street, West Bromwich, was charged on remand with havipg been drunk' a week,ago.— The Chief Constable said that at first prisoner had said that he murdered his three-year-old daughter at Stockton-on-Tees three years ago. There was no truth in that statement, but the police had had to go to considerable trouble and expense. Prisoner ought to be taught a lesson. —A fine of 10s., including costs, was imposed, with an alternative of seven days' imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>IN THE COURTS.</head>
        <p>IN THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>CARE OF THE SCHOLARS.</p>
        <p>The Responsibility of Parents.</p>
        <p>The difficulties which the Education Com- mittee•meet with in getting parents to see their proper responsibilities in regard to the care of their children were instanced at the Borough Police Court on Tuesday by Mr. A. T. Hallaway (deputy town clerk). Before Councillor Henry Roberts in the chair) and other magistrates, Edward Connor, 5, Windsor-street, was summoned for the alleged wilful neglect of his daughter. Mr. Hallaway said that the summons had been taken out by the Education Committee and was for failing to provide proper medical aid for his child, who was very shortsighted and had a squint. The parents were told about this a considerable time ago, and on May Ist a pre- scription was given by the Infirmary for a pair of spectacles, at a cost of 3s. The father was earning good wages, and the Education Com- mittee decided that he should pay 3s. in weekly instalments of is. Only is. had been paid and there had been repeated visits to the parents by officers of the Education Authority, and letters had been written by the Director of Educb.tion and the Town Clerk. He (Mr. Hallaway) asked that the case should be adjourned for a week, as the parents now seemed to see their position. • They were prepared to buy the spectacles and pay the cost of the summons. He (Mr. Halle- way) would like to take that opportunity of referring to the great difficulties which the Education Committee had in getting some parents to see their responsibilities. There were at least twelve cases at the present time in which the Education Committee had been pressing parents to look after she eyesight cif their children. They either did net care, pr thought that the Education Committee were not in a position to enforce them to provide proper attention. As a matter of patience %Job would have to take a second place- to the Education Committee in those cases. That patience Iphd become exhausted. and in future all oases. itftAr reasonable time had been given, would be brought before the magistrates. The case was adjourned for a week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>NEGLECTFUL PARENT.</head>
        <p>A NEGLECTFUL PARENT.</p>
        <p>Sad Case ip_Warrington.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Wednesday, before Mr. J. W. Hayward (in the chair) and other magistrates, Thomas Taylor, Admiral- street, was charged with having been drunk, and also with neglecting his three children. P.C. 12allard said that prisoner was drunk in Napier-street the previous day and was sur- rounded by a large crowd of women and chil- dren. He was shouting: "I will not go; I want my wife." He refused to pull himself together, and on the way to the Police Station he became very violent. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne, who prosecuted on behalf of the N.S.P.C.C., said Inspector Wainwright had had the case under his supervision since April. The man was a pensioner, and every pension day he spent the greater part of his money—X2 12s. 6d.— in drink. The last time he gave his wife 7s. 6d. out of it. He had had to give up his work at several places during the last few months entirely for his laziness or impudence towards the managers. Notice for them to quit the house in Admiral-street expired on Monday, and Inspector Wainwright found furniture in the backyard. There was no food, and a Mrs. Haslam, of Napier-street, took the woman and children in and fed them, afterwards allowing them to stay the night. The man never went to see his wife until the previous day. The woman and children were half-starved, and the relieving officers, Inspector Wainwright and neighbours had given them food. Prisoner knew perfectly well what to expect, for he had had-a month's imprisonment when prosecuted by the Guardians. The wife said it was no use giving him any more chtnces; he either would not work, and if he did work he got discharged ler impudence, or was too lazy to work. When pension day came round he did nothing but drink. When charged he said: "I thought they would charge me with it." Mrs. Ma'am deposed to giving tire woman and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</head>
        <p>BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Mr. It. T. Fairclough (in the chair), Alderman Tinnion, Councillor Locker, Mr. A. J. Jolley, and Mr. T. Pemberton. CHASED THE LADlEs.—James Connor, farm labourer, of no fixed abode, got too much drink, and was seen by the police to be throwing his arms about and trying to catch hold of the ladies he met. The police locked him up for being drunk and disorderly. He was fined ss. "Yss, SHURE."—Henry Seymour, no fixed abode, was charged with peddling books without a hawker's licence.—"Shure," said Henry, ,"they are only A. B: C. books for children."—The Chief Constable: Yes, but this is often a pretext for begging.—The Chairman: Will you clear out of the town if we give you a chance.—Seymour: Yes. sur, ehure.—He cleared. THOM:MT OF LEAvlso.—Arthur Jones was found helplessly drunk in Winwic,k-street.---Sones : I was just thinking of leaving the town when the police took me.—The Chief Constable: You wel4e not in a condition even to think.—Jones was fined ss.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>MONDAY.</head>
        <p>MONDAY.</p>
        <p>Before the Mayor (in the chair), Alderman Smethurst, Alderman Bolton, Mr. W. Roberts and Mr. S. Wright. BEGGING.—James Hopkins, no fixed abode, got drunk on Saturday and annoyed people in San- key-street by asking people for "the price of a pint." The police took him to the lock-up. There were 43 previous convictions against him, and lie was sentenced to 14 days' hard labour. COMA) NOT STAY :THE PACE.—Thomas Mitchell admitted being drunk, but not incapable, in Bridge-street on Saturday.—The Chief Con- stable: He alleges that the cause of his downfall was that he started drinking too early in the day and could not stay the pace for the rest of the day. (Laughter.)—Fined 55., including costs.</p>
        <p>DESERTED His FAmn.Y.—Norman Price was charged with deserting his wife and children. "Guilty to leaving my children," he answered, "but I have no wife."—Mr. B. Shearer, for the Guardians, said Price denied that he was mar- ried, but the woman was undoubtedly his wife. He was sent to prison for three months, with hard labour.</p>
        <p>CARRIED 300 YARDS.—Found by the police drunk in Winwick-street, Patrick McLaughlin lay. down and refused to get up. The officers had to carry him 300 yards to the lock-up.—The Chief Constable said he had got damages for injuries to his hand, but he had spent the money in drink. He was a perfect nuisance in the town.— The Mayor: Evidently he is suffering from no injury to his throat when he drinks so.— Patrick was sent to prison for 14 days, with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>WEDNESDAY.</head>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Mr. J. W. Hayward (in the chair), Alderman Smethurst, Messrs. W. T. Williams</p>
        <p>and C. Jackson Holmes.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED WIFE ASSAULT.—Joseph Clancey. Dial- street, was charged with assaulting his wife the previous day.—The Chief Constable said that prisoner was walking along Buttermarket-street. His wife, Catherine, was following him. Detec- tive Clough saw the prisoner deliberately strike his wife a violent blow in the face, knocking her to the ground. The woman was suffering' from injuries which she had evidently received prior to the assault mentioned. She was practically insensible and was taken to the Infirmary, and three stitches were placed in her wrist, and her face was wrapped up. Earlier in the day it was thought that the woman was in her sister-in- law's house drinking. The husband came home, and, his meal not being ready, a row ensued. He struck his wife, who stated that she picked a cup un for the purpose of striking him. She missed him and stated that she bit her own hand. It was a most violent assault, as the woman wsi in a delicate state of health. He (the Chief Constable) thought she would endea- vour to screen the man, but this would not be</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>COUNTY PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>COUNTY PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Messrs. Roger C. Parr (in the chair), C. E. Parker, G. Mather, and J. Ball. LICENCE TRANSFEHS.—James Patrick McHale and John Derbyshire' were granted the transfers of the licences of the Black Swan Hotel, Glaze- brook, and one in Houghton Green, respectively. IN THE CANAL.—Alfred Marsh, Woolston Lock, Woolston, was charged with attempting to com- mit suicide by throwing himself into the Bridge- water Canal at Woolston the previous day.—lt would appear that prisoner had been suffering from nervous debility for some time, and had been attended by Drs. Langdale and Anderson for about five weeks.—Albert ,Marsh, labourer, employed by the Manchester Ship Canal Com- pany, said the prisoner, who was his father, rushed past the house about 8.15 the previous morning. He jumped into the canal. He (wit- ness), along with a man named Platt, got prisoner out of the water. Prisoner said: "I have done. it and I don't think I shall get better." The water was about six feet deep.— The wife, Rebecca Marsh, said they had been married 31 years. There was a small family trouble, but her husband would not say what was upsetting him. He had attended the locks for 15 years and only gave UD the work a fort- night ago.—P.C. Kay said that prisoner gave himself up and said: "I have done it and I shall have to suffer; I was upset and I did it."—PH- sonar said he was irritated and jumped in.—He was committed to the Workhouse for 14 days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>PADGATE BOWLER'S SUCCESS.</head>
        <p>PADGATE BOWLER'S SUCCESS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Charles Roberts In Blackpool</p>
        <p>Bowling Tournament.</p>
        <p>taken in bowling circles n ir tbheepgerformances of Mr. Chas. Roberts,genial in ial landlord of the Farmers' iA%o7ral7eusr:, tl'lstienatgill•tnelili interesteg i aae d. It will be remembered that a few weeks ago we gave an account of a remark- able victory he obtained over a crack bowler of Harpurhey. Mr. Charles Roberts.is taking part in the great 'annual tournament "fit Blackpool, in which there were over 1,000 entries. In the thirderound Mr. Roberts met Mr. C. Thornton, of Newton-le-Willows, who is recognised as a leading bowler in Lancashire circles. A remark- able struggle for supremacy took place and a great game was witnessed. Roberts, towards the end of the game, was leading 20 to 15, and had a .wood by the " jack." Thornton, with one of his woods, dispjaced this and made his score 16-20, and then after some good bowling scored four at the next two ends, making 20 all, and led the "'jack." He bowled his first wood near the "jack," but \Roberts, following, just beat this, and Thornton failing with his second wood to go one better, Roberts, to'the delight of his y, Roberts was d admirers, won the match. 21-20, a verdict Predit- able to both victor and vanqnished. In the fourthramii againstrou d, m playedr. F Fielding, dTi nhgu, r a bowler of somes d a reputation in Heywood. Roberts was in excel- lent form and won at the conclusion with fair ease, the score reading 21-13. - Other Warrington bowlers entered the taurnal. ment. but all have been vanquished in previous rounds, Mr. Roberts thus being the only local representative left in. There are now 64 com- petitors left to fight for the much-coveted first nrize, and all Warrington bowlers will watch Roberts's future rounds. with considerable , interest and wish him success. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>16/01/S 45-SO OW,</head>
        <p>16/01/S 45-SO OW,</p>
        <p>Bei,vsey Road Wesleyan Church.</p>
        <p>The harvest festival was held at the Bewsey- road Wesleyan. Church on Sunday. The lady members displayed great taste and skill in the scheme for decoration. The Rev. E. Morgan preached the morning sermon and his text was: "Ye are God's husbandry." The trio "While the earth remaineth " was admirably sung.</p>
        <p>At the evening service the preacher was the Rev. W. A. Beckett, the superintendent minister, who preached from: "When ye have gathered in the fruit of the land, -ye shall keep• a feast unto the Lord."</p>
        <p>In harvest messages, he said, the first was the ever-ruling providence of a personal God; the second was the benevolence of God conjoined with the co-operation of man, and the third was the duty of personal and practical thanksgiving. The anthem "Let all the people praise Thee" (Maunder) was given a particularly fine render- ing, and the solo was well accomplished by Mr. J. Hannan. Mr. T. W. Pearce efficiently acted as organist.</p>
        <p>matter is the fact that every feeling tends to become deeper by repeated indulgence. The yielding to passion may be more harmful than the injury which caused it. Similarly with hatred, lust, or any uncontrolled passion, indulgence provides food for growth. The remedy for such failings depends largely on the conditions which cause the weakness. The will must exercise its authority in one of the follow- ing ways—divergence of thought, postponement of anger, or an entire suppression of the feeling. There is, however, what may be termed a legi- timate anger. Human nature is at its best when agitated by its instincts and passions, provided only that the annoyance is justifiable. The passions were divinely implanted to be roused against all forms of evil--against cruelty, injus- tice, blasphemy, oppression, deceit and fraud. In fact, most of us are not angry enough. The men who have been the- means of uplifting the fallen, and bringing about advances in morals and ideals, in laws and practices, are the men who denounced without fear. . No words are too strong when denouncing such evils as dismissal for religion and politics, abuse of charity, the bankrupt restarting business in Holland), a large way without paying his debts, cruelty to wife or dumb creatures, sweating, white slave trafficking, &amp;c. Indignation is wrong only when excited against occurrences which involve no moral conse- quences. It is very wrong not to be angry against sin, debauchery, lust, oppression, un- faithfulness, and the like. . H. The Williamson President exti sed their appreciation of the paper and cordially i invited Mr. Appleton to favour the class with and(M r .other .. T C. ineonallbned, the Rev. another visit. Mr. William Silcoek, superintendent of the Sunday School, will be the speaker to-morrow.</p>
        <p>Workingmen's Mission, Bank Quay. Failure on the part of man or woman to recog- nise the handiwork of God in tho splendid harvest of the year, or to realise His presence meß's Mission on Sunday, when services were held. The in Nature, was to be pitied. Such was a t je dl large rngwe esi ; number oufm fruit,tbhe the the pulpit and com- sentence in the morning sermon on "Harvest United Methodist Frte College, Manchester, at Thoughts" preachedstefbuyllyMaree. decor at inunion table especially presenting an artistic lc 1, thehea vegetables evrtceai3lbt lethanksgiving s Workingav ne dr very fit tastefully w. e r s , appearance. Some very choice blooms were of which were from the conservatories of Mrs. John CrOsfield, of Colwyn Bay. At the morning service the preacher, in referring to the abund- ant harvest, showed how that, in the returning seasons, each playing its separate part in the production of the earth's gifts, we had an ex- planation of the so-called mystery of life. God was behind it all, and greater good would result wore it more widely recognised and less debated. A sad feature of the harvest was in the paradox it created, that while there was enough for all, yet we were confronted with a large amount of poverty. That might be due to an unequal division or lack of sympathy on the part of the rich to the poor, but whether or not, it was the duty, of the Church to alleviate, as far as possible, such poverty, and to bring about a unity between all men.</p>
        <p>- In the, afternoon a musical service was held. Some capital items were finely rendered by the choir; while Mr. McArthur was heard to adian. tage in his solos "When I survey" and "Behold I stand." Miss M. WlAitmore gained much appreciation in her sweet rendition of "Calvary" and "0 Divine Redeemer." Mrs. R. Tanner presided, and Mrs. J. Dodds, of Penketh, gave the address.</p>
        <p>In. the evening Mr. Jones spoke on the pre- sentation .of the very best to God. In whatever department of life, nothing was worth the giv- ing, unless it involved sacrifice, and it was in the light of the Divine sacrifice that men ought to .test their gifts. The choir rendered two Anthems in -a very -creditable manner. Mr. F. Atkinson was the conductor, while Mr. T. Tan- ner presided at the organ. On: Monday evening a fruit banquet took place, 'during which a tour through Snowdonia, by means of lantern Slide's, lent by the London and North-Western Railway Company, was con- ucted by Mr. W.Worrall. Mr. N. Hough presided over the gathering, and Mr. Sam Whitlow mani- pulated the lantern. A sale of fruit and vege- tables was afterwards conducted, the proceeds of which were devoted to the Mission League of Help work.</p>
        <p>- --.4404 (5.441 Cht , 4. • TILF -To .ri</p>
