      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>What Is Goodness P</head>
        <p>What Is Goodness P</p>
        <p>The theme of the sermon on Sunday evening at Cairo-street Chapel was " What is goodness?" This was a continuation of the sermons on "Morality and the Moral Life," preached by the Rev. Dr. Mellor.</p>
        <p>Goodness, said the preacher, consisted of an active desire to realise an ideal. There must be more in -goodness than simple avoidance of what was bad. The man was not always the best whose hands were the cleanest, nor was he necessarily the most righteous. Goodness con- sisted in active effort towards an ideal, but such effort must be sincere. The only genuine good- ness was that which flowed naturally from the pure will to goodness; the source of goodness was the sincerity of the inward life, and goodness depended on the loyalty of a man to his ideal. So it is right to tell you that it is not easy to be good, and goodness is no child's play. On the contrary, it is the hardest labour of life for strong, brave men and women. Its nature and essence, we say, lie in this, an active and loyal effort to realise an ideal! It sounds simple and perhaps easy. But try it in all sincerity and truth, and you will find you are engaged on a difficult adventure. But I think it is an adven- ture which may well call forth a response from the power and courage t native in us, and an adventure, too, which, even though it brings wounds and hard travail, brings surely a fur- ther, deeper, richer life.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A</p>
        <p>The members of the Wycliffe P.S.A. held an " open " meeting on Sunday and this was marked by a large audience. About 170 books were dis- tributed as prizes, and the meeting was gener- ally bright, brief and brotherly</p>
        <p>The Rev. T. T. Jaiss, who -presided, referred to the members' effort o increase the attendance of men at the Sunday afternoon meetings. They had sent out 30,000 circulars of invitation, and he hoped to see a large response. He wished them every success in their great effort to get at the men of the town.</p>
        <p>The address was giveil by Councillor J. Miller, of Widnes, who took as his theme: "The foxes had holes, the birds had nests, but the Son of Man had not where -to lay His head." • The poor, he said, always found a friend and helper in-Jesus. Labour He dignified all round. Christ drew many of His lessons from child-life, and His sayings/were always from nature and practical. The birds, the lilies and everyday life were His illustrations. Christ always tried to make men good, and not simply great. He in- spired men to• labour for great ideals. Mary's act to Jesus was not forgotten, but would be remembered for all timt</p>
        <p>The poor -widow's mite received His attention because of her good spirit. The giving of her all was more than all, the rest who had not given their all. The better the home the better were the chances in a e,hild's life, and a heal-. thier bodY conseeiit&amp;; Praise was. due to many parents for the. self-sacrifice they were making for the.sake of their children. All pro- perty owners Were not doing what they might to help the poor, as many houses were a dis- grace to civilisation. Miss Howell sang "Angels ever • bright and fair" and "Come unto Me," and Mr. Bailey gave "Thou art passing !fence," and the services of the soloists were highly appreciated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>Rev. H. and Mrs. Ogden.</head>
        <p>The Rev. H. and Mrs. Ogden.</p>
        <p>Reception at Colborne-st. Baptist Church.</p>
        <p>To signalise the return of the Rev. H. Ogden and Mrs. Ogden after their marriage holiday, which took the form Of 'a month's tour in Hol- land and Belgium; a reception of unusual intereii was held in connection with the Golborne-street Baptist Church and Sunday School. The schoolroom, which was beautifully decorated, Mr. Buckley, the superintendent. having displayed his customary taste, was well filled when Mr. Ben Few took the chair. Mr. Stell, one of the deacons, led in prayer, and then a handsome present was given by the church and congregation to Mr. and Mrs. Og,len, in addition to the valuable presents that were mado by individual members of the church and con- gregation. "'</p>
        <p>The presentation was made by Mr. E. Wynne Jones, who, in his racy and breezy, style. com- mented upon the work and Character of their minister. He.said it was a joy to meet in the new .schoolroom, and he trusted that the saeri - flee of to-day would result in a growth of Church and Sunday School in the future. The Rev. H. Ogden responded, and thanked the Church for their generosity and kindly thought. So much had been done by individual members in the giving of presents that ono hardly anticipated such a gift as the one of that night. There was r..f..uch scope for work now, and he trusted all would work with 'goodwill and harmony.</p>
        <p>Presentation to Mr. and Mrs. Longshaw. Thie meeting gained added interest by the pre- sentation to Mr. and Mrs. Longshaw of a lovely water-colour drawing, by Steele, of Manchester. Mr. and Mrs. Longshaw were married dome two months ago, Mrs. Longshaw being the youngest sister of the Rev. H. Ogden. The presentation was made by Mr. H. Buck- ley, the superintendent of the Sunday School, and he drew attention to Mr. Longshaw'a stead- fastness. Through all atmospheres he had stuck to his work for the Sunday School and Church, and no more loyal friends of Golborne- street than Mr. and Mrs. Longshaw could be found. , Mr. Longshaw, in responding, said he had al- ways striven to do his best for the Church and would in the future continue with that ambi- tion.</p>
        <p>An excellent programe was provided for the whole evening, but special thanks were due to Mr. E. Balinford for his violincello solos, and to Mr. Walker for his songs. After light re- freshments had been served, one of the best meetings at Golborne-street was brought to a close about 10.30 p.m.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>Bold-street Wesleyan Church.</head>
        <p>Bold-street Wesleyan Church.</p>
        <p>Men's Bible Class.</p>
        <p>The speaker at the Bible Class on Sunday afternoon was Mr. A. Appleton, of Great San- key, who .read a very excellent paper on "In- temperance of the emotions." Shakespeare causes Shylock to exclaim: "Hath not a Jew eyes? Hath not a Jew organs, dimen- sions, senses, affections, passions?" thus giving expression to the fact that mankind is essen- tially the same in physical and mental nature, despite so many varieties of rank, race and en- vironment. Nor are the " organs " and " dimen- sions " wit4out connection with the "affections" and "passions." The feelings exhibit varying degrees of intensity, and the question arises as lie. how far ought we, and can we, govern these I emotions.</p>
        <p>Each individual has what may he termed "a range of temperament," and fhe dispositions pre- sent a normal attitude, subject to the stimulus of certain environments. The range is fairly constant; but our capability to control the rise and fall is a matter of grave -moment. Exces- sive violence of feeling is a demoralisation of the whole being, and the serious aspect of the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>WOMEN'S LIBERAL ASSOCIATrONS.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S LIBERAL ASSOCIATrONS.</p>
        <p>Annual Council Meeting at Chester.</p>
        <p>• The annual Council meeting of the Lancashire and Cheshire Union of Women Liberal Associa- tions will be held on Wednesdax, October 15th, at • Chester:</p>
        <p>Mrs. Osborne, honorary secretary of the War- rington Association, is nominated for the Execu- tive, and. Mrs. Lindley represents Warrington on the General Committee. The delegates from the Warrington,Association are: Miss Crosfield, Mrs. T. Locker, Mrs. T. Pemberton, Mrs. Bunch, Mrs. M. Roberts and Miss Booth.</p>
        <p>Protection of Girls.</p>
        <p>Among the 'resolutions to be submitted are the following:—</p>
        <p>"That this' Council, while rejoicing at the passing of the White Slave Traffic Bill into law, recognises that this legislation, .unless backed up by amendment and strong public opinion, may become, as in the Piccadilly Flat case; almost a dead letter, urges as a first necessity, that the age of protection for girls be raised to 18 years, and that women police ,be appointed, . to be responsible for the cases of girls and womell exposed t 6 the temptations of the streets. And also urges that the law relating to illegitimacy be strengthened and enforced."</p>
        <p>Bider Boys also should be protected by the Criminal Law up to 18 years of age."</p>
        <p>Housing Reform and Land.</p>
        <p>"This Council expresses its opinion that no scheme of Housing Reform can be satisfactory unless based upon a just valuation of land, and is deeply gratified by the declared intention of the Government to proceed with such legisla- tion."</p>
        <p>Woman Suffrage.</p>
        <p>"This Council gratefully records its reCogni- Lion of the splendid 'support given to the Repre- sentation of the People (Women's Bill) on the 6th of May, 1913, by the 145 Liberal Members who voted for it; and whilst re-aMrming,con- tinned loyalty and support of the Liberal party In the constituencies, expresses its bitter dis- appointment at the rejection of the Bill. And this Council pledges itself to press unceasingly for this measure of justice to women until it is granted."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>LAND VALUE.</head>
        <p>LAND VALUE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wedgwood, M.P., speaking at Newcastle- under-Lyne, defined the position of the Land Values Group to, the land campaign. A mini- mum wage for agricultural labourers, rent courts, and Mr. Runciman's housing scheme were, he said, the agonised suggestions of land- lords driven id a corner, and would not cheapen land, which is the one aim of the Land Values Group. The. Group proposed to exempt build- ings and improveirtents from rate's. This did cheapen land, and, if adopted, would inamke the other schemes both palatable and innocuous.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>Quite True.</head>
        <p>Quite True.</p>
        <p>"Them pesky Suffragettes wants everything nowadays," growled Farmer Brown, peering over the Morning Star. "Yes," replied his meek spouse. "I heered, Deacon Applegate say last Sunday that soon they'd be saying 'Awoman !" at the end of a prayer."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>WAR g/ 1 /41 GTOti</head>
