      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>FARM AO GARDEN.</head>
        <p>FARM AO GARDEN.</p>
        <p>GREAT DEMAND FOR DAIRY COWS.</p>
        <p>If farmers could rely on a continuance of the present value of milking cows, the rearing of heifers Would be regarded as a more profitable pursuit than that of steers for grazing. There is indeed no doubt that such has been the case in recent years, and there is certainly no pros- pect of its being otherwise for years to comb. Let us take a few of last week's quotations. At Preston cows made from .X 25 to £26 each, at Penrith the top price was £29 10s., at Der:lf £27, and at Bristol .R 26 was realised for an Irish COW.</p>
        <p>Those who have been recently selling 1 ul- locks, whether fat or lean, will best be able to appreciate these values. Fifty years ago £2O for a good three-year-old heifer was consiOred a good price, including her calf, and it was a commbh saying in those days that there nothing in farming that paid better. Now, those of medium quality are easily fetching £23, and such prices should be alone sufficient to counteract the excessive slaughter of calves. British and Irish farmers seem scarcely to realise that in the rearing of dairy cattle they have the field to themselves.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>ROOTS AND HAY.</head>
        <p>ROOTS AND HAY.</p>
        <p>Hay and roots are two of the most important crops on the farm noi.vaditys, when so much depends on live stock. When there are, says a writer in "Farm and Home," good crops of both farmers have no anxiety as to the wintering of their stock. This year we have beautiful crops of hay, but the roots were sadly tried by the August drought. Boots have, however, won- derful powers of recovery, and they have every chance to exercise these now the rain has fallen in most districts.</p>
        <p>Swedes and turnips felt the drought more than mangels, as they always do, but they have a longer period in which to recover, since they are lifted later than mangels. Some crops have failed altogether, but many are now mak- ing a fresh start, new green leaves springing up above the old and 'yellow ones. To make the most of the swedes they should be left until the end of November, and then lifted, whether required for sheep or not. It is a wasteful prac- tice to leave them to be consumed in the ground. It saves labour, but much of the root Is wasted, some rot, and the risk is run of harm to the sheep from eating frozen roots. It is very much better to throw the roots into heaps and cover them with earth in the field. They will then keep sound, and, moreover, will ripen and make better food. Swedes that are required for cattle in the yards keep better tn clumps, like mangels, than in root houses.</p>
        <p>Mangels have less leeway to make up than swedes, but they have a shorter time in which to do it, because they must be harvested before they are endangered by severe frost. Once their, leaves turn yellow and begin to fall off there is no use in leaving them out, as they have matured and will not increase further. But so long as the leaves remain green growth continues. In most localities mangels may safely be left out until the first week in Novem- ber. As a matter of fact, they can stand a fair amount of frost, provided that they are not lifted whilst frozen. Should they be overtaken by frost, the thing to do is to leave them to recover, and then lift and clamp them promptly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>HAY GROWN AT 2.35. A TON.</head>
        <p>HAY GROWN AT 2.35. A TON.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ernest •Parke's twelfth annual report on his grass manuring experience at Kineton (Warwickshire) records an under-average crop, but a fair one for the season. In one field phosphates and nitrate of soda gave a yield of 361 cwt. of hay per acre, and in another field 40cwt., the yield of Jim adjoining plots which are kept continuously without manure being only 12 to 13 cwt. These latter plots (which, like the former, are annually hayed and the aftermath grazed off in autumn) represent the natural or unassisted yield of the clay soil on which the farm is situated. The average increase of nearly 6cwt. of hay per acre due to manuring was Obtained at an average cost of less than 30s. per acre. Mr. Parke applies the superphosphate at the rate of 3cwt. per acre, and basic slag at the rate of scwt. per acre, the latter being used by preference in the field which is poorer in lime. These fertilisers are put on in late winter. The nitrate (at the rate of licwt. per acre) is •sown in early spring. Where phosphates alone were used, the yield of hay per acre was half-a-ton less than where nitrate was used with them, but nitrite alone, as in previous years, yielded a comparatively poor crop. Dr. Dyer, who advises Mr. Parke In connection with his experiments, reports that not only the heaviest crop, but also the best, herbage, in which clovers and grasBcs flourish with equal luxuriance, is found on the plots which get both phosphates and nitra:e Potash salts, on this land, do not give n remunerative increase even after twelve years' use of phosphates and nitrate without th..ni. This is attributable to a good natural reserve of available notash in the soil, coupled, no doubt, with the fact that the land is autumn- grazed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>PLANTS TO INCREASE.</p>
        <p>Insert small portions of Lavender slipped off the old plant firmly in the open ground, six inches apart.</p>
        <p>• Among Pansies an Vic)las plenty of good cuttings are available at the base of the plants. Insert in sandy soil in a cold frame, keeping moist.</p>
        <p>Carnation layers or cuttings which are now rooted, may be planted out twelve.inches apart. Perennials may now be divided, and many of them will be all the better for being broken up into smaller sections. Better results are obtained by autumn divi- sion than spring, especially if dry weather follows the spring operation. The following may be taken in hand now : Phloxes, Campanulas, Pyrethrums, Chrysan- themum maximum, Galegas, Delphiniums, Achilleas, some Irises, and Helianthuses, Plant firmly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>SICKLY HOUSE PLANTS.</head>
        <p>SICKLY HOUSE PLANTS.</p>
        <p>The cause of house plants being in an unsai-- isfactory condition at this season of the year is Mostly due to being grown in the same soil for many years, and without any nourishment being given. Ordinary garden soil is most un- satisfactory, and soon becomes s&amp;ir and heavy. The present is not the best time for re-potting, but sickly plants had better be seen- to at once if they are to be preserved. Good potting com- posts can be purchased at a nurseryman's, and it will probably be cheaper to purchase a small quantity than to attempt to make up a compost. Mistakes are often made of putting plants in pots too large; use as small a. size as possible to comfortably take the roots. Watering should be carefully done, giving enough to keep the soil moist and keeping it so.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>ROOTING ROSE CUTTINGS IN POTS.</head>
        <p>ROOTING ROSE CUTTINGS IN POTS.</p>
        <p>Rose cuttings will readily root in pots if the right kind of wood is chosen. Firm portion of this season's growth must be selected, for if too soft they will not do. Shoots which have borne flowers are especially suitable. Cut off the extreme tops, leaving portions about four inches long of hard stem, having three, or at (Continued in next co lumn.)</p>
      </div>
