      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>LAYING POULTRY-</head>
        <p>LAYING POULTRY-</p>
        <p>Remarkable Results in the Twel" Months' Competition. CROSS-BREEDING OF FOWLS'</p>
        <p>The Utility Poultry Club competition ends c'rrle the 14th October, so that there will be one 10,1 monthly report issued before the final and list of awards. The positions of the leading pens remain the same as last 1011„n, the total number of eggs laid by the leading P`d, viz., six White Wyandottes, being 1,180, acc°rof ing to market prices .obtained from the Board 5 Agriculture weekly returns. A pen of Hocks still holds the second position, but 101,"be they make very rapid progress they will able to catch up the leading pen in the rernt,s ing short period for which the competition to run.</p>
        <p>tbe It is interesting to note thaf althoug- leading positions are held by white Wyandob:ci buff Flocks and Leghorns, pens of these Vett; are also to be found towards the end of ;0 list, which gives the order of merit of the iry pens competing. This bears out the the°,:o that egg production is more a question of sfr,„e than breed. Thei competition is open to ,IOP-g public, and may be viewed on application Del.ng made to the college authorities. intendlitle visitors should make early application, as, Ise, competition will soon be drawing to a c!,°•ng The following are the records of the six lea pens for the eleventh period of four weeks</p>
        <p>, Total eggs Total /11 to to a Order. Pan. Breed. Aug. 19. An-9 1 'GO White Wyandotte, 1,180 £5 1° 2 86 Buff Rocks 8 5 1,045 . 6 401 3 32 White Wyandotte, 1,099 6: oi 4 29 90 Pt 1,066 g 1 i '', 51 5 24 Black Leghorn, 973 4 6 45 White Wyandotte, 973 4 I.' -</p>
        <p>A GOOD MOULT. nd Moulting is a natural process of Nature, api. must be encouraged in every possible rilanilike. Many breeders promote a good moult ne'h'el. autumn sets in by confining the birds in s"nly tered quarters and reducing the food suit s almost to starvation point. In a natural VCBJ fowl's moult lasts about three months, althonte an enforced moult is much quicker, and flitlYgic completed in eight weeks. ImmediatelYA feathers begin to fall, nutritive food sholikLin given liberally, as it is naturally a great sti"rs, on the fowl's resources to grow new fenth,f,o Directly the moult is over, laying eominel'„nti in real earnest. In addition to shelter extra food flowers of sulphur and boiled lin.settle added to the hot morning soft food asais'mol new growths of feathers, whilst when a overmoults herself and becomes almost tna7e-rf It is necessary to rub sulphur ointment ill e% night and confine to the house. f Some poultry-keepers, as no eggs are coming during moult, reduce the food, v'"„jv is a great mistake, and will prejudice the aulf)oro'd of eggs at a later date. Plenty of green„,,ing must be given to keep the blood Cool 1.70"05e moult, or feather-picking will result., feathers quickly accumulate, and shol-0,,, infest mtheovheodueszrypfoeuwitrdyaze,eporerisnsweciltl peivisl,say7b.e 1.‘.-01! after their biro' repaid for looking a . duff!, moult.</p>
        <p>THOUGHTS ON CROSSBREEDING. fill A cross-bred fowl, says a writer in and Home," is the product of a process ,se deliberate selection. A mongrel, on the otP, hand, is a nondescript combination of a seriei of haphazard matings. The economic value the latter is entirely individual, and cal; quently very uncertain, but that of the forMerr more generally Uniform, and advantageasAti. 'otherwise, according to circumstances. though here and there a mongPel may be feltjapel that is capable of a considerable egg-Yield's despite the fact that many mongrel chic""er make good table birds, the element of _,llll,,crif tainty is so great and the number of "wt12";1,1 so large that such stock must ultimatelY P.° unprofitable. Cross-breeding, on the other hand, 08; er very useful for some purposes and undelLpeer tam n conditions, but it is by no means so 6,,-r ally desirable for the commercial pcodllc-coll. some would have him believe. Whete it l'Apite sidered desirable, there must be a de", Ole understanding of the object in view and &amp;too best material to employ for its attainment,iita • the object may be more effectually and cerwoooti attained by the crossing of stock that is ftori upon both sides, than by the unsatisftiv4iiell attempt to revive a degenerate stock bY - means. • :cts The chief benefit of cross-breeding the possibility of combining existing qualities plus the hardiness that is a cotil"-ro• characteristic of cross-bred chickens; but; to vided the breeder is sufficiently experienciro form properly and maintain a pure-bred st'n'ges suited to his purpose, the possible advanh"Tole of crossing do not always outweigh the Pr°l",e6.o drawbacks. Breeds are crossed to illetn` prolificacy, to add to the size of eggs, or prove their colour, .and the same prortocls adopted to intensify table qualities---1115e5 that are rather justifiable in particular than as a general rule. In egg-production the breeder' is folloo more,direct line in the selection and rejfjOrier-cotr