      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>PEARSON AND KNOWLES.</head>
        <p>PEARSON AND KNOWLES.</p>
        <p>A Prosperous Year and a Dividend of 8 Per Cent.</p>
        <p>LED REFRESHING FRUIT."</p>
        <p>RhThe fortieth annual general meeting of the :;:iireholders of the Pearson and Knowles Coal Iron Company, Ltd., was held at the corn- -14)411,,Y's offices, Warrington, on Monday. Sir to "armood Banner, M.P., chairman of direc- krbB, Presided, and there were also present: ti`• John J. Bleckly, Mr. W. Peter Hylands, Mr. th S. Bleckly, Mr. J. Bleckly, jun., Major Har- .()Zl Banner, and Messrs. J. Paul Rylands, A. p uleckly, Wilfred J. Bleckly, Thomas McLeod H. S. Woodcock, Gordon H. Fraser, C. A,:,,Eames, William Chadwick, John Temple, ttattr Duckworth, J. W. Black, W. Richard- G. Bowden, F. J. Bowden, E. Massey, C. C. urn ess, W. J. Wallington, Thomas Atherton, G'`Vles E. Bunch, J. Pickles, F. Pendlebury, R. hreenhalgh, A. Hulme, T. Truslove, and A. r4therton (secretary).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>REPORT OF A PROSPEROUS YEAR.</head>
        <p>REPORT OF A PROSPEROUS YEAR.</p>
        <p>l'he -re-Of; for --year ended June 30th, Whi P eh has already appeared in "The Examiner," -Lltained the following* points l'olits for year, £118,870 3s. 9d.; balance °Ought forward from last year, £13,822 165.; , 41, £132,693 Os. 3d. Interim dividend for first half7year at the rate 5f • 813 i per cent. per annum on the first and tieleGrid preference shares and six per cent., on (te ordinary shares absorbed £28,250. The iZeetors now recommended a dividend for the nrBt half-year at the rate of six per cent., on the „stand second preference shares, and 10 per per annum on the ordinary shares, mak- -18 With the interim dividend eight per cent. "I' the year, and a total distribution of £67,98,8 6s• 8d _ .</p>
        <p>PU-riher recommendations: £30,000 to the iess erve fund, raising it to £382,500; £15,156 th sd, the expenditure on capital account for et' Year to be written off; leaving a balance t 19,548 6s. 2d. to be carried forward. The company holds all the ordinary shares Li the Partington Steel and Iron CornpanY, o,lltel, amounting to £250,000, which are 'Al' Paid up. 8011 departments of the company's 'business a'eAr satisfactory results for the year, although erious check in demand and a fall in Prices errred in the June quarter, mainly due to the eels of the Balkan War. of'vir• R. Brancker has retired from the position whi!lanaging director of the colliery departrnent, he has held since the year 1893. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>CONSOLIDATED BALANCE SHEET.</head>
        <p>CONSOLIDATED BALANCE SHEET.</p>
        <p>io'llte consolidated balance sheet for the Year (Tlorating the assets and liabilities Of tulds Bros., Ltd., gave the following figures: ,"orninal capital, £1,000,000. ti..Ubscribed capital, £1,000,000; reserve fund, .1500; loan, £147,400; debentures—secured the assets of Rylands Bros. Ltd., £21,000; itors, £124,899 7s. Id.; profit and loss, t,132,693 less £28,250 interim dividend paid), 4,,14,443 OS. 3d.; total, £1,750,242 7s. 4d. reehold and leasehold land, buildings, col- tge„ries, iron works, wire mills, railways, cot- re-8, machinery; plant, &amp;c., £754,699 2s. 3d-; iorve fund investment account, £338,399 12s. •, mine rents suspense account, £13,468 T.!/* 4d.; stock, as per stock books, £348,08fi i4LI Id.; sundry debtors as per ledger accaunts, Ec4o,, 413 3s. 3d.; cash and bills in hand and at Incers £28,980 14s. Od.; total, £1,750,242 4(1.-1 Con' fro lery leases and goodwill of collieries and le, It works (written off out of profits), £315,559 ra. Od. •</p>
        <p>INCREASE IN MEN AND WAGES. CHAIRMAN, in moving the adoption of the th' Port and accounts, said: During the year all L'e departments of our works have, without t„kception, been fully employed, with a result 'Qat in respect both to tonnage and output and ()t EUnount of invoice value, our figures are in of any previous years. As evidence of 41, ellnount of work with which we have dealt 11;1111, g the past year, I would inform you that ips;Ltal number of men employed in 1913 was wt 7, in comparison with 9,789 in 1912, and the , te4 paid in 1913 were £734,220, as against ! t7568+128 in 1912, or an increase this year Of c0,..492, which does not include any wages in I, Qo'nection with the Partington Steel, and Iron vrllllany, Limited. as you know, it is absolutely necessary laVe good conditions of work and trait if hre to make good profits, and. I think we th'Y say such conditions have prevailed during toe last twelve months; but, of course, we have lOOk forward to other times, and it LS our O'erience that it is difficult in these days to i:lciittee cost when slackness comes and a dimin- ed output. There are so many fixed charges Provided for nowadays that. without good qlitions of trade it, is difficult to keep up idends to shareholders. (Hear, hear.) We h.know that conditions ere not always good. 41;18 sort 'of war between employers and work- 'S one of the conditions that militates th:llgly against success, and whilst, during Past year, we have not suffered materially .44`;111 it, we cannot but look forward with great rt) tetY to future years, because, apart from the g,LII themselves, there seem to be so many rtAZ4lled friends who are always stirring no the lln_ll to agitate against their employers. (Hear, -qr.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>Increased Costs.</head>
        <p>Increased Costs.</p>
        <p>Effect of Legislation.</p>
        <p>- I, au all is not gold that glitters, and whilst, instance, in coal you will hear suggestions florrhous profits being made, I will, in order show you the increased cost that has to be lided for before profits are made, give you &lt;rt gil ures of our coal trade. The sales of coal 10-0"se(I this year showkan increase on the. year ot :Of £54,863. On the other hand, the cost liltt,w4ges has gone up £36,822, and the cost of ef,4erials (principally timber) £11,585, so you Dr-onth, tit, whilst there is great talk of these big ' kid-'l„Ls, there is not, in fact, such. a very large out of the extra price, which eon- ti's have to pay in the coal trade. Part of tvesult, of course, arises from some of the Of Parliament that have been passed. It ttie,,r7rhaps, not to be wondered at that, when is sunshine and good weather, men do not NV's like to go down into the depths of the liQp`'.ll, but we *suffer very largely' at- our col- , litqnle's from absentees. This morning, for in- -26 per cent. of our menfare not at work o'hci, that is a very. frequent occurrence. I ttIQ mv4l almost say- that, on an average during Ma. 4ridr, we have been 20 per cent. of men short, the result is reflected in the output. In u, there is a reduction of two-thirds of Per man in consequence of the shdrler 4lle.now worked, under the Eight Hours Act. ,;h, the fact that there is now a guaranteed o 461 mdePendent of the quantity of coal sent; ttiq h '',at 'there is not the same stimulus for a Lci Make such a good output as he formerly NI). AS have pointed out, good conditions are Nt Chlred in order to obtain good profits,( and ttqllo,oresent time we have to face the condi- Ivlik",l have outlined arising from these Acts It'fiNg °lir legislators.have been good enough to nitse, uPon us. You will, therefore, see that 1 aflt the increased price received, there large proportion of increased cost in seB . dnd stores. iksarue applies in the iron trade as weii, ie ditlieult question arises when there is Ni,pness in trade whether it will be easy, to '4rt e theadditional costs that have now sfrl</p>
        <p>Pit tter Results and Eight Per Cent. the reult tDrofit and loss account shows a better last year, or for several yearS pest,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <p>and enables us to declare a dividend this year of eight per cent., and doal with thq profits as stated in the report.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>Additional Burdens on Trade: Insurance Act</head>
        <p>Additional Burdens on Trade: Insurance Act</p>
        <p>Unfair.</p>
        <p>As regards the future, the coal trade is doing well, though we have increased ,charges to bear on wages and stores, and I hope we will not have any labour troubles during,;:this year. The conditions as regards the ,fron and steel trade are not quite as satisfactory, but now that the war is over and peace assured,: and with the good monsoon in India, we may hope that the trade in iron and steel will at least compare favourably with the trade of 1912. The Health Insurance Act has cost the coin- pany .26,145, or at least 1 per cent, dividend during the year, and as you will observe from the paragraph in the report the directors see no adequate benefit either to the shareholders or to the company's workmen. The Health Insur- ance Work- men's dis.tinct from the Work- impe.intsa6etilon Act, which costs this mAccto s erirY company £8,935 per annum, and is entirely a proper' contribution to pay for men injured through no fault of their own in the course of their n employment: The Health Insurance Act affinityhas with the terms of employment any morethan old-age pensions,and it is grossly unjust to employers at any rate to make them not only collectors for compulsory insurance, but also contributors to an expenditure which ought to be national and borne by the whole community. (Hear, hear.). The increased cost of production has been lightly dismissed by those in authority as being easily collectable in the price of manufactured articles sold. • Any- one who understands business knows this is an absUrdity—(hear, hear)—and, as we know, our Government religiously deClines AO take any steps which may assist trade, whatever addi- tional burden they may place upon it. (Hear, hear.) lam not going to say whether the Insurance Act is right or wrong. All I would say is that it is most unfair to employers tn industrial concerns. Those who consider it " rare and refreshing fruit" may say so-- (laughter)—but I am not going to say that, nor am I going to offer any opinion against it. The burden is not so great on the coal trade, as it is possible. to pass the cost on, there being no competition by the import of foreign cod.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>Staff.</head>
        <p>The Staff.</p>
        <p>All our official staff have worked hard to make the year a success. lam much indebted to them, and I can only .hope that with the excellent reputation we have for our products and with the anticipated results from our in- vestments from the Partington Company, we shall again meet you next year with a satisfac- tory balance. (Applause.).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>NO BOOM.</head>
        <p>NO BOOM.</p>
        <p>Mr- JOHN 3. BLECKLY, in seconding, said they had paid a record amount of taxation and Government imposts, and they had not made a record of profit from the business. Several times in the history of the concern the prat of £llB,OOO had been exceeded. Fog instance, they could go back to the year 1901, when they made &amp;profit of £142,000. Their turnover and year were a million pounds less they thwatere last year, and the capital they hnow invested in the business was between xs thanaa3ldooe ,000 and £500,000 more. He did not think they could say that this had been a booming year. That was not his idea of a boom. They had had a satisfactory year so far as circum- stances surrounding them would allow. That was all. There was no boom, and "anyone who described it as such did so for sinister motives and to misrepresent the facts. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>What happened last year was this. In 1911-12 there was a very great deficiency in their busi- ness, and they actually made no money in their iron works at all. That deficiency was made up in 1912-13 by the extra business they did. They must put the two years together if they wanted to be fair, and they arrived at only a normal result.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>Company Has Nothing To Do With Politics.</head>
        <p>Company Has Nothing To Do With Politics.</p>
        <p>The Chairman had referred to the National Insurance Act. There were observations in the report which had given rise to accusations of political bias. This company had nothing to do with politics. Whe#l they had to _pay £6,000 or £7,000 a year this question was a pure ques- tion of business. When they were taxed un- fairly, as they thought was the case, and saddled with a burden that did not belong to them, it was a proper thing for the directors to draw public attention to the grievance. By passing the Old Age Pensions Act, the country had admitted the pripcifile of State-aid to the unfortunate.. Did anyone mean to tell him that men who had been called "the soldiers of the industrial army," as they fell out by the way from sickness or ill-health, had not quite as good a right to claim the assistance of the State as others, whose journey to the end had not been successful, and who were rewarded by an old-age pension? (Hear, hear.) That was not a question of charity at all. The working tlasses who would benefit contribute their full share to the taxes that would furnish the 'funds and the balance might be fairly distributed amongst the rest of the community, Sand no one wouy have reason to complain,</p>
        <p>Labour Unrest: A Delicate Situation.</p>
        <p>He had carefully avoided any reference to the condition of tile labour unrest which existed around them, as they were themselves at that moment involved in some delicate negotiations , with one class of their men, which he was anxious not to prejudice, and whicii, if not care- fully handled on both- sides might. result in the stoppage' of the whole works. The duty and responsibility of deciding the Issue before them was a. solemn and serious one, and could not be lightly regarded; on the one hand a strike here would entail incalculable privation and distress over nearly the whole town, and on the other hand if concessions were made that would permanently cripple the company in its busi- ness in future, its workmen would in the long run suffer even more than the shareholders. He was glad to see that the President of the Union, with which they were now negottating, was Mr. Clynes, M.P., who had just been re- quested by the Board of Trade to mediate ip the Dublin strikes. He trusted that his ap- pointment was a good omen for peace in War- rington: (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>MOSS HALL COLLIERIES. The Explosion.</head>
        <p>MOSS HALL COLLIERIES. The Explosion.</p>
        <p>In answer to a shareholder, as to whether the Moss Hall Colliery had completely recovered from the explosion,</p>
        <p>The \CHAmmAN said that was a large q4estion, but the company were doing their best to recover. There were still some expenses left in connection with the opening out the roads, which were considerable. They had also to pay interests, but the colliery was showing fairly good results now. The report was then adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>DIVIDEND.</head>
        <p>THE DIVIDEND.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN moved: "That the usual divi- dend at the rate of 6 per cent, per annum on the first and second preference shares, and a dividend at the rate of 10 per cent, per annum on the ordinary shares, all less income tax, for the half-year ending 30th June last, be. paid forthwith."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>MR. RYLANDS AND A CHECK TO</head>
        <p>MR. RYLANDS AND A CHECK TO</p>
        <p>COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>comparatively Miserable Dividend.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. PETER RYLANDS, in seconding, said that, after what everybody had regarded as a boom year in trade, one felt that the dividend now declared was a comparatively miserable one. At the present time it was easy for people to find investments, excellently secured, that would yield a return of six per cent., and, that being so, it would seem a modest thing for an investor to expect from 10 to 20 per cent. when he had to take the risks of the fluctuations of a, busftiess proposition Such us theirs unquestion- ably., was. Unfortuneella he was afraid that</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>~z,zj ,</head>
        <p>~z,zj ,</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 4, 1913. was a .chronie condition, and that they could IFARTINGToN sTEEL woRKs not look to ordinary industrial productive undertakings for any such interest upon their investments as was commensurate with the risk involved. It had, however, one Vffect, upon which he touched last year, in thdt it tended to check competition; for when he could get six per cent. very easily, it was net likely that a,wise man would risk his capital in industrial undertakings with the bare chance qf getting eight per cent. when limes were exceptionally gbod.</p>
        <p>•</p>
        <p>V</p>
        <p>Value of a Large Reserve.</p>
        <p>Criticism had often been passed upon the 'directors of that company because they had for many years made such heavy allocations to the reserve fund. Now they could say, with confidence, that had they not put to reserve the money that was represented by something like £700,000, they would not have been in a position to declare eight per cent that day. (Hear, hear.) It would be very poor consola- tion to them to receive, for the moment, hand- some dividends, if, es had happened with some companies, those dividends were obtained at the cost of future disaster and the whole of their capital was lost.</p>
        <p>Legislative Burdens. Unfortunately burdens were placed upon them by legislation and in other respectos—bur- dens which it was no doubt perfectly proper should be imposed upon industrial companies to some extent, but which, in their case, amounted to something like £40,000 a year, a first charge upon the undertaking, representing a four per cent. debenture of something like £1,000,000. It might be, as the Chairman had pointed out, that they -could recover from the consumers of coal any, increment in the cost-1 of production of that commodity, but in com- petition with foreign manufacturers it was quite hopeless to do the same with iron, steel and wire. The extra charges had to be met ' out of profits, and the only way to face the diffi- culty was by introducing capital which would take the place of what was nothing more or less than water, representing the capitalised valuel of the burdens imposed upon , them by the State.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>ZAM-BUK, the Successor of the Rare Balms of Ancient Rome.</p>
        <p>A Valuable Invention &amp; A Household Boon</p>
        <p>When Gladiatorial fights were at the height of their glory 2,000 years ago, herbal balms for the healing of the combatants' wounds attained great fame and perfection.</p>
        <p>These rare and costly healers knitted the damaged tissues together in a way that would be regarded as marvellous and even mystifying in this present day</p>
        <p>Unfortunately, the highly-prized secret of these miraculous' bal perished with the fall of Rome itself, and for generations we have had to put up with poor artificial substitutes in the form of coarse ointments deriving their uncertain action from crude mineral drugs.</p>
        <p>But the discovery of the great herbal healer, Zam-Buk, has furnished a true successor to the precious balms of old. It is so marvellously like them. in action. One touch of Zam-Buk seems to make the whole tissue well.</p>
        <p>A careful dressing with Zam-Buk spread on clean rag or lint 11000 takes the fire out of a wound and allays the burning irritation of eczema. Zam-Buk is a soothing agent of unparalleled power. It draws poison and inflammation out of the tissues; it quickly cleanses and cures the ringworm-ridden scalp of a child; and it will 'rescue the body and limbs from pains and sores that have defied all other measures. Nothing known to Science performs the same marvellous healing or dispels disease from the tissues as Zam-Buk does. This is due to the unique herbal character and wonderful origin of Zam-Buk, and its complete freedom from the rancid animal fat and coarse minerals of the cheap artificial ointments of to-day. Scrupulous care also is taken over the secret processes by which this rare balm is prepared.</p>
        <p>ZAM-BUK</p>
        <p>Is unequalled for</p>
        <p>A box of Zam-Buk takes three months to finally produce; and it is only by the large scale .on which the manufacture is carried out that it is possible to bring the cost down to a point within reach of the most modest purse. Zam-Buk has such a wide range of usefulness for every-day accidents and disease that no home can afford to be without this "greatest healer of the twentieth century."</p>
        <p>Ulcers, poisoned sores, ringworms and scalp sores, piles, running sores, bad legs, diseased anklts, cuts, wounds, scratches, burns, scalds, bruises, abscesses, sprains and strains, sort ehm after shoving, pinsples, scaly patehes,sors feet,inflamed and irritable skin, rhtumaticky Pints, igatertnO, eta. There is only one Zam-Buk, and that is sold In sealed boxes, of registered design, as and 219 (219 box contains nearly four times the 16). Of all chemists or pest fret at above prices from the Zam-J&amp; flo., Leeds.</p>
