      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Mr. JoRN I. BLECKLY, sec- onded by Mr. RYLANDS, Sir Harmood Banner was re-elected a director of the company.,</p>
        <p>Mr. HERBERT WOODCOCK moved that Messrs. Blease and Sorts be re-appointed auditors for the ensuing year, at a remuneration of £3OO.</p>
        <p>Mr. THOMAS ATHERTON seconded, and the motion was carried.</p>
        <p>THE CHAIRMAN'S KNIGHTHOOD.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. S. BLEclay, in moving that the best thanks of the meeting be given to ifie Secretary, managers and other officers of the company at the ironworks and collieries for their services during the past year, said he had seen deputed by the gentlemen named respectively to con- gratulate the Chairmen upon the honour the King had conferred upon him recently. (Hear, hear, and applause.) Major BANNER seconded, and the resolution was heartily carried. • Mr. C. E. BUNCH briefly responded on behalf of the iron ilepartment.. - - -</p>
        <p>- Mr. EAMES returned thanks for the collieries department.</p>
        <p>COMPLAINTS OF BURDENS.</p>
        <p>:gr. J. J. BLECKLY proposed a vote of thanks to Sir J. S. Harmood Banner for presiding. He said they were twitted about their complaints of the onerous -character of the burdens they had to bear. "What is the use of grumbling when you can make eight per cent:?" they were asked. If the wind were' tempered to the shorn lamb, and they could look forward to some relief when bad times came, that would be a specious and perhaps a well-founded argument, ,but it was not so. One per cent. off nine per cent. might not be a serious thing, but one per cent. off three per cent'. was. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>Mr. C. E. BUNCH seconded. On behalf of the large number of workmen with whom he was associated, he would like to tell Sir Hannood Banner how pleased they were to hear of the honour recently conferred upon him. They sincerely hoped he would live long to enjoy it. (Applause.) The vote of thanks was accorded with" accla- mation.</p>
        <p>Sir HARMOOD BANNER, in responding, ex- pressed his satisfaction to receive the congratu- lations of the workmen. He had always tried to be on good terms with them, because he had a very high respect for the British workman in all phases of his life. I-Te only hoped that the workmen would have tfie same respect for the employers, and always be ready, in the case of their own firm, to meet them in full, fair and frank discussion. If they did so at all times, he did not believe they would ever have a strike at their collieries or at their ironworks. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>HOW A BLACKBURN MAN REGAINED AMBITION AND APPETITE.</head>
        <p>HOW A BLACKBURN MAN REGAINED AMBITION AND APPETITE.</p>
        <p>It seems peculiar that a man's ambition should be dependent upon his appetite. But such is the case—no man can be keen, energetic and ambitious unless his stomach is in full working order. A Blackburn merchant recently made the interesting statement that he cured his digestive troubles, and presumably regained his ambition, by the simple expedient of taking a course of Stomoxygen Tablets—the new ozone preparation which eliminates stomach trouble. Read the following letter—there you will see the story in his own words: " For the past four years I have suffered from indigestion and catarrh of the stomach and bowels. I have had no appetite, have had no ambition, and my food would ferment on my stomach, leaving me very depressed and miser- able. Though I unsuccessfully tried many remedies, I believe I can safely say that Stomoxygen has cured me. It is ten days since I had the slightest distress. When it will do this for such an obstinate case it is worth recommending, and I do so whenever I can." Stomoxygen can be obtained from your chem- ist, or a P.O. for 2e. 9d. will bring a large pack- age per return, post free, from the Stomoxygen Company, Norwich-street, London, B.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>MAKER AND BREAKER OF GLASS.</p>
        <p>James Moore, glassworks labourer. was, charged at St. Helens with drunkenness, assault- ing Constable Robinson, and smashing a glass panel, value ..t1 10s., in the door of Pilkington's Men's Hostel, Bridge-street—Evidence was given that Moore, who was an ex-Army man, had been working at the glassworks, and living at the hostel provided by the firm for about ten weeks. On Saturday night he went to the place drunk and bearing signs of having been fighting, so he was refused admission, and then he smashed the glass panel and struck out at the officer.— Moore was fined £2 Bs., or 21 days.-</p>
        <p>MATERNITY BENEFIT. , The first prosecution under the maternity benefit clauses of the Insurance Act was heard at St. Helens on Monday, when Isaac Kinder, bricksetter, was charged with neglecting to make adequate provision for the care of his wife dur- ing confinement. Mr. Allen said that on March 26th Mrs. Kinder was confined. The midwife showed the man how to claim benefit. He was then employed by the St. Helens Colliery Company, and wns on sick benefit under the Act. He went to the colliery and was paid £l. Of that amount his wife received 2s. only, and next day the accused was found by the midwife lying drunk on the bed. The relieving officer had to be called in, and the midwife had to buy milk herself. It was admitted that the only money the man had with which to get drunk was the maternity benefit</p>
        <p>Kinder blamed his wife, and said that he had to use the money to get bedding, &amp;c., out of pawn, his wife having put them in. Mr. Allen said it was very important that this benefit should go into the right channel. The magistrates said it was a serious matter. Although it was the first prosecution they must mark their opinion of the seriousness by sending Kinder to gaol for 14 days' hard labour, without the option of a fine.</p>
        <p>Kinder: That is for paying insurance money.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>BALANCE SHEET, arni JUNE, 1913..</head>
        <p>BALANCE SHEET, arni JUNE, 1913..</p>
        <p>CAPITAL AND LIABILITIES..</p>
        <p>Nominal capital, £700,000.</p>
        <p>Subscribed capital (350,000 6 per cent. cumu- lative preference shares of £1 each, fully paid, £350,000; 250,000 ordinary Shares of £1 each, fully paid, £250,000), £600,000; first mortgage debenture stock—£lso,ooo at 5 per cent., on which has been paid the sum of £77,865; sundry creditors, £31,845 15s. 7d.; total, £700,710 15s. 7d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>PROPERTY AND ASSETS.</head>
        <p>PROPERTY AND ASSETS.</p>
        <p>Expenditure in respect of freehold land, build- ings, railways, machinery, plant, &amp;c., £536,562 12s. 3d.; sundry debtors, £10,059 ss. Bd.; pre- ference share issue expenses, £12,919 16s. id.; debenture stock issue expenses, £4,507 12s. Bd.; stocks on hand as per stock books, £26,908 Os.: rush and bills in hand and at bankers, £117,852 I.ld. ; total, £709,710 15s. 7d. At a meeting of the Parlington Company this report was adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>OFFENCES BY MOTOR DRIVERS.</head>
        <p>OFFENCES BY MOTOR DRIVERS.</p>
        <p>John Johnson, Ifuspratt-street, Widnes, was charged at Runcorn with driving a heavy motor waggon beyond the speed limit, at Sutton, on September 24th.—Sergeant Brown deposed to timing the defendant from Sutton Weaver to Preston Weaver, a distance of 1 mile 1,188 yards, and found that he covered it in twelve minutes, which worked out at an average speed of 8 miles 660 yards per hour.—A fine of 10s. and 9s. 6d. costs was inflicted.</p>
        <p>Francis Edward Jones, Tilesbury, Lancashire, was ordered to pay 7s. costs for failing to keep the rear light lighted to illuminate the identifi- cation-plate on a motor car.</p>
        <p>Twenty-one vessels belonging to the fleet of the first destroyer flotilla visited the Holyhead harbour of refuge during the week-end.</p>
        <p>Samuel Roberts, village postman of Llanystumdwy, a friend of the Chancellor's boy- hood days, retired on Saturday after 39 years' service.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>MIGRATION OF BIRDS.</head>
        <p>THE MIGRATION OF BIRDS.</p>
        <p>The periodic movements of birds have at- tracted, through many ages, the attention of the poet and the philosopher, but, strange as it may teem, they have not received the at- tention one would expect from the scientist. And the truth is, that though certain facts concerning these movements are well estab- lished and widely known, their origin and reason are but little understood and fre- quently debated. The passing of birds in large numbers from land to land is a continuous process ex- tending throughout the whole of the year, and is subject to such order and law that one could almost base a calendar upon the times when our feathered visitors arrive or depart. At the end of February certain hardy species of geese make for the north, to be followed by other of their relatives in March. April sees the full tide of bird life setting towards our shores, and by the middle of May, the swift, the last of our summer visitors has put in an appearance. Before June is out adult cuc- koos and young birds of Arctic species have began their southward flight and the exodus from the British Isles at any rate continues right on to December, sometimes well into January also.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>HOW DID IT BEGIN?</head>
        <p>HOW DID IT BEGIN?</p>
        <p>The first question that arises is—how did it all begin? Why did the first swallow leave the land of his fathers for some more distant realm of whose condition his untutored in- telligence could have had no knowledge? There are two main theories that attempt an explanation. One set of philosophers bids us seek the ori- gin of migratory movements in changes of climate. The birds in cold or temperate countries were driven south by the oncoming of the glacial epoch and they only returned to their original homes as the ice-cap once more retreated to the north. And then, year after year, as winter approached, the birds went back again to the southern lands of sunshine and of warmth. Thus the golden plover makes, annually, a kind of elliptical circuit in America travelling in autumn down the shores of the United States through the Ber- mudas and the Lesser Antilles to Argentina and returning in the spring across the Gulf of Mexico and up the Valley of the Mississip- pi; and Mr. Wells Cook, a great authority upon the movements now under consideration gives it as his opinion that the golden plover found the northerly route up the Missassippi Valley when ice gradually retreated at the end of the Great Ice Age.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>IN SEARCH OF FOOD.</head>
        <p>IN SEARCH OF FOOD.</p>
        <p>Another 'body of scientists seeks the origin of migration in the need of fresh food sup- plies. Overcrowding diminished the avail- able quantity of berries and worms and com- pelled the birds to look for new stores of pro- vender. There is some modern evidence to support this view for it has been found, in lAmerica, that the bobolink, which feeds amongst the hay, explores fresh routes as barren land is fertilised by irrigation or waste land brought under cultivation. But that this is not the sole cause of the periodic- al bird flights will be seen by considering the migrations of the skylark. The British spe- cies is residential, so far as the whole Wands %re concerned, though it has a series of local migrations. But in the north of Europe there are various foreign species that arrive upon our shores in large numbers about aut- umn time. These always return to their own breeding grounds in the spring, and never permanently remain amongst their foreign relatives. Then too, thousands of skylarks from other parts of the continent move south- wards about the same time and are found here merely as birds of passage. They spend only a few hours amongst us, but help to swell materially, the number of skylarks in England during the autumn. New why do some birds come here to stay while others of the same family just pay us a passing call? And besides, our own skylarks that have been bred in the northern parts of Britain migrate to the south, not leaving the country at all but simply passing on to warmer dis- tricts. The places that they vacate are the very areas frequented by the immigrants from Scandinavia. It is not possible there- fore to attribute the migrations of these birds purely to a search of food. For if there is food enough in the north for the visi- tors from Norway, why do the Scotch sky- larks go to the south of England, and if there are sufficient stores for them in the south why do the Continental visitors that call upon us not remain instead, of proceeding hurriedly still nearer to the Equator? It has also been not:ced that the tracks of different groups of birds of the same species often cross each other, one lying east, the other west. The explanation of all these curious pheno- mena is not yet within our grasp. 110 W DO BIRDS FIND THEIR WAY 9</p>
        <p>How do the migrants find their way? have to answer that it is a matter of instinct, which means we known nothing about it. The matter is rendered more difficult of compre- hension because it is usually the younger b.rds who go first and who have never previ. wish traversed the rode. This is sot UN&amp;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>Great Industry that will Shortly be in Full Swing.</head>
        <p>A Great Industry that will Shortly be in Full Swing.</p>
        <p>COMPLETE TEST THIS MONTH</p>
        <p>OR NEXT.</p>
        <p>At the annual meeting of the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Company on Monday, at Warrington, many references were made to the great steel and iron works at Partington, which are rapidly approaching completion. The report of the directors stated that the Pearson and Knowles Company hold all the ordinary shares in the Partington concern. amounting to £250,000, all fully paid up. Sir J. S. HARMOOD BANNER, M.P., who moved the adoption of the Pearson and Knowles re- port, made several long references to Parting- ton in the -course of his speech.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>PERFECT, MODERN PLANT.</head>
        <p>A PERFECT, MODERN PLANT.</p>
        <p>The principal item, in the reserve fund ivestments, he said, is £250,000 invested in n- ordinary shares in the Partington Steel and Iron Co., Ltd. Last year our investment in the Partington Co. was £58,376 155., so that during this year out of our ordinary resources we have paid in £191,623 55., and now hold £250,000 lordinary shares fully paid up. I should like, on behalf of myself and co-directors, to say how satisfied we are in the course we have taken in using the money of this- company for , the ' establishment of the immense concern which most of you will have seen on the banks of the IShip Canal on the way to Manchester, and I hope you will have observed also that during the Ilast two months the coke ovens and the blast furnaces have been in active operation. Mr. lohn Bleckly started one, I started the other, and we are leaving the third for Mr. Rylands. (Laughter and applaUse.) I had quite hoped that the steel works would have been also at work before this meeting, but the erection of some of our plant has ,not been complete, and, unfortunately, about three weeks ago our managing director (Mr. Arthur H. Cooper) was taken seriously ill, and the state of his health gave us great cause for anxiety. I am pleased, however, to say that he is now out of danger, and well on his way, to recovery. He has been well represented by his officials in Partington, and work has been pushed forward most diligently. I anticipate that in October or November at the latest we shall have a full test of these great works, which have caused us an immense amount of thought and anxiety. I would only like to add that I believe, and I have been told on the very best authority, that we are equipped in every way with the most modern and perfect plant, and with the advan- tageous situation we possess and our other facilities, we ought to be able to give a really satisfactory account of ourselves. No doubt our shareholders will offer the criticism that the large expenditure on Parting- ton out of the profits of the year, and which is dealt with to some extent out of the increased amount placed to resetire, and the larger balance carried forward, is a hardship upon them, and reduces the dividend which they might otherwise have expected to receive; but I think the directors may state that it is char- acteristic of their policy to take a prudent course, and they -anticipate that the reward will come in future years. (Rear, hear.) -There is no question about this fact. It is impossible for works of any magnitude, to stand still, and In keeping up to date they must expend money.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>BOLD STROKE TO SECURE CONSTANT SUPPLIES. To Meet Every Enemy.</head>
        <p>BOLD STROKE TO SECURE CONSTANT SUPPLIES. To Meet Every Enemy.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. J. BLECKLY, who seconded, said the Chairman' had mentioned the Partington Steel and Iron Co.'s works, and the large amount of money which had been -invested in that con- cern. The difficulty - and responsibility which had been felt at Pearson and Knowles for years had been the fact that for their raw material they had been dependent upon outside works, a large proportion being derived from abroad. t hat was not a safe position for a concern of such a magnitude as this to occupy, and they had been looking about them year in and year out for an opportunity to remedy that state of things, and they had finally arrived at it. Perhaps what they had done was a bold thing to do. Some people thought they had acted rashly, considering the ages or some of the directors present. He (Mr. Bleckly) ,had just completed 50 years' serviceon that ground, and some people thought it wa34.yery venturesome that the directors at their time of life should take up suet' a big enterprise. It must be re- membered, however, that they had to look at a limited company as gifted with life everlasting, and realise that their successors and the share- holders would reap the benefit. • _</p>
        <p>He felt now that in the magnificently equipped works on the unrivalled site on the Manchester Ship Canal, they would be able to handle any process from the coal and the ore to the last stage of finished material, independ- ently of, everyone. He did not wish to say that they could defy competition, but he thought they could meet every enemy they had to con- tend with; and if they were not victors there must be something wrong with the men who would follow the present Board_</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>SOLUTION OF GRAVE DIFFICULTY.</head>
        <p>SOLUTION OF GRAVE DIFFICULTY.</p>
        <p>Mr- -JOHN TEMPLE knew the anxieties with which the company had to contend, and in reference to investments he wished, as an old hand at finance, to say distinctly that he en- tirely approved of a great company like this putting its reserves into kindred companies, which it had to run, particularly in the case of the Partington Steel and Iron Company,. He could cifrry his mem* back many years, and he knew that at one time the existence of the pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Company lay heavilV on the minds of the Messrs. Bleckly and their co-directors, and that it was with no light heart that they at last agreed that the solution of the existing difficulty was the Par- tington works—a solution with which he en- tirely agreed. tHear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>REALLY SUBSTANTIAL, HELPFUL CONCERN.</head>
        <p>A REALLY SUBSTANTIAL, HELPFUL CONCERN.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. PETER RYLANDS said that although the reserve fund investments at Partington did not appear in their assets as plant, they could see upon the walls of that room their reserve fund in pictorial form. Calling attention to a series of large photographs of the Partington works. mr. Bylands said that if the shareholders asked: "In what is our reserve fund invested," he would reply, "Si monurnentum requiris, cir- cumspice." They would feel satisfied that in the Partington works they possessed a really substantilil concern to help /them along the difficult road which lay before them, and that when they met next year the directors would be able to give them a good account of the early days of the undertaking. They could not ex- pect a great deal in the first steps they took along the road, but he hoped they would be able to report that the works, up to that point, were thoroughly justifying their existence. (AP plause.)</p>
        <p>PROGRESS AND PROSPECTS.</p>
        <p>PARTINGTON STEEL -AND lIION COMPANY,</p>
        <p>LIMITED,</p>
        <p>The following is the report of the directors of. the Partington Company : The directors have pleasure in' submitting their report awl balance sheet for the year ending June 30ti.., 1913. Considerable delay was experienced during the latter part of last year, and the early part of this year, owing to the difficulty in obtaining delivery of the plant and material on account of the boom in trade, and several contractors were much behindhand with their work.</p>
        <p>Taking these drawbacks into consideration,</p>
        <p>your directors are satisfied with the progress that has been made. Washery Coke-oven and Residual By-products Plants.—The washer3r was put into pperation on March 24th last, and No. 1 and No. 2 batteries, consisting of 54 ovens, commenced making coke six days later. On August Ist last, No. 3 bat- tery, consisting of 27 ovens, was put into operation, and the fourth battery, which will complete the plant, will be ready in about five or six weeks' time. Blast Furnace Department.—No. 2 and No. 3 blast furnaces were blown in on the -9 rns of August last and are working satisfactorily. Steel Furnaces and Bolling Mills.—Good pro- gress has been made in these departments, and it. is anticipated that a considerable portion of the finished steel plant will be ready to com- mence operations within the next two or three months. Your directors are quite satisfied with the results already obtained from that portion of-the plant already put into operation and working_ Election of Auditors,—The auditors, Messrs. Blease and Sons, retire, and offer themselves for re-election_ ‘Signed) J. S. HATIMOOD BANNEII, Chairman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <p>, :</p>
        <p>7</p>
        <p>i - ' , , /</p>
        <p>ZAS</p>
        <p>however, in the case of tile cuclax), the older birds of this family always preoeding the younger. As a rule birds migrate together in great numbers, the desire for company being apparently as great as the desire for food. But the crowd is rarely a heterogeneous one. Old birds keep apart from the young even. When they move at the same time, and amongst the older ones the Eexes are separat- id at all trues. Our visitors usually perform. their journeys at rught, but there is no hard• or fast rule in this as in any other matter keonnected with migration. It has been frequently stated that the birds fly with the wind and when the wind, is unfavourable, rest for better conditions. But there are many instances to the contrary and generalisation can only be accepted within limits, solwide as to prevent It being regard- ed as more than a popular belief.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>VELOCITY AND DISTANCE.</head>
        <p>VELOCITY AND DISTANCE.</p>
        <p>The altitude at which birds fly is a vari- able quantity, it may be a few feet or a few hundred feet, according to the species. The i speed of the movement s also unkno\wn with any accuracy, though it has been suggested that the Yelocity of migratory flight is great. Cr than the normal rate. The Arctic tern sees more daylight and suelight than any other of earth's inhabitant.. To reaeh their katareto quarters the terns must make a journey of close upon 22,000 miles, and as this journey has to be aecomplished in about twenty weeks, they must fly at a rate of 150 maes allay. When seeking for food they must travel with a speed greater than this. By way of contrast, we may state that the slowest traveller known is the American rob- in, which has a speed of only eight miles s lay. Distance is of lAtle consequence and birds seem to prefer one long journer with plenty of provender at the finish to a aeries of shorter flights with less plentiful supplies en route. The golden plover has been lcnown to cross the Atlantic in one journey without stopping for rest anywhere; this means a "non-stop" flight of 2,400 miles. In order to collect sufficient data to enable us to formulate laws of migration a numbsr of experiments have been set on foot either by the British Association or by the British Ornitholog'sts' Club. At the variona houses and light-ships around our coasts se- guar observations are TIOW made and record. ed. At some places birds are caught, and, after aluminium labels have been fastened round their necks they are set free again. These labels bare the date and locality of the capture, and when the bird is recaugl7.t as it frequently is, there is definite ;nfermation‘ available as to its movements. As the num- ber of competent observers is increased and the results become more numerous we shall be ire.a position to know with some certainty the facts of migration and when these have been classified, there will doubtless' ariPt amongst us some scientist to formulate the laws that govern and explain the facts. At present we are almost entirely in the dark.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>PIMPLES VERY THICK ON FACE.</head>
