        <p>PTOMAINE POISONING</p>
        <p>saints be good to us,- he said, whipping up the horse, "he says he's obsarvint, an' he can't obsarve me eoughin'!"</p>
        <p>After a hearty supper of tinned salmon, on the occasion of her second cousin's nuptials, old Miss Kelly was laken extremely ill, and was obliged to send for the• doctor. In the presence of the second cousin, who was act- ing as a chaperone, she confided all her sym- toms, and the tale of the tinned salmon was related to his ears. Although the doctor ad- mitted that she was very sick indeed, he had not given her the satisfaction -a naming the disease. Miss Kelly did not intend to iot him go, be.-..fore he christened it oorffmtiy fat her; she hoped devoutly that it would be a long high-sounding one. As the doctor rose to depart: "Am' what name would ye put to me sickness, Docthor ?" elle said.</p>
        <p>"I would call it ptomaine poisoning," re- tuned the doctor firmly. ," Ah d'ye m'ane it replied Miss Kelly, frith the more consternation that she had not the remotest notion what he meant.</p>
        <p>He lingered a few minutes, prescribing to the spinster what she must eat and what avoid, when:— "What's the name ye put on it ?" she en- quired again. "Pto-maine poisoning!" he returned dis- tinctly. The second cousin, frowning at the spin- ster's show of ignorance, plucked her hastily by the sleeve, shook her head at her with ve- hemence and whistx,red in her ear. Where- upon a look of livid horror oro2.nd old Miss Kelly's face. "Well, I'll be lookin' in to-roonow," oai:t4: the doctor nodding at her, "and thsn ii ye're no better, we'll see what we may have to do." "Never in this world!" exclaimed Miss Kelly vociferously. "Not if I was to die for it.-</p>
        <p>"Cone now," said the doctor, persuasive, but surprised at the change in her manner. "You're not o-oin' to turn against me, now? I don't want to eat you." "Ye should think shame," returned Miss Kelly, "an' I a single .poor gerrl. Me ould mother would turn in her grave. Sixty-five years I've a dacint name, an' I'm not goia' to lose it now. An' I don't credit ye, nayth-ar: for there's plinty more places the salmon'd settle more aisy, without throublin' to thrav- el that far."</p>
        <p>"Now will ye tell me what's ailin' th•‘? era'ythur," said the dolor 'turning to th chaperone. "Sure." answered Miss Kelly's COI!F</p>
        <p>"aren't ye afther sayin' she's p'isined fn the toe. nail, an' she's in dread ye would ax her to uncover the little shins I"</p>
        <p>The death took place on Saturday of Alderman William Oulton, j.P., a prominent Liverpool educationist and an ex-Lord Mayor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>SOME GOOD IRISH STORIES. ,</head>
        <p>SOME GOOD IRISH STORIES. ,</p>
        <p>, i</p>
        <p>Tii.6 COW WITH THE warrE</p>
        <p>They went on a little further. "Still, you know," said the Englishman, "one impudent fellow does not leaven the whole malls of national stupidity, you know." "Well, try the next man that you meet," said his host. They had not gone very far, befocre they encountered a fellow returning from the fair, and leading a fine Hereford cow, with a long white face, by a rope about her head. "Good day," said the obtrusive English- &amp;tan, planting himself in the way, "that's a nue cow, you have." "It is," said the Irishman, betokening by his gesture that he had no time to lose. "And—and—hasn't it a very white face," pursued the Englishman not to be beaten. "Begorra," said the Irishman, - beginning to move off, "if you'd your neck in a halter as long as she has, I warrant your face'd be whiter." Half an hour's walking brought the friends within thirty yards of the host's house, which stood on the opposite side of the road. They now beheld a ramshackle outside car coming towards them at a slow .crawl. The horse which seemed half asleep was being driven b,v a cadaverous man, whose shoulders were peri- odically shaken by a kind of paroxysen.which took him now and then. The friends prepared to cross the road before the crawling horse, when the cadaverous man sat up and shouted " Hould!" "Market-merry, as we say," observed the Englishman to his host. "Where's yew manners!" said the man upon the car, his utterance interrupted by a spasm in his breath. "How dare ye cross a funeral!" The two men exchanged glances, and pro- ceeded a step or two. • • Hould!" said the man again, "have ye no mark o' respect to spare for the dead." I've none at all,' Sakti the Englishman aloud, "to spare for the living, let me tell you." "Hats off," cried the cadaverous man on the car, "don't ye see whet I'm carryin'." I do indeed" retorted the Englishman angrily, "and it's a great deal too much." " Will ye tell me, where are the eyes in your 'head," the man persisted. shaken by an- otner paroxysm. "Amn't I only axin' ye to observe." The two men, greatly irritated, began nevertheless to think that the man must have a child's corpse in the well of his car, since nowhere else could it be seen. "Well I flatter myself I'm fairly observ- ant," said the Englishman, "but you can't expect me to see what's invisible—wherever you may have your corpse concealed." For reply, the man burst into s nether par- oxysm se crle, ^: .• nl.l no</p>
