        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 4, 1913.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 4, 1913.</p>
        <p>recovered himself and brazened it out. though his face had blanched, and for a mo- ment his lips trembled. "It's a lie," he almost screamed. "It's one of your cursed, clever City tricks, but it will not work. Once again, you pay." ' "Yes, two thousand pounds, and we let you go cheap," the Jew added; but his voice and manner had lost conviction, and his tongue travelled furtively round his dry lips. Ware fixed Plumb with his eye. "Not a single penny," he said. "You are in my hands—absolutely." Plumb almost foamed at the mouth..</p>
        <p>" Liar ! " he growled; "and even if it scorns true it will not hang me. You will pay or I play my card—the Ware secret will appear to- morrow."</p>
        <p>"Not one single coin." . Plumb ' seized a chair and raised it threat- inglv. - Ware touched a bell. ' His face was grim and set. His purpose was .ClCftr. "It's good etous,ll to give you both a trial," he said, tersely. " 'lwo men were in the house at the time of the murCer.- The footprints proved it. One man was you." . He pointed to Plumb, and as he F..pc,T.e the police, who had boon waiting, entexed th.2.room. Elderstein's nerve Was broken. "Your soft little touch is going to hit us hard," 1:-.! i-poaned. Plumb swung his chair as if to brain him. A policaman sei?.ed al office .ruler n:::d struei, his arm. The chair fell with a crash to the floor, and one arm of plumb's hung, broken, at his . sia. 'lli.-3 face was white. A damp ' perspiration bro::, out over lis fore7la?ad. A spot of blood started froni where ono of his clenched teeth had torn his lip; but still he brazened it out. . "It's all a lie—a made-up lie from begin- tbh e cried,bag recklesS the. road after I—' - in H down fling to. end," he screamed. . "I never was near the house." "If you was, I wasn't," . snivelled the terni-• fled Jew; "my dying, Pieldu moath on that." • "Yotaingsherer,ir,,yolil carried liar, r'e stopped suddenly. -Ware nodded to the police. "Enough,,, he said to the inspector. " You hear what they say. Take them away. At least there is a ease." . Plumb 'sneered. " But still the article aoes in." He growled the words goadingly. , b - "NQ," 'Ware answered, "the Yellow Reef Mine is safe. They have given up, the game. I . 'phoned them just before you came in, and while they enjoy blackmail the prospect of being associated with murderers seared 'them off." Ware turned to his papers as the police and :heir prisoners left the room.</p>
        <p>ITnE. END.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>GErgIIE.TI EXAMINE' STORY,</head>
        <p>GErgIIE.TI EXAMINE' STORY,</p>
        <p>[ALL RTGIITS RESERVED.)</p>
        <p>THE SOFT HAND.</p>
        <p>BY GEORGE EDGAR</p>
        <p>Author of "Seaside Stories," "Roadside Ro. .manoes," "Love at the Prow," "he Myste- rious Minstrel," "The ,I3lind Man's (Bluff," Ste.</p>
        <p>Two men, suddenly pale through a sharp encounter with the issues of life and death, stood stupefied with amazement, gazing down on another man, paler still. The other man was dead. The elder of the two men drew in his breath between his clenched teeth, and his dry tongue formed his words huskily. " Don't stand staring there—staring don't help. He's dead—curse him !—stone dead. I've seen that colour before," he added, in a sibilant whisper. "Curse his ugly face—much good being a millionaire matters to him, or anything else. I didn't want to kill him— hang me if I did! I hit him soft." The younger man turned his eyes shiftily away from the upturned face—the foolish, futile, upturned face which still seemed to have character enough to glare an accusation. He spoke with a whimpering note of com- plaint, using his words after the manner of the Jew. "You hit him soft—did you? So help me, and a soft heart d "Soft you say! an you did. You hit him soft enough to crumple .his poor old nut like an ege:shell. ' ' The man's 'tongue— ran gotueraroautsicolfthihs hand dy a soft way with you, you have—the sort of softness that hurts when it tries to be kind. Look here, I don't like it. Murder ain't my line." " Oh ! cut it out, he's, dead," said the older man. " Murder ain't my line, but I had to hit him. It is murder, and you are in it. It was either him or us. Let's get out." He gathered some tools together lying near a safe—a drill, a file or two, and a steel wedge.' His hand slipped into the dead man's -pocket. It pulled out a leather cigarette case. "No good," he snarled, casting it aside. His hand again searched the waistcoat. This time the search yielded a sovereign purse. "Gold," he said, tearing it Open. "Ten of 'em, and notes, I think. Let's get away now, and make what we've got do. I don't like staying longer with that "—he cast a shudder- ing look behind him. "You take the bag." The older man walked to the open window and looked out._ His cruel, pale, and coarse face showed for a moment in the moonlight-- a red scar on his forehead glowed angrily, shown up by his unusually white skin. He looked out on a scene restful to the senses—a violent contrast to the scene within. Silence all around; the house in shadows,; the lawn a soft area of light under ,a smiling moon; a rustling wind stirring the shrubbery near— and cool—refreshingly cool—as it blew gently across his damp forehead. • "All right," he whispered sharply; "all is clear everywhere. We can cut now.". The younger man's Jewish voice whimpeied complainingly still. my oath!" he said, " I want to hop it. The moonlight is shining on his silly face and I don't like still faces which stare and see nothing." "Don't be a babbling fool," the older man answered curtly. "Pull yourself together and follow me." He dropped out into the garden, over the window ledge, with a catlike 'base which was practised and sure. The other man looked out, fearfully. He released a canvas bag which fell gently on the soil, and then, very quietly, he shinned over th-,- window ledge. Together they crept-2a sinister pair of jackals—out of the square of light; the old man walking warily, the younger man steal- thily with a bulky bag slung over his shoulder. They paused at the gate, and listened in the shadow of some trees. The road was silent and they ventured out. As they crept down the quiet road to safety, the thought uppermost in their minds found expression. "Strewth! I didn't mean to do him in. I only hit him soft," the elder man said. " Soft ! " the younger, Jewish an an- swered, and he clutched at the collar of his coat, as if he were strangling, and then spat out. In the library the fignre still lay on the thick pile carpet, staring out of the open case- ment at the moon and the silvery clouds scud- ding across its face. The sightless eyes of George Ware, which had looked on strange things in this world, where evil and good run in a twisted skein which no man shall unravel, would see no more. The millionaire speculator was at the end of the long journey. He lay there, until they found him next morning, and the cry of murder, started by the domestic- in his library, spread throughout the land for weeks, and then quietly died away.</p>
        <p>IL Three years after, Geoffrey Ware, a nephew of the dead man, sat alone in his office. He had inherited much of his uncle's wealth, most of his responsibilities, and all his secrets. In George Ware's life there were many secrets—secrets built into the story of his successful career which would, have played havoc with the dead man's reputation. Geoffrey Ware had spent three years fight- ing to keep the wealth he had inherited, and to squeeze the secrets into silence. They came trailing through the office like ghosts; rising in the great world outside, taking form—the form of the blackmailer with the greedy maw. He had to pay away large slices of his inherited wealth to control these spectres, to keep them rattling in the cup- board, and to prevent them walking abroad and brazening their knowledge to the world. George Ware died a man of good Jpute. He had controlled his secrets. After death his ill-repute rose like an angry tide, and threat- ened to sweep his memory with dishonour. Geoffrey Ware remained to meet these waves of dark knowledge and to fight them down. He sat in his office, in the centre of the city, the window showing glimpses of the roof of the Bank of England to the right. He was a keen, well-groomed, immaculate City man, his black hair shining, his eyes bright, his thin lips compressed with restrained strength. His small white hands, which toyed with a pen looked as strong as steel. A, clerk knocked and came in with a card. "Mr. Plumb to see you—with a friend," he said, quietly. • "I don't know Mr. Plumb," Geoffrey an- swered decisively. "Tell him to write for an appointment. After a pause: "Why don't you go?" The clerk hesitated. "Mr. Plumb said you didn't know him. He said you would see him. He said I was to say, 'Alias Wendover.' He said you would know, sir." The clerk spoke quietly, deferentially, but he was not astonished to see the managing director of the Empire Exploration Syndicate first turn an angry red and then grow pale with passion. Geoffrey's eyes narrowed. "Show him in— both of them,'! he snap strong white teeth. ped, with a click of his The two men entered. The elder was dently Mr. Plumb, for he introduced the younger as "my friend Elderstein." • Mr. rilllllb WV) about fifty years of age. He was well-dressed—in a way. His coat was well cut, his well-shaped pants were a shade too light; his patent-leather boots sparkled though they were cheap, because they Were new. His round face was clean shaven. His eyes were green with that glittering coldness which comes from alcoholism. A mark on his forehead burnt redder than his florid face. He had that indefinable air which one asso- ciates with the shady side of life—rascality on the racecourse, round the boxing-ring, or in heated saloons or West-End music-halls. _ _ _</p>
        <p>- His companion was a keen, dark Jew, with furtive eyes, a predatory nose, and strong ti.,,eth a dark brown, showing through a drooping moustache and a hanging underlip. His clothes were well cut, of a light .grey, and he carried a white felt hat. As he introducea Elderstein Plumb smiled, and Elderstein, in bowing, dropped his hat. Ware surveyed them as if he were a fight- ing man, going into action. Well? he asked, sternly. Plumb ceased smiling. Elderstein drew himself up. "You don't ask me to take a seat!' Plumb said, irtsolently. "it is warm outside, and 1 don't mind if I do."</p>
        <p>lie dropped into a chair. Lldersteui put his white het in the centre</p>
        <p>of the table and sat nonchalantly on the edge of it.</p>
        <p>Ware remained standing near his heavily padded chair. " Well? " he asked again, and this time the ward was a challenge. Plumb cleared his throat and began. "Alias Wendover," he said, nodding his head, with an annoying expression of famili- arity. "That partially explains it. You see, I can prove Ware---old Ware—was Wendover." Elderstein nodded in sympathy. "lie did time," he added. "It was forgery." "It was not," Plumb almost shouted. "It was a matter of illicit diamonds." Plumb snorted.</p>
        <p>"You've got to pay out," he said pointedly, to Geoffrey. "if you do not pay the Gold Critic will give a report of the proceedings, a photogranh of Wend Over and Ware; a descrip- tion of the polices proceedings, and they will come out on Friday morning, when you put out the Yellow Reef Prospectus." Ware winced. The dogs know the moment," he thought. . "What. is to prevent me from handing you over to the police," he asked, aloud.</p>
        <p>Plumb ziodded genially. " Nothing," be answered., " only von cz-,nnot afford to do it. You'd have to tell the story; and the al=ticj) would go out just the same. We've thought it Over from that point of view. You needn't mind us—we have nothing to 105 e.," Plumb suggested, with renewed insolence. It's a hundred to one against .you doing us in," Elderstein added. "Gentlemen, of our profession take the odds." •' "What do You ask?" Geoffrey demanded. "Two thousand pounds," was the quick re- sponse of Plumb, whose eyes twinkled with greed. "A big sum," Ware reflected,. irresolutely. "It's a big game," _ Ehlerstein answered. "We hold the Yellow Reef flotation in the hollow of our hands."</p>
        <p>He spread out his /hands as he spoke. Ware made up his i;laind.: "Let me think it over," he said. " Come back this afternoon at five."</p>
        <p>Elderstein's brown teeth showed doglike; Plumb smiled.</p>
        <p>"There is no hurry," Plumb answered, pressing on the top of the table with his thumb for emphasis. " We—want—two—thou- sand—and—you've—got—to—pay." He tapped the table with his spatulate,thumb as he spoke. Then, rising, "Ready Isaac," he said, look- ing at the Jew. "I'm with you, old Evergreen," Elder. stein said, slipping from the table. The two men passed out of the room like evil shadows. Ware was left looking at the corner of the table where Plumb had sat. He was thinking deeply. Half an hour after, he could almost see and hear Plumb, as he sat, confidently expounding the situation, with his fat expansive thumb 0 ba—ltina time to. flth words, as it struck the table.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>111.</head>
        <p>111.</p>
        <p>Fo, some minutes Ware remained staring at the table. He knew the position _too well. Of course, he would have to pay. At any time he would have had to arrange some kind of a deal with then—now he had: no option but to pay their price. On the eve of their greatest flotation the syndicate could not stand a whisper about the morality of the founder of the firm, and he, as its representa- tive, would have to pay for silence. But— two thousand pounds! The price stuck in his throat.</p>
        <p>"This city of secrets," he pondered wearily. " I wonder if everyone in the city has secrets like mine, and is a prey to such vermin day by day? What would I not give to have clean hands and to be free of all this chicanery. Always to bear this kind of guilty knowledge; to add more; to go on riding the Whirlwind of dishonour; to set the darker side of life running like a snowball, and to go on pushing the ball, as it daily becomes a heavier burden—is intolerable."</p>
        <p>Geoffrey sat staring at the table minute after minute. Then his eyes began to focus things distinctly—his returning control gave back the power of consciousness to external facts.</p>
        <p>His eyes lingered on the; thumb prints left by the smiling, insinuating, insolent 'Plumb. "I'd give a clear thOusand to bang his round head where he trounced the table with his thumb," he said, savagely, to himself. And then—his mind suddenly groped back- wards to a memory. He gazed at the marks on the table and his figure stiffened. Hastily he reached for a jeweller's enlarging glass and focussed the thumb prints. He noted their curious formation and a broken scarlike inter- ruption of the circular lines on the ball of the thumb. " Whew ! " he muttered, "what a start- lingly odd coincidence." Eagerly he ,turned out a drawer in his private desk until he found exactly what he had looked for—a photograph. For five minutes he studied the photograph intently, and from time to time examined the thumb prints on the table.</p>
        <p>"The very same," he said at length. "Now, whatever happens to the syndicate's flotation, one rascal is going to get the shock of his life. I'll see to that," and his eyes glistened with the light of battle. He was busy during the afternoon, arrang- ing Many things. When Ave o'clock struck he sat waiting. He sat at his great desk, idly Playing with a steel paper knife—waiting. Thera was a grim, cold, masterful smile, on his thin lips. The outer clocks had barely chimed five when the two men—Plumb and Elderstein— presented themselves. They were immedi- ately shown in. Both were inclined to be insolent—through. loitering and drinking in the city, to, pass the time, the subtle air of rascality about them had grown more sinister. Plumb sat down, again uninvited. Elder- stein sought the corner of the table and swung his leg nonchalantly as he sat there. The older man was smiling confidently, but when he looked at the set, stern face of Ware his manner changed, and though the smile remained it was forced. " Well? " said Plumb, aggressively, delibe- rately using Ware's own words. " " echoed Elderstein.</p>
        <p>The Jew looked for the first time into the face of Ware and winced. "I; have decided-" Ware said tersely. "To pay out, eh?" asked Plumb, with a corrupt smile, meant to be pleasant. "To pay out," Ware answered, slowly, "and to pay back." Plumb ceased smiling, and looked steadily nt Ware. The Jew's leg stopped its non- chalant swinging. "What do you mean?" Plumb blustered, angrily. "' To pay back,' you say! You don't owe us anything you've got to pay back." "There are debts which some people would prefer to forget," he said tersely. His manner was breaking down the Jew's nerve.</p>
        <p>"Look here," the older man shouted; "I don't like your looks, and I don't like your talk. If you are going to throw us down, say so." What I have to say is soon said," Ware replied. "The night my uncle, George Ware-"</p>
        <p>Both men started, and the Jew turned pale as he jumped from the table. "What I have to say is soon said, if your friend Elderstein will refrain from interrupt- ing," Ware went on, icily. "The night my uncle' George Ware, was found foully mur- dered there was no clue. It is three years ago, and the murderer still holds the secret." Plumb smiled as the tension slackened in his drumming pulses. "The only sign left in that still house, on the night of the murder, w a leather cigar- ette case with a thumb print on it—as clear as if it had been etched there. I have still that case and a photograph of that print." Plumb looked at the Jew, and the Jew's eyes cringed. "To-day," continued Ware, "the man who left his thumb print on my uncle's case called here and made a dozen exact copies of that print on my table. He made them with his own thumb.</p>
        <p>Elderstein's face turned livid. "If I may die here, I haven't touched the—" he be- gan, clutching his .hat as he epoke. Plumb turned round on him with a snarl. "Hold your trap," he shouted. "As for you "—he turned again to Ware—" bluff will not help you."' "It is the truth," Ware said; "there is no bluff. The thumb that marked my uncle's cigarette ease marked. my table to-day, and that thumb was yours. I have never seen your thumb, hut there is something on it you can- not conceal—the mark of an old scar.</p>
        <p>Plumb started. Almost involuntarily his sight hai4 slipped into hie pocket, Wiwi he</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>.10 –</head>
        <p>.10 -</p>
        <p>A TWICE TOLD TALE.</p>
        <p>Good news bears repeating, and when it is confirmed after a long lapse of time, even if we hesitated to believe it at first hearing, we feel secure in accepting its truth now. The follow- ing• experience of a Warrington man is con- firmed after two years. NEARLY TWO YEARS AGO, Mr. C. W. King, of "Cheshire View," '268, Manchester-road, War- rington, said :—" I caught a severe chill, which list have affected my kidneys I think , because I had an attack of sciatica afterwards, which laid me up for three months. ' A. sharp, cutting pain used to start in 'my left hip, and shoot right down to my ,knee. I was in dreadful pain night and day.- "At times I was almost hopeless. I could not get any sleep at night, and was confined to the house for three .months. "I tried many remedies before I used Doan's backache kidney pills, but was unable .to get any relief. This medicine, however, sobn did me good, and by th,b time I had taken five -boxes I was quite cured A and able to return to my work. I am only too pleased to give this account of my experience for the benefit of other sufferers. I have every reason to speak highly of Doan's pills, and now I never miss an opportunity of recommending them. (Signed) C. W. KING." NEARLY TWO YEARS LATER, Mr. King says :—." I have been keeping first-rate since Doan's -baek- ache kidney pills cured me. I take a pill or two new and again in order to keep me right." price 2s. 9d. a box, 6 boxes 13s. 91.; of all dealers. or from Foster-McClellan Co., 8, Wells- street, Oxford-street, London, W. Don't ask for backao.e and kidney pills—ask DISTINCTLY for DoAN's backache pills, the same as Mr. King had. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>REMARKABLE AMERICAN.</head>
        <p>A REMARKABLE AMERICAN.</p>
        <p>Probably the unique titular distinction in' the world is borne by an American citizen, and an intensely democratic one at that. Its possession does not privilege Ernest K. Coulter to append any part of the alphabet to his name. The only outward sign of Coulter's title lieS\ in his smil- ing, sympathetic face. There is something of tenderness tempering strength in the eyes and mouth, of the man that Puzzles you a. bit until you learn its source. Then you understand; for Ernest Coulter is the orginal "Big Brother," the 'born, steadfast, unwavering .big brother' of every ragged, unfortunate .street gamin in the world. The Big Brother.ing habit got a firm strong- hold on Coulter long before he came to' New Yoric it nearly cost him his first Job. Gradu- ating from the Ohio State University. in 1893, he' secured a position as 'cub reporter on a Pitts- burgh paper. Here he evinced so much interest in the street urchins of the Smoky City that his city editor informed him he had a mistaken notion of the natural functions of a newspaper; that it was to gather and dispense news, not to run a slum kindergarten. Thereafter he suc- ceeded in creating a purely imaginary line be- tween.his vocation and his avocation, and held on to both.</p>
        <p>eveloping into a first-class reporter, he - D gravitated toWard New York a few years later, and 'naturally also gravitated to the East .Side, where he again began his task of -winning the friendship of the shrewd, suspicious young denizens of the streets. Of course Ile. suc- ceeded; for even then the born Big Brother understood the gamin's view-point of life 11 probably no other man. Quickly the word was spread that " dat guy is on de level," arm Coulter was adinitted to their friendship. And the friendship of these youngsters knows no reservation. Coulter soon learned of co7•iitins that appalled him. One remedial chance he saw, that of correcting the absolute injustice of classing unrestrained, mischievous boys as criminals, and meting out the, same sort of punishment as is given the confirmed crook. He believed, and subsequent events have demon- strated the truth of his conviction, that there were few really bad boys ; all any of them needed was a fair chance to understand, and a real opportunity to get out, of habits creal2d by an environment_ into which they WPrO- LOrrl.</p>
        <p>In 1902 the Children's Court was New York, and in recognition of his work for its establishment Coulter was made its clerk. From that day the avocation became a life task, and the Big Brother came into his own. To-day he is practising law ; but helping his boys still remains Coulters real life-work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>LIBERAL CLUBS' UNION.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL CLUBS' UNION.</p>
        <p>Fourteen clubs, making the total nearly SO. were admitted to membership .of the National Union of Liberal Clubs at a meeting of the Executive Committee of that body held at Old- ham on Saturday. Letters were read from Mr. Winston Churchill (president of the union) and Mr. Lloyd George, respecting the next annual meeting of delegates for the federated clubs. The matter was left in the hands'- of the Chairman and the Honorary Secretary (Mr. J. Cook, of Shipley). Mr. L. V. Harcourt, M.P., was elected a vice-president of the unions</p>
        <p>It was agreed that an address setting forth the Objects of the National Union be prepared for presentation' to the Liberal members of Par- liament with a view to enlisting their active co- operation in an effort to increase the usefulness of Liberal clubs in relation to political work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>3, RIDGEFIELD. KING-STREET, MANCHESTER.</head>
