      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>MEWS QUESTIONS.</head>
        <p>MEWS QUESTIONS.</p>
        <p>Female Scientists Apply Ilesults to Remedying Evils.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>cliaL GRADUATES IN BUSINESS.</head>
        <p>cliaL GRADUATES IN BUSINESS.</p>
        <p>Lady Boot who is offering encouragement to university girls to take up shop life, has Played no small part in the building up of her hus- band's huge businesg, and has a seat on the Board of Directors. She is offering positions to a number of girls educated at Girton, and similar colleges,- as distinct from the ordinary school-trained girl. Lady Boot is of opinion that women of this class will rind plenty of scope in shopland for their intellectual abilities, with the possibility of eventually earning a lucrative salary, and at the same time seeing the active side of life. All this. may be true, but it is not exactly to start life as a shop assist- ant that parents send their girls to Newnham, Girton, or the variotis Oxford halls, and such a training does not usually fit a girl with a tem- perament suitable for the work of an assistant in a store. She would find it difficult to be sufficiently respectful to an uneducated cus- tomer or shop walker, wh6 affected to order her about in a brusque 'manner; and it would not be easy for her to adapt herself to such sur- roundings. Then for such work, it is no use having brains unless one has physical strength, for long hours in a. shop means at the best a hard life. iris true there are highly-paid sales- women in the. large ' establishments, but they are the comparatively few, and the great majority of shop assistants finch it no small struggle, to make both ends meet.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>RADIUM TEN YEARS HENCE.</head>
        <p>RADIUM TEN YEARS HENCE.</p>
        <p>Madame Curie, whom the British Association has delighted to honour, and who was intro- duced to the members by Sir Oliver Lodge as "the greatest woman of science of all time," advises the English nation te watch Dr. Ruther- ford, whose work in radio-activity surprised her greatly. Great developments, Madame Curie prophesies, will. transpire shortly, to which the discovery of radium wag only the preliminary. In discussing with men of science the difficul- ties and possibilities of radium, the great Frenchwoman, who, however, is by birth a Pole, took the lead, although she avoids public speaking. Her keenness was delightful to watch, and her close set lips and clear eyes re- vealed an active and brilliant intellect. She thinks that the next ten years has great things in store for the world, through the medium of radium.</p>
        <p>MISS SARGANT AND THE BRITISH ASSOCIA- TION.</p>
        <p>The honour conferred upon Miss Ethel Sar- gala is somewhat behited, but it is 0: triumph for women, all the ,same. There are, however, numbers Of the sx equally fitted and capable of filling prominent positions in the worlds of Art, Literature, and Science, from which they rare debarred from doing by the prejudice of the other sex. Miss Sargant deserves the honour of being the first woman elected as president 'of a section of the British- Association. Her work in the field of science had previously gained her a fellowship of the Linnean Society. The paper she read shows with what intense Interest she enters into scientific questions. All the women's papers exhibited a superiority over old debaters, who left the subjects they handled in a state of nebulosity, to be rendered clear at some future discussion, women, however, have shown a tendency to apply the results of their investigations to the remedying of evils and to the urgent needs of the present day.</p>
        <p>NEW FASHIONS IN PELTRY. Fur coats are much more fanciful in cut this year than last, and two or three furs are fre- Elli _ently used on one garment. There .is much working of pelts in various patterns in circular and diagonal lines, also many combinations, such, for instance; as mole b and musquash, coney and ringtail opossum, Persian lamb and coney, &amp;c. Short and threequarter coats are shown in the best houses; but the full-length coats, \Vitt sloped fronts, clipped in closely at the knee, are decidedly the most popular. Many of the newest fur coats have belts also of fur that are placed considerably below the waist. line. Sortie of the shorter coats are in dolman style, and made in Persidn lamb. These are rather fully shaped. The skins of this beautiful fur this season lack the fine silky curl which is so 'highly prized, consequently it is ,somewliat lower in price.. For general wear the pony skin is most in demand. ; This pelt dyes a good black. The best coatsUre made from foal skins, and these are beautifully worked and very supple in texture.</p>
        <p>FASHION'S FAVOURITE. The blouse this season is not only indispens- able, considered by itself as a garment, but it is chosen as the model for the corsages of all elaborate dresses, for these are one and all pro- vided with a loose-fitting bodice, which . to all intents and purposes is a blouse. In its most captivating guise, this evergreen favourite of fashiOn is expressed in the soft bitche crepes, that won a place for themselves as soon as they appeared. Only some of the new blouses are decorated with sashes, and there are par- ticularly novel and smart. .The evening dresses of last season were, when made of lace, mounted on flesh pink net, or just as often net on pink ninon, which gave a pleasing faint rose effect under the upper transparency. This idea has now been exploited in the case of the evening blouse, for net and lace are again very much in evidence in the latest designs, and with these two..materials fur is frequently combined. Ecru net, with touches of black, is again favoured, and plain crepe de chine, silk Crepon, and the soft Makes of satin, in bright colours, are still popular for shirts and the plainer types of blouses..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>DEVELOPMENT OF THE BLOUSE</head>
        <p>THE DEVELOPMENT OF THE BLOUSE</p>
        <p>GOWN..</p>
        <p>Every 'season, the ready-to-wear gown is an improvement on its predecessor, however ex- cellent that may ,have been in cut and make. This autumn is no exception to the rule, and it is -doubtful whether one would get as good value for money by buying materials and hav- ing them made up by a local dressmaker, as by purchasing a ,F.eady-to-wear gown. For the woman of moderate means, and especially the busy'. woman, fo whom time is of special im- portance, the blouse-gowns of this winter are a boon ,and a blessing. For a guinea, a smart little • silk robe, suitable for semi-evening and theatre wear, can he procured.. For treble that sum, _quite delightful dresses, trimmed with fur or handsome beaded embroideries, 'some- times with both, are obtainable. And an in- expensive type of dinner gown in various black materials, suitable fOr such functions, appears to be a speciality introduced for the first time</p>
        <p>Continued in next column</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>WAR IN THE INSECT W3RLD.</head>
        <p>WAR IN THE INSECT W3RLD.</p>
        <p>Setting insects to attack other insects that do harm to crops is a comparatively recent Pr(); cedure, but it has already proved so successful, that the method is now to be tried in a differen' form—that of setting insects to destroy certain, ticks which spread disease among beasts an" men.'</p>
        <p>The special ticks against which this new Warr is directed travel by the euphonious names °I Ixodes Ilicinus and Dermacentor Venustus. The first is very common on dogs and cattle and ist known to be the carrier of serious fever tint infects the herds. The second is especially eonr mon in the Rocky Mountains, where it coin" municates the so-called "purple fever" human beings. This is a sort of typhus, whim causes many deaths every year, and is said t° be the principal reason for vast districts being allowed to remain desert.</p>
        <p>The Dermacentor has been studied for years,' but up to the present no means of extirpatirt it have been discovered, except the slaughter 01 all wild animals known to harbour it, the wash- ing of domestic animals in arsenious acid or tar' and the: burning of brushwood. Now United States Bureau of Entomology is imPOl4,. ing a certain fly named Ixodiphagus Caucurio• which has the usefuLhabit of selecting the living Dermacentor for a nest, boring a hole in back and laying from five to ten eggs in body. These _eggs soon hatch into tiny grubs. which proceed to eat the insides, of the Derr centor. In four or five weeks they have deve!i oped into adult flies and they climb out 115 1„ from a chrysalis. leaving the Dermacentm nothing but a hollow shell. The Ixodiphagns, is about, one millimetre long. It has been use(' so successfully in parts of France that certain noxious ticks have entirely disappeared.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>TEARS OF PAIN.</head>
        <p>TEARS OF PAIN.</p>
