        <p>BOAT THAT FLIES.</p>
        <p>A flying machine which is in part a bon' to be introduced to the Admiralty shortly Mr. Glen . Curtiss, the famous American air man, who has arrived in London. "Before I evolved my flying boat," he sai,,di "we would not receive any women pupils our instruction, schools at San Diego and Hail', mond-sport. But I consider this machine tit" safe that with it flying has lost its danger, women need have, no apprehensions. We about \5O pupils undergoing instruction at tll4 two schools. "The second flying boat I built is. still in Ilse," for instruction purposes,And has travelled ove:i 50,000 'miles. .Altogether 'we have turned otg nearly 40 of the machines, which are now flYirl„o ,in different parts of the world. The Aineric" navy has adopted the type." • A Chicago business man named McCornll- , has discarded an automobile in favour el tile flying boat, and regularly traverses through air the distance between his country home Wilt his office in. Harvester Building, twentY-eig miles away.</p>
