      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>GREAT CLIMAX OF THE PLAY.</head>
        <p>THE GREAT CLIMAX OF THE PLAY.</p>
        <p>tilc", new play, •. The Ulster Refugees," by a ented celebiity, will shortly be put upon the „ilteal stage. We were privileged to be present 'tho rehearsal. The piece represents what, in e fancy of the writer, is to occur a yOr hence wh ti 'en the minority of the people of Ulster, par- 4,eillarlY of Belfast, are to rise in rebellion r.6ailist King, Government, and people, and pre- ;lPitate Civil War in the North of Ireland. The ;°eal interest of the piece is attained by the sup- ion that when they begin their war their (Crnen' and children wilr be in imminent Vger (from what is not quite clear). the members of the Warrington Women's Reform League, " from ' the bottom of L:'_elr Warm, womanly hearts," are then to offer rOteetion to over a thousand of these women children, and the Play represents the corn- of the Refugees. They march in proces- ,ton • to their Warrington refuge under the leadership of the author, who, mounted on a fl,erY charger, takes the part of hero and i'helleral. The procession is well staged, and 7„ Manner in which the hero ads his part de- warm praise. A banner, which the ton's Tariff Reform League has presented fe,ii"ue author, heads the procession. Closely foi'eWing, and in the place of honour, are the dn'stocratic representatives of the class long fir;4l,Mant in the North of Ireland. One c,lent dame, who is followed by her attend- 0, asks her neighbour "If she thinks there Btlll be any select society, people of leisure and loYle, in the town or district, with which it will advisable to associate." Behind these, and NeenPying a ,central position is the bold nted leader. He has placed himself be- hween the aristocrats and the shipyard women, the indicating by subtle suggestion that he th 'lle long-lost missing link joining together e extremes of society. , ,thr ()flowing the shipyard women are the glum :ter Presbyterians; ,they only take things eitiously, but appear somewhat out of their eAtnent. th: good deal of fun is introduced by one of •‘‘,l,`" Shipyard 'women, by name Mary Mullins, thllo with a good deal of sauce puts forward • Ehe request to the brave leader that "their lot" tt,,e.uld have rooms at Walton. 'The hero Ives to silence her by intimating that this 'Coveted accommodation was all booked weeks (3tio, but that he would try and secure them Mfortable rooms at Pig Hill. At this the gets very irate, and Li,sks if he does not think they get enough of the company of pigs ot th nome, and threatens the hero with a dose of e Medicine their gallant men folk gave to the</p>
        <p>Baleildfarset lisghiiopuysaradssc'efnoid' atnheeyi,naain'nd- Ctt eh ane t hno Ocl enc osoufirncoitvhfielKing Carson and of King Wil- liam of glorious memory. "It's a d—d shame," shouts fhe lady, "after all our lads have PDC fOr the cause." Fortunately for the safety of our hero a diver- sion is created by a general stampede of the shipyard crowd to a neighbouring " public," where. free drinks are kindly provided for the weary travellers. The thirst 'of Mary Mullins is stronger than her desire to roast the author, who makes a providential escape. Consequent:on this diversion, it is long before the leader gets his procession again on the move; several of the women loudly _affirming that they won't go home till morning. • The hero attempts a rally, he gets the Presbyterian section to sing a psalm, but this falls flat. He then bids the Orange section beat loudly upon' their drums, but the din makes the con- fusion Worse. An attempt is made to start the National Anthem, but the 'strain drags, the 'vul- gar jeer, and the leader is for -a time sadly dis- traught. He (hereupon:borrowing from a well- known play, and with telling dramatie effect, gives forth the following : The time is out of joint; 0 cursed spite, That ever I was born to set it right! This is received with great applause by those around. Mary Mullins and her crowd do not understand what he is talking about, but they think his acting so " foine" that they give him a special cheer, garner round, and press upon him what little remains of their "free drinks." "Drink to the success of the revolution," say they, and he drinks, as drink he inust. Then Acting on a. happy inspiration, he saves the situation by seizing and holding aloft a banner, up:in which is represented the dtstinguished features of another king. The bait takes!</p>
        <p>"God save the with sa --ne;v—mane-a-n.ing, is again started, and taken up, with spirit by the whole crowd. At length the procession is got into some sort of order. The march then goes merrily on. Snatches of songs, " Boyne Water," "We'll hang John Redmond ,to a sour apple tree," "Dolly's Brae," enliven the way. After a series of incidents of a minor character they arrive at Warrington, and the climax is reached when the company is received and wel- comed in Sankey-street with great rejoicing by the six thousand "warm, womanly hearts" of the Women's Tariff Reform League. All the women, the children, banner bearers, and other oddments, are made honorary members of the Warrington .Woments Tariff Reform 'League.' At this increase of members, all 'of</p>
        <p>whom beeorne ardent Tariff Reformers right away, there is a great shout of triumph, and the _curtain drops.</p>
        <p>We are afraid unless the piece is consider- ably Altered, the public will be at a loss to know whether it is intended as trßgedy or -comedy. The .authOr hs cleArly set out with the inten- tion of writing a serious play; at least, he evi- dently desires his audience to think so—not quite the same thing. He wants the piece to curdle the blood, strike terror into Warrington Radicals and Home Rulers, and above all, make everybody vote for a popular actor, the accomplished player to the gallery. But his company do not appear to have caught on, have not grasped his purpose, and all through seem to think they have been invited to take part in a farce, and act accordingly.</p>
