      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>ULSTER ARITHMETIC.</head>
        <p>ULSTER ARITHMETIC.</p>
        <p>rp TT E rn 71 T 1 11l Ito</p>
        <p>\iiADIA_</p>
        <p>_RY : "We're loyal, Carson, that's pwhat we are! Loyal to the backbone !" Shure we are ! An', bedad, if they woa't belay,: us we'll '6 litart an arm.:..l rebelion to prove it</p>
        <p>The showman side of the Carsonite • Move- Ment peeps out rather delightfully in its arith- metic. Here is the curious case of last Satur— day's Belfast review. The " force " was sup- posed to consist of 14 battalions, each 800 strong, which would make 11,200 in all. The correspondent 'of the "Westminster (gazette," however, had it very carefully 'counted, and .found that it totalled. 7,960. *Nevertheless, the Carsonite "Northern Whig," of Belfast, came out on the Monday with a leading article, trum- peting the' lisplay of " 12,000 " volunteers, i.e., a whole battalion more than there were even pretended to be. Sir Edward Carson' himself scorns such petty exaggerations. Speaking on Wednesday at Dungannon,he saicl : Someone ,has expressed curiosity in the Press as to what I was thinking about at the Belfast review. I was thinking of the fifteen thousand men I saw before me.</p>
        <p>So here we have it in a nutshell—official reckon- ing, 11,200; actual count, 7,960; Carsonite reck- oning (two days after the event), 12,000'; Sir Edward Carson's reckoning (four day's after the event), 15,000. COmment isoreally superfluous. But we must be allowed one further example. If Belfast at the high-water mark of agitation can 'produce 7,960 volunteers, then the • whole of Ulster, if it followed the same ratio to popula- tion (and no sane man .supposes that it could) would produce about 30,000. The regular Car- sonite figure, as everyone knows, is 100,000 !- 'Daily Chronicle."</p>
        <p>From Reynolds's Newsparer.</p>
        <p>C , ..,... N 1 S. (1. . ,11,111,1\1 1\ \ll\‘\ i) i .1\11!\:\ i 1 111 1 ( . •((..... 1 , 1, .:,-,-it ~ • „.•kw.,-•,,,.;v. '1 \ i „.,,...,,44,1\ , ' , ''''',A. ~. • , ,C Il \\ 1%. I cp. , . k, 'l4t , ~: t l i ~,-. , -- . . , t r CrD \ I 1 11 t ..14t t ' ' \ eTh C ', .' ' i 1 I ' .• k(' ' , . 1.,1i' - 1 4P * .., 4 . _ -,,.... , 7 L • 1/4..._ ........„... • „ , ~.,,,• ......„:„,-,....-. _ r • , ; • 'll I I 11' i ,i -y.. '--- N' • / illit ), .1i,A14 . (-. ~ , I V _- • ____:--e--. . . O\V • , t •:i ,;:l'''\''..:\-.o\ti j'it...,l. ~1:•:: 1 / ----5-rj" . --,,,.• 400 , , , . I - I . , sosl.t.:A . • . . - :.--- . , .. ...., - „ - , \ , ~ . 42", A --.------- ~,.4_4±-' /P,&lt;-• ---........_______ --- -----*..,- /"4')-- " , r ..._, _ \. A 4. . ------ 0,,,_ . ..:- . -i, \ ~.... ~ 7/ 4. ~..,.i it . ' k 7 / \ LONDDNDER. 'TAR ON: " -,..._</p>
