      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>CARSON'S ARMY.</head>
        <p>CARSON'S ARMY.</p>
        <p>A Mob that Would Commit Suicide by Takiag to the Field.</p>
        <p>COULDN'T': "FORM FOURS."</p>
        <p>Mrs. Handel Booth and the Straggling, Waving Line that was Out of Step.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Handel Booth sends to the "Westminster Gazette" an amusing account of one of Sir Edward Carson's reviews of Ulster volunteers— "the greatest comedy," she says, "it has ever been my fortune to witness."</p>
        <p>Folks about here (she writes) say _these re- views are held for the benefit of the .English newspapers, or to ,guy the Saxon across- the: water. Mr. F. E. Smith, Captain Ciaig and Sir E. Carson drove, into the town with a body- guard of twentyeight mounted men, their steeds varying in size' from a cob to a heavy dray horse; the ,pace was 'uneven,. and many of the riders were obviously ill at ease, and were glad to dismount at the entrance to the field .where the members of the Orange Lodges from the surrounding districts were drawn up; some of the- men oozing over into-the adjacent cauli- flowers, which were mercilessly slaughtered. The sashes . and insignia of the lodges consti- tuted ,the uniform, and bowler hats were much in evidence. The officers wore Boy Scout cos- tumes decorated with yellow dahlias, and carried walking-sticks. 1 The rank and file I were unarmed—no signs of the rifles and cannon which have so long terrorised the peace- ful inhabitants of the sister isle.. In the front row, opposite the Chairman, stood a weedy,</p>
        <p>anmmic youth of sixteen, with bent shoulders and quaking knees; a kindly English M.P., who shall-be nameless, stepped down and asked-him to\pass into a row further back before the photographers commenced operations. Sir Edward Carson, clad idl in black, walked up and down the rows of men. He looked sad. He .was perhaps thinking of the Irish Regular regi- ments, and wishing his awkward squads looked a little more like them.</p>
        <p>Then came the military manceuvres. An .attempt was made to Akt, the men into line, but</p>
        <p>the word of command was insufficient, and an officer (?) rushed down the back of the line,</p>
        <p>frantically qapping men on the shoulder with a stick, or giving them a push to get the,,,m into place. "Form fours !" came the command, and the men started counting their neighbours, and shuffling about the field. But the march forward was- the great coup, when a straggling, wavy line of farm yokels, chauffeurs, grooms and veterans of seventy winters stumbled forward in rag-time, keeping no kind of step, and caus- ing much hilarity to the onlookers. A spectq- tor remarked, "We are tolerant folks about here," and so I thought when the Ulster mem- bers drove through• a hostile crowd, which, however, uttered no sound. From one end of , the town to. the other not a cheer was raised, not a handkerchief fluttered; not a hand was waved. Then the meeting began, and the back rows of the army melted away to seek liquid refreshment. One man remounting his horse had to be assisted by three boys, who, after two futile attempts, hoisted him into the saddle. In all the speeehes the remark that it was unthinkable that circumstances could 1:11-.Ige wherein their services would be required was received with cheers. and, heaving deep sighs of relief, the companies dispersed.</p>
      </div>
