      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 4, 1913. 'OF INTEREST TO WOMEN.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Some Subjects that are More Important than Fashion.</head>
        <p>Some Subjects that are More Important than Fashion.</p>
        <p>THE , FEMINIST COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>"Of Interest to Women." This title too often</p>
        <p>heads a column on ,that everlasting subject, ' dress ; what is being worn in Paris, in the new- est comedy, at smart functions, whether skirts will be tighter or blouse necks more decollete. Or else there will be a disquisition on how to re- move blemishes from the skin by electric or other remedies, how to make the hair grow, and so forth. Are-these really the main and staple subjects of interest to women? If that is the old type of feminine mind, the sooner we out- grow it, the better, surely, for the nation's sake. I have often thought that not a little of this eternal dress and beauty interest has been, foisted on to women by adroit journalistic pens, for the purposes of exploitation. We have more brains, more good sense, and more breadth of perception than most of the Press would lead one to think. Only this last week I have been much struck by several items of news, which should be of absorbing interest to women. First, there is the announcement in the speech from the Throne by Queen Wilhelmina of Hol- land, that the Governintnt Ihere will back woman suffrage. What a contrast to ours!</p>
        <p>An Irish Paradox.</p>
        <p>Then there has been the amazing spectacle of violent anti-suffragists announcing in ' their "Ulster provisional government" scheme that woman suffrage should be included as one Of its item'! Yes; despite Sir Ed. Carson, Mr. F. E. Smith and the "Times," there it Stands. Shall I guess that there is something in the nature of a bribe about this offer? Or do the Ulster leaders honestly believe in the reason they advance for it, namely, "that women should be -given their proper share in. the management of , the affairs of Ulster, whilst we are holding" the province in trust for the-British nation, in which matter- we fully realise that their interests re as Much at stake as ,those of men." As on- lookers at present-day politics, women may well be struck dumb with amazement at this curious state of affairs. Here are the Nationalists, the bulk of Whom are in favour of woman suffrage,. destroying our chances of a Bill, for "tactical reasons," and here are the Carsonites, avowed anti-suffragists, offering us the vote in their pro- visional government, with what'motiVes we can Only surmise. Anyway, how much does all this help our belief in the sincerity, honour and 'good faith of .present-day politics! I think it is a clear sign that new life. new ideals, and a gher standard of good faith are needed there.</p>
        <p>Miners and Women.</p>
        <p>Next week the Miners' - Federation holds its Congress at Scarborough, This Federation is Wet strongest in the Trade Union Congress, its Memberihip extending to oyez 600,000. A special suffrage' demonstration is to be held for the gathering in the theatre. - Miss I. 0. Ford; one of the ndblest and most practical ;women in England to-day, will be in the -ftair ; Miss Muriel Matters, the well-known and indefatig- able organiser, will speak; and the niiners are officially sending as representatives to this -de- monStration Mr. William Brace, M.P., Mr. Albert Stanley; M7P., and Mr. Jahn Robertson, V.-P. of the Scottish Miners' Federation. It should be a grand gathering, and we may be sure of a fair hearing and genuine•baeking from- " the one party which has kept its. word to us." Many suffragists rather naturally now believe that the only gentlemew(i.d., men of their word) now le-ft in polities as, a .party is the Labour</p>
        <p>party. And when they ask whether such at opinion is not .consistent with the treatmen meted out to our Bills In the last few years, i is very hard to answer Them..</p>
        <p>InternatiOnal Council.</p>
        <p>On top ,of these items of activity amongst women, comes .a most interesting account, sent rue by a French friend, of the International Feminist Council,-to be held in Bone•next May. This'Council, as its name conveys, is drawn from all the civilised countries of the globe, and Meets once every five years. It is organised in a very perfect manner. There are seven chief commissions that sit, :namely, those on law, peace, suffrage, public health, education,, emi- gration, and to 'stop the traffic in women. The ,main objects urged by each of these .coinmis7 sions are such as should surely afipeal to every woman of common sense and common sym- pathy in the country,- and if the •Editor Will kindly grant me a little more room at a later date, \I will 'set forth fiTe chief ones. But to-day</p>
        <p>I have no doubt more than exceeded the.space allotted .to subjects of interest to women, and will close with sincere thanks to the courtesy which includes this article.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>T. H. CROSFIELD,</head>
        <p>T. H. CROSFIELD,</p>
        <p>NE ll LIBERAL IA MIEN'S UNION.</p>
        <p>BY A LADY CORRESPONDENT.</p>
        <p>A Liberal Women's Suffrage Union has been formed lately, as the outcome of the defeated resolution brought forward at the Council meet- ing octhe Woinen's Liberal Federation, by the Tunbridge Wells Women's Liberal Association. This resolution, which was defeated by about 600 votes to, 300 votes, was as follow"NWomen's Liberal Association is eligible' for membership of the Federation unless it is prepared to abstain from working for Xnti- Suffrage Liberal candidates." Those Who were foremost in promoting this resolution have now formed the Liberal Women's Suffrage Union, of which tne object is defined as f011ows:</p>
        <p>"To promote among Liberal women such a strong -conviction* of the need of women's en- franchisement that they shall take all possible constitutional means to secure the adoption of Liberal candirates in favour of Woman suffrage, and that they shall abstain froth working for any anti-suffrage candidate." Mrs. Francis Acland wrote on the new Union</p>
        <p>in the "Englishwoman," and said: "The Union will be a voluntary association of women Who, so far from putting suffrage before party, desire to unite Suffrage and party forces; with the object of securing a suffrage measure as part of the next Liberal programme, and thus bringing to an end the miserable situation of a Liberal party at loggerheads with an essentially Liberal movement—a situation which Liberal women deplore as much for the sake of their party as of suffrage." . This lady further says Mat all Liberal women dt'e eligible for inemberglip on condition- " that•they ,urillertake not to work for any anti-suffrage Liberal until after the next General Election."</p>
        <p>The offices of the Union are at Denison House, 296, Vauxhall Bridge-road, S.W. The officers are Mrs. Mac/aren (chairman), Lady Abercon- way (vice-chairman); hon. - secretaries, Mrs. Heron Maxwell and Mrs. Francis Acland ; hon.</p>
        <p>reasurprs, Lady Cow dray and Lady Norman</p>
        <p>I should like to menlion, adds our correspond- ent, that every Saturday "The Common Cause," the non-militants' weekly paper, is on sale in the streets or Warrington, and we hope that the public will at least help us by investing in the paper, and also by reading it; so that they will he "au fait" with the doings of our societies. We trust also that it will be the means of in-</p>
        <p>creasing the membership of the Warrington branch of the Women's Suffrage Society, so that they will rally round us in the great fight for freedom which is before us. Liberals in War- rington, men and women, you like fair play, and you know that this is, as Mrs. Acland states, a "Liberal movement " ; then come, and help us by joining our society, in orler to bring about as soon as possible he enfranchisement of women. '</p>
        <p>Would Try to Arrange It., "Can I borrow your umbrella?" "I don't know. Wombat has it and he.seldom lets go of anything he has. I'll give you an order on him for it, however, and you can try your luck."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>PROGRESS OF HUMANITY.</head>
        <p>THE PROGRESS OF HUMANITY.</p>
        <p>Is human Nature a Fixed Quantity</p>
        <p>or Progressive?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>OUR BROTHER'S KEEPER.</head>
        <p>OUR BROTHER'S KEEPER.</p>
        <p>Professor Benjamin Moore, of Liverpool University, delivered a lecture on "Education and Progress" at the Adult School, Academy- street,, on Saturday evening, before a la:rt. t. audience. Mr. J. M. Murray, Director of 'Ed'u- cation, presided in the unavoidable absence of Mr. J. A. Bansome.</p>
        <p>*PROFESSOR MOORE ON SOCIAL DUTIES. Professor MOORE disclaimed any intention of speaking of the education and progress which meant the "education" of a boy sso that he might become a clerk instead of an iron worker, and so save him from soiling his hands. He intended rather to speak of the progress that would flow from true knowledge and would result in the prom.otion of that evolution that would make life better for ourselves and the generations to come. We wanted to foster the power for thinking out things instead of read- ing about them; a very rare gift, which was much more valuable than book learning. The object of the Workers' Education Association was to set people thinking for themselves about the problems of life, without which we were not going to progress. The education of thoughtfulness and the progress of the commu- nity were-the education and the progress he had in Mind.</p>
        <p>The degenerates of our civilisation—who were responsible for thein?. They themselves or others who had such social problems before them every day? It behoved us to give these degenerates a: chance: We could see, al any rate, that the children were removed- from their horrible surroundings and that they had Some- thing bright, and beautiful put before them. If we were to progress we must all progress to- gether; we must be our brother's keeper in this world, (Applause.)</p>
        <p>Human Nature Not a Fixed Quantity.</p>
        <p>That is the answer to those Wilo tell us how bad human nature is. Human nature is bad, but we must remember that human nature is not a fixed .quantity. What we have to remora- her is that humarrnature has not always been the same; that it is pot the, same now as it was fifty years alp:s; that all human affairs progress and that man as a whole, both civilised and un- civilised, is progressive. I:luman •nature is progressive. You can improve human nature— it is not a fixed evil thing like sin. - •Things accounted goodness and virtue in one• generation we tegard as horrible vices. We had progressed since the days of the Vikings— though boys-still read with a:relish the doings of those far-off duys,-and perhaps some,would say we had not imade so much progress when theSe demands were made for £100,060,000 with the object of blowing.our 'German brethren out of ,this world, and .Germany had its, standing army of million, With like designs against us. ' That was one direction in whiCh we needed -education,. Sociology 'had begun to prepare to ' put an end, to it. We. wanted education upon , the question of brotherhood of man, and how</p>
        <p>the world was to be divided- and its interests reconciled without warfare. The same, thing applied to the industrial world._ We had trade unions and erhployerS' unions preparing ..to</p>
        <p>fight, whereas with 'proper education on the greater problems of life there ought to be no rivalry. Masters and men ought to be working together not to fill their own pockets, but for the good of humanity. The employer ought to be the father of his works.</p>
        <p>Our Idea of Hell and the Devil.</p>
        <p>However, religion, sociology and science give evidence of the progress of the world. We are becoming better than our 'grandfathers were. Macaulay said religion differed from all other sciences, because it was not ',progressive. Macaulay's fundamental error arose from the ,fact that he did not allow for the production of the inspired man, who could throw a hew light even upon religious matters, just. as a new reading could be ,given to Shakespeare.. The Hell of Milton and Dante was a real, material Hell in their day, and the painted devices, with long dagger-ending tails, were real devils up to, say, 30 or 40 years ago,. but we had pro- gressed in this matter, just- as we had progressed since the days when our grandfathers held the doctrine that a child stood in danger of eternal</p>
        <p>amnation unless it were baptised, a sacrament</p>
        <p>held to be absolutely necessary to salvation. It was only the super-added human element that disappeared from religion in the light of real knowledge.</p>
        <p>Shortcomings of This Generation.</p>
        <p>We think we are almost a perfect generation; but, continued the speaker, I wonder what three or four generations .hence will think 'about us? They will, I think, be hardlyi able to believe that a city Like Liverpool allowtd thousands of its children to die of tuberculosis that could have been saved, and that before they died those children were allowed to go to school. with others to contaminate them. They Will think the same of that and us as we think about the burning of witches and those who lived century before our time. What will they think of a city like Glasgow for allowing 100,000 of 900,000 inhabitants to live in a single room, pigging together simply because the com- munity could not develop a proper housing Scheme for its people? (Applause.) These things would not have appealed to the people of the fifteenth ov, sixteenth centuries. The only reason why hospitals arose was that people lay about the streets showing their sores and became a nuisance.</p>
        <p>llumanising the Heart.</p>
        <p>It was the humanising of the heart he wished them to take up in Warrington, and he wanted them to go to the tutorial class more with the object of learning how the industrial system had arisen. and how it could again be moved ; it would not be done*by class war. We should not get the economical aspect right until all cla6ses had been educated—AV millionaires and employers needed educating just as much as the people. at the bottom— (applause)—When the employer whose only object Would be to make the biggest dividend possible would live a miserable existence, while employes who went on to fight for the pre- dominance of the working classes would find that while they did those classes .Very little good they upset the whole social fabric in the process.</p>
        <p>H Wages Were Doubled.</p>
        <p>Referring to syndicalism, the speaker asked: "Have you ever realised what would happen if wages were doubled all. round?" It would mean, he said, that every sovereign would</p>
        <p>purchase just as much as half a sovereign did beftme, and you would be in pre6isely the same position a you were -before, Wages are only what wages will buy, and if you simply increase everybody's wages you probably leave off where you started. We want conciliatory machinei'y to stop these disputes and take the place 'of methods under which the workman starves and the manufacturer may fume, but never loses hi 4 dinner. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>Every, educated person ought to be a leaven, a centre of thought and teaching for others that would tend towards the solving of these problems. He was hopeful of the future, for they were in the presence of an ever- quickening pulse, and in the midst of conscious evolution instead of the unconscious evolution</p>
        <p>of past ages, which, handicapped by u•ncon- sciousness, still made progress. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>The Director's Plea.</p>
        <p>The • CHAIRMAN said men's opinions were - largely formed. by reading cheap literature in :the half-penny newspapers. They were not trained ,to think. Their position was one to be pitied, fol they were mostly taken away from school at the age of 13, just at the time when the brain was being awakened to real thought. He pleaded for only another year to be added to school life, and he was certain that we shouki make more rapid strides than we had done. Towns like Warrington suffered keenly from absenteeism;. the formation ofvcompanies had ended- the old relationship betwegn employer and employe, and the motor car and other facilities existed to take away to a distance those who formerly lived with and among their :people to help them to solve their problem's. ; Mr. Ransome and the School-leaving Age. Mr. RANSOME, who arrived late, said condi- tions• had changed, and it was impossible for .an employer or manager, however willing, to Jive with 1,200 workmen and ,their wives, but it was possible to take a close interest in their lives than many did. He agreed that to raise everybody's wages practically made no differ- ence. In these dayt of competition and com- panies it was difficult to get the employers and employed to see each other's point of view, but if they could get. both sides to think pretty hard and produce a satisfactory point of view, it I was more than ,possible that the - diffi- culties in the labour world woiillt not arise at I all. His firm, believing as the Director believed,</p>
        <p>, that*children should not leave school till the age of 14, decided they would not Jake boys and girls before that age. The result was that others engaged-them at 13, and none remained for them to engage at 14, although there was a Very profitable tradd awaiting them. The Adult School and the Workers'Edueation Asso- ciation were nurseries and training grounds, where people were taught to study these pro- blems', and he was glad they had had so aus- picious an opening for What -he hoped 'would be a fruitful new sessiop. , (Ap.nlause.) Mr. WALTER Twiss asked the lectury whether he thought eVolution NN:ou Id be more rapid .in the future than in the past.</p>
        <p>Have We Progressed?</p>
        <p>Ml'. • JOSEPH drawing attention to the proposition that human nature was better to- day than 50 years ago, said that on last week he saw the employers were intending to blind</p>
        <p>- - up a fund of i:0,000,000 for the purpose of crushing and destroying the Working men of . . .</p>
        <p>is country</p>
        <p>Working men also .1402 their</p>
        <p>fund for fighting the .einployer-l-eqh was de- claring war against the other., lie wondered whether human nature was not the.same except in its method of manife§taVon,</p>
        <p>Pernicious Half-time-Systern.</p>
        <p>Ndrse ALcocK asked whether jit was possible eguntgurit' fk scheme, :,of &amp;ling Lee4nical ihstratinil to children-ernplo3::ed in works dur-</p>
        <p>g business hours.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said this was possible, and such instruction would be given shortly. Children would spend part time in., school and, parr in the works, but not at the age of 12 or 13. There wopld be no more of the pernicioushalf-time system. 'Detective Parental Responsibility.</p>
        <p>Mr. WILLIAM ASHWORTH said boys and girls kept at school until they readied the seventh standard did not want to do any work at all. Could they point, out better boys and girls who had passed their standards than some of them in that, room had been who had never passed a standard at all? All they could do now.was</p>
        <p>to become clerks and go to football matches and picture shows. He said ; "Send them to work as soon as you can and make better men of them." (Laughter.) The CHAIRMAN asked, those to wlioni the lastt• speaker had -referred whether they had never regretted.. that they had not had the opportunity of passing. their standards. (Hear, hear.) The condition of things the' last speaker had described was:not due to. the .education boys and girls were receiving in school, but to neglect at bomb.. (Hear, hear.) As to the alleged need for the wages of children, he knew that the increase recently given after the carters' strike "in Warrington had' gone hot in making the homes .better, but in attending picture shows and football matches. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>Dr. MANSON proposed and ZMr. JENKINS (who said he would like to see the school-leaving age raised to 15) seconded, a vote of thanks to the lecturer, which was carried by acclamation. *, Professor MoonE said where children could not, be managed parental responsibility ought to be 'withdrawn, because it was defective. It was: a shameful injustice to send a child into the factory before its 'body was matured. It was uneconomical from the parents' point of view, because 'Or an immediate small wage the 'chance or ultimately earning mtich more as the result of fuller instruction was thrown away. Means ought to be provided for these children I to go on to the Universities. They owed h., debt not only to their own children, but to all the- other children of 'Warrington, and if the law was so defective that a good employer could not make a change, such as had been mentioned by Mr. Ilansome, the law should be 'changed. EVolutionl was going on more rapidly Allan formerly, and he believed we shuld go more rapidly still, fie hoped some noble-hearted millionaireN'yould give the .C 50,0,00,000. to which</p>
        <p>Mr. Elwell had referred. If they cbuld have the money used at once it would demonstrate how preposterous was the idei of settling diffi- culties in this way. There 'would be no more rapid way of showing the need for 'constitu- tional means of‘coneiliation than the failure of the employment of this £50,1 ii,ooo, but it would not happen—it was only net .spaper humbug. (Laughter.) • \</p>
        <p>Mr: n. A. DUNLOP, proposing a vote of thanks to the Chairman said Mr. Murray's sympathies - were with the democracy and would not be satisfied until the school-leaving age was 18, as in Germ any. Mr. ROWLAND seconded, and the vote was' carried with applause. •</p>
        <p>The CfIAIRNiAN said he would not ,say Mr. Dunion was right,. because he \vas .wrong--: (laughter); but he (the speaker) held. that if we were to have a democracy the only safe: one would be an educated democracy. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>SCHOOL MATTERS, –</head>
        <p>SCHOOL MATTERS, -</p>
        <p>The St. Helens Education Committee have deckled to close the Crown Glassworks half- timers' school, because the average attendance is now less .filan thirty. George Alfred Molyneux, one of the boys Parr Mount School, has been awarded a "Hill" Scholarship to Cowley Secondary School. A University Scholarship for students from the Technical School Of the value of £B5 a year, and tenable for three years, has been granted to John L. Greenwood.</p>
        <p>cleverness is the unpardonable sin. Perhaps that is why so many people would live to be clever. •</p>
      </div>
