      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, OCTOBER 4th, ,1913.</p>
        <p>MR. HAROLD SMITH, M.P., ON BLUFFING.</p>
        <p>Mr. HAROLD Ssura, M.P., has appeared in the constituency this week with the evident inten-</p>
        <p>Lion of converting the town to an anti-Home Rule frame of mind. Judging by the reception he had in Fairfield Ward on Thursday night, he will find .Warrington as undismayed by the partisan grievances of the Belfast zon&amp; as is the rest of the country. Early in the meeting, shrewd man that he is, he iecogrilsed the cold- ness with which his reactionary suggestions were to be met and he prefaced what he had to say by declaring that he intended to deal With a controversial subject In an uncontroversial manner, and that be would not tread upon any- one's political corns.. When Mr. Smith pro- mises to respect the corns of his opponents we may look- out for some potent' reas4d.n, and our readers ,are not so dull is to need telling what the reason was in this case: But if he did not set out to tread upon poW.lcal corns, he bade his audience shed political tears at the sight of the position into Which Sir Ellward Carson has been forced. But an unfeeling spectator cried "Bluff." Mr. Shirrs repudiated the suggestion with Indignation; but there were ane or two ether passages during the evening that savoured very much of bluff. Mr. Shunt .made great play with his claim to being a democrat, and said that all he asked before this great injustice was done to Ulster was that the question of /Home Rule should be submitted for the decision of the country. That would be a democratic suggestion if it were not the fact that Home Rule has been in the pro- gramme of the .Liberal party for three decades, and that- the Conservative party made their opposition to it one of their main cries at the last election. • Mr. HAROLD Slurs denied this, but, unfortunately for himself, he condescended to go into details in the dialogue. into which his speech at times degenerated. We quote fully what happened:—</p>
        <p>Is there any man here to-night, said Mr. SMITH, who went to vote at the last election, had the slightest idea of what the Home Rule Bill would contain? (" Yes, sir.") It is unnecessary for anyone to get angry on this. My answer to my friend who says he knew what the Home Rule Bill would contain is this: It had never been introduced in the House of Commons at that time. No one knew the details of the Bill. (A Voice: "Mr. Arthur Crosfield did.") But he never mentioned it in his election address. That man, who is in Mr. Crosfield's confidence, says he knew all about it You can read his election address from beginning to end and you will not find it. But does Mr. SMITH forget that he has already taken credit for having won Warrington on the Home Rule question? When Sir EDWARD CARSON visited Warrington, so recently as lass November, what did Mr. Small tell his audience? This: -</p>
        <p>Two years ago (he said) he was elected Member for that Borough. His opponent was one of the indiscreet members of the Radical party, who mentioned Home Rule in his address. They fought him as much on Home Rule as on any question, and they beat him, and would beat him again.</p>
        <p>Put Mr. HAROLD SMITH in November with Mr. HAROLD SMITH in the following October, and one has to rub one's eyes and ask are visions about. Or shall we call it merely bluffling? Another nice turn was to ask why the Government did riot prosecute Sir EDWARD CARSON. Mr. ARTHUR CROSFIELD had already answered that question at Belle Vue by saying that the Government will inevitably punish those responsible if any- thing serious does happen, but that in order not to make martyrs prematurely, the Govern- ment will choose their own time to strike.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>FRUIT TREES.</head>
        <p>FRUIT TREES.</p>
        <p>Fruit should be gathered as soon as ripe. The best test of its fitness for gathering is to raise it gently from the pendant position in which it hangs naturally, to a horizontal position. If it parts from the branch readily without the use of force, it is quite ripe and would soon fall.</p>
        <p>'The month of October is one of the best months for the planting of fruit trees, and pre- parations may be now, made by getting the ground in good order for planting. The ground should be trenched, about two feet deep, accord- ing to the quality of the soil and the natural depth of good soil. Do not change the position, of the soil in trenching. Let the bottom layer of soil go back to its original position.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <p>CLEARANCE SALE OF CYCLES.—AII prices reduced. --I. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge-street, War- rington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 4, 1913.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 4, 1913.</p>
        <p>THE PASSING WEEK.</p>
        <p>THE POLITICS OF THE HOME.</p>
        <p>The Women Liberals who assembled at Belle Vue in their hundreds on Monday were fortu- nate in finding Mr. Crosfield in the best of form. In a speech of sustained vigour, which took about half sri hour to deliver, the prospective Liberal candidate entertained his audience with much thoughtful matter, liberally lightened with those touches of irony and humour that are always acceptable to Warrington audiences. Naturally it was the politics of the home upon which Mr. Crosfield concentrated, and the Liberal policy has been such that he was able to do so with telling effect. Old-age pensions, insurance against times of ill-health, the legislation in favour of children in various ways are highly practical object lessons in the way our present rulers are looking after the interests with which women are pre-emi- nently concerned. Beyond insular politics, Mr. Crosfield drew attention to the efforts he is making to bring about, by an international movement, shorter working hours in certain trying trades, a point which was so appred. ated by the women present that Che reference drew forth the loudest cheer of the afternoon. Mr. Crosfleld's aims and objects for the better- ment of home life may be contrasted for effect against the speeches on Belfast blood by the Member for the -borough,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>PEARSON AND KNOWLES.</head>
        <p>PEARSON AND KNOWLES.</p>
        <p>_ At. the annual meeting of the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Company, it was dis- claimed that there was any political bearing in the cornthents in the annual report, to Nhich we drew attention last Week. - Pearson and Knowles are someone else be- sides Pearson and Knowles, as will be gathered from the speech of the Chairman at Monday's meeting. Not only did the accounts " incor- porate" the assets and liabilities of Bylands Bros., Ltd., but £250,000 of the reserve fund has been invested in the Partington Steel Company. The balance of reserve fund investments, some £BB,OOO, he said, "Is made up of sundry invest- ments in the Moss Halt Coal Co.. Ltd., shares and debentures, the Wigan Junction Colliery Co., Ltd., which has paid us a very good divi- dend this year, the Bishops Wharf Carrying Co.. Ltd., and other cognate concerns, all of which are akin to our business and are, I think, we may say, doing well."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>SMOKE ABATEMENT.</head>
        <p>SMOKE ABATEMENT.</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett is to ask the Chairman of the Health Committee at the Town Council on Tuesday night. wkat steps the committee pro- pose.to take in view of the report which recently appeared in "The Examiner" with regard ro the emission of black smoke from certain chim- neys in the town,</p>
        <p>IGNORANCE THAT IS DANGEROUS. We are glad to know that the unfortunate Warrington forward. Clark, who had his leg fractured at Wilderspool last Saturdays is pro- gressing satisfactorily at the l...rifirv.iary. The accident, which had such sad results for Clark, was a means of illustrating once again the ignorance that prevails in many quarters as to what ought to be done at a critical moment, a moment when a limb or a life hangs in the balance.' When a football player goes down and the accident is not serious, he is assisted out of the arena and the game proceeds without loss of time. Last Saturday the ambulance men who ran to Clark kept him in the position in which he fell, and to the practised eye it was clear that they ruled that the leg had been broken, and had ordered him not to be moved. From more than one part of the flekl the cries went up " Take him off" and "On with the game.* It was, we hope, not so much callous indifference to the sufferings of others as ignorance of what was due to a seriously injured fellow-being that was. at the bottom of this impatience.</p>
        <p>_ We need instruction in thesp matters, such instruction as is meted out at the Police Station Parade Room, where the Warrington Ambu- lance Brigade practise every Wednesday even- ing from now throughout the winter. The first essential aid for a person with a broken leg is that he shall be kept still until the limb has been put in splints and so made rigid. The slightest movement may force the broken, jagged bone through the flesh, and a simple fracture becomes a compound fracture. Weary weeks are thus added to the period of incapacity, with the additional risk of blood poisoning and other dangerous complications. The expert knowledge of the ambulance men on Saturday detected the fracture at once, and Clark is now going on all right,-which is ample compensation for a delay of a match for live minutes. The crude practice of letting an injured man or child stand up to prove that a leg is not broken, and the hasty bundling of an injured person into a cab, without having made proper preparations, ought to be superseded by common-sense. The treatment of fits and faints is usually .carried out in precisely the opposite way from the right way. In a faint, the head should be lowered y encourage the flow of blood back to th 9</p>
        <p>brain; in a fit the patient should be propped up to facilitate the flow of superfluous blood from the brain. In nine cases out of ten this proces is exactly reversed. In like manner a wounded man is kindly given alcoholic drink, which un- kindly sets his heart to work pumping away his life blood at a greater rate than ever. The cry of "On with the game" will have done some good if it has only emphasised the danger of a very common and very lamentable ignorance for the existence of which there is no excuse.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>TANNERS AT PEACE.</head>
        <p>TANNERS AT PEACE.</p>
        <p>An Advance In Wages of 5 to 10 per cent. and 551 Hour Week. BOTH SIDES SATISFIED.</p>
        <p>BY "A WORKER."</p>
        <p>The differences between the tanners and their masters were happily settled, after prolonged negotiations, on Monday last. The delegates agreed to recommend the settlement to the members of the Union. At the meeting of the men there were some exprepions of disappoint- ment that tlfey fiad not got more. The settle- ment was, however, finally approved. It is very difficult to express briefly the terms of the settlement, it is so much involved in detail. Broadly speaking, there is an advance from 5 to 10 per cent. in the piece work of. flesh- ing; slight advance in unhairing ; 554 hours to constitute a week's work. ,</p>
        <p>The Day Labourers. There is a fixed minimum of 64. per hour for day laboureps ; time and a quarter to be paid for overtime/. Two full weeks'notice is to be given by the Tanners' Union before withdrawing their men. Master refuse to make any distinction between union and non-union men. Masters, most of whom had cut down their quantity, agree to resume full input.</p>
        <p>A Joint Board.</p>
        <p>- This agreement still leaves the question of piece work, other than beam work, to be re- viewed, and if any alteration is required, the question is to be considered first between the masters and the men, and failing agreement, it is to go to a Joint Board, to be hereafter con- stituted, of masters and men; we presume, with fulr power to settle the question.</p>
        <p>Union Recognised. . Apart from the actual advances, the men have gained the recognition of their Union. There has also been swept away a number of petty irritating conditions, which have been in opera- tion in several of the tanyards. All yards now are, as far as possible, brought on to the same basis, and this has given satisfac- tion to the masters as well as the men. In some tanneries the actual advances mean very little; in fact, in a few isolated cases the change would imply a reduction in rates; in the other yards, however, the improvement is considerable.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>WHAT OF THAT?</head>
        <p>WHAT OF THAT?</p>
        <p>Tired! well, what of that? Did'st fancy life was spent on beds of ease. Fluttering the rose leaves scatter'd by the breeze? Come! rouse thee, work while it is call9d to-day. Coward, arise—go forth upon the way!</p>
        <p>Lonely! And what of that? Some must be lonely; 'tis not given to all, To feel a heart responsive rise and fall,— To blend another life into its own;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>Work may be done in loneliness; work on.</head>
        <p>Work may be done in loneliness; work on.</p>
        <p>Dark! Well, and what of that? Did'st fondly dream the sun would never set? Dost fear to lose thy way? Take courage yet; Learn thou to walk by faith and not by sight; Thy steps will guided be, and guided right.</p>
        <p>Hard! Well, and what of that? Did'st fancy life one summer holiday, With lessons none to learn and naught but play? Go, get thee to thy task; conquer or diet It must be learned—learn it then patiently.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>From Everywhere.</head>
        <p>From Everywhere.</p>
        <p>When,a man ceases to be a single man he, ipso facto, begins LO lead a double life..</p>
        <p>Nothing reveals the character of a man so much as the way in which he behaves toward fools.</p>
        <p>A man expects a woman to remember 170,\‘' many lumps of sugar he takes in his tea, but to forget the promises that he Once made to her.</p>
        <p>A man may sidetrack a lot of temptation by getting himself locked up in gaol-</p>
        <p>Helping Him Out " You know, there was something I wanted to say to you, but it has quite gone out of my mind. I can't remember what it was."</p>
        <p>Maiden (hopefully): "It wasn't `good-night,' was it?"</p>
        <p>Hint for Warringtoo Shoppers. Prominent women of Yonkers, N.Y-, have banded together to try to get better prices and to have more sanitary ,stores and shops. To help tradesmen, they have pledged to ask but one delivery a day, to pay cash and to give pre- ference to early closing stores. The merchants are said to be much in sympathy with the move- ment.</p>
        <p>Oriental Sayings. Don't ask any man about his origin; you can read in his face. If you censure your friend for every fault he commits there will come a time when you will have no friend to censure. If you spend your ;inieloptlecting money for fear of poverty, you are fpraaising poverty. Man is .often an eneray 'to 'things of which he Is ill-informed. Knowledge without practice is like a bow without a string. The next best thing to belief in God is to sym- pathise with people. ""</p>
        <p>Rather Bright, After All. In an English village lived a dull-witted boy, who yet was shrewder than was generally sup- posed. It was a great local joke to offer him the choice of a penny and a threepenny piece. The boy invariably chose the penny. A stran- ger, noticing this, and sympathising with the lad, one day asked him: "Why do you always-take the penny? Is it because the coin is larger?" " Naw," came the quick response, delivered with an air of impish mischief, "not because it is the biggest. Because if I took the threepenny bit, they'd soon stop offering it to me, d'ye see?"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>OUR COMMON THINGS.</head>
        <p>OUR COMMON THINGS.</p>
        <p>All e,ommon things, each day's events, That with the hour begin and end, Our pleasures and our discontents, The sorrows which our ways attend.</p>
        <p>Our menial duties, cares and fear, That to us each day strives to lend; Things that seem paltry to appear, Are rounds by which we may ascend.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>GAS EXPERTS AT WARRINGTON</head>
        <p>GAS EXPERTS AT WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>Visit to the Richmond Gas Stove Works.</p>
        <p>The members of thf Yorkshire Junior Gas Association and a few friends, to the number of 80, visited the works of the Richmond Gas Stove Company on Saturday. The visitors in- cluded Mr. Edward Allen, engineer, Liverpool Gas Company, this year president of the Insti- tution of Gas Engineers, and also of the Brit- ish Commercial Gas Association; Mr. S. Glover, St. Helens; Mr. T. Banbury Ball, Rochdale; Professor Harold Dixon, Manchester, who is this year president of the Society of British Gas In- dustries; Prof. J. H. Cobb, Leeds; Dr. Dawson, Leeds University; Councillor William Kay, chairman Manchester Gas Committee; and Mr. Price, superintendent Manchester Gas Depart- ment, and others. Mr. H. M. Thornton, vice-chairman, in the absence of the Chairman, welcomed the com- pany to Warrington. There were also present Mr. John A.. Ransome, managing director; Dr. J. F. Atkins, director, and Mr. James W. Slack, director and secretary.</p>
        <p>*Mr. Thornton stated that he thought their distinguished visitors would see before tney had completed their tour of the works That the com- pany was in a position to supply the industry with the apparatus it required, from a small bunsen burner to the largest furnace it was possible to use with coal gas.</p>
        <p>Mr. Allen thought that in the gas industry there was still plenty of room at the top, and 'though the juniors might at present be said to be some distance from the top, yet it was pos- sible by industry and perseverance and careful attention to their daily duties to secure the 'best positions in the industry. The meeting was also addressed by Professor Dixon, who expressed his pleasure at finding one or two of his old students actively engaged In the research department of the Richmond Gas Stove—arid Meter to., Ltd. Councillor Kay, Mr. Samuel Glover, Mr. Ball, and Dr. Atkins also spoke. Mr. Ransome then invited the company to make a tour of Cie works, and the party pro- ceeded to inspect the various departments. Having finished tne inspection-of the works, the party made for the Patten Arms Hotel, where they were entertained to a substantial tea by the directors of the company. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>MAYOR FOR 1913-14.</head>
        <p>THE MAYOR FOR 1913-14.</p>
        <p>Councillor Peter Peacock Selected Without</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>Opposition.</head>
        <p>Opposition.</p>
        <p>CONGRATULATIONS FROM FAR AND NEAR.</p>
        <p>The Mayor of the Borough for the ensuing year was selected at a special meeting Of the Town Council on Monday afternoon. It will be remembered that some time ago it was decided to approach several gentlemen, who, it was thought, might accept the honour, Councillor Peter Peacock being placed first on the list. For various reasons, Alderman Bennett, Alderman John Evans and Alderman James Evans did not wish to accept the Mayor- alty at present, and Councillor Flory Roberts intimated that he would not care to be one of a number of candidates. Councillor Peacock .having intimated his willingness to accept office, he became the only candidate in the fold. On the motion of Alderman TiNsioN,.seconded, by Alderman BURTON, Councillor. Peacock's selection was agreed to without opposition. Councillor PEACOCK, in thanking the Council for the honour they had decided to eonfer upon him; said, ardent politician as they knew him to be, he would sink his personal views and endeavour to act with impartiality. He would see justice done to all sections of the Council,</p>
        <p>mittee we were directed to send to you our heartiest congratulations upon your selection as Mayor of the Borough, and to convey to you our sincerest and best wishes that your term of office may be one of the best in the history of Warrington. Knowing you as we do from personal acquaintance, we are perfectly sure that Warrington will have a Mayor who will worthily uphold its best traditions in'the carry- ing out of the many and various duties apper- taining to that high office." This was signed by Mi. Clegg as chairnian and other members of the committee. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>WHITECROSS.</head>
        <p>WHITECROSS.</p>
        <p>At the monthly meeting of the Whitecross Ward Liberal Committee on Wednesday even- ing, Mr. Dutton made the following reference: I have been asked, on behalf of the Ward Committee, to congratulate Councillor Peacock, one of the representatives of our ward,, on his becoming Mayor for the coming year. Knowing him as I do, I am sure that Warrington will</p>
      </div>
