      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>MAYOR AND MAYORESS-ELECT.</head>
        <p>THE MAYOR AND MAYORESS-ELECT.</p>
        <p>Councillor and N</p>
        <p>and hoped to have gained their approbation when he came to the end of his term of office:</p>
        <p>ALDERMAN BENNETT AND MAYORAL SELECTIONS. PARTY ROTATION SUGGESTION.</p>
        <p>We understand that, Alderman Bennett wrote to the Town Clerk from Dexthorpe, on the 6th September, to the effect that, he would be in Lincolnshire on _ the day the deputation had arranged to meet him, and that he did not want to put them to the trouble of making a special appointment, as, although he was /entirely opposed to the introduction of politics in tha Council, he was of opinion that the Liberals might fairly claim the position on the present occasion. He had often thought that, in order to avoid any question of this kind in the future, it would be a good thing to offer the Mayoralty to each party in rotation. "Furthermore," he added. I have such a high sense of the dignity of the office that I should not feel justified in seriously considering the matter, except in response to the obvious wishes of the Council as a wholes"</p>
        <p>[rs, Peter Peacock.</p>
        <p>have a Mayor who will worthily uphold its best traditions, in the carrying out of the duties appertaining to such a high and iinportant office. lam sure he will carry it out with dignity to himself, the Corporation, and to the chair. We also trust that God will give both Councillor and Mrs. Peacock health and strength to carry out the duties of the office. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>He moved that in the form of a resolution. Mr. T. H. Nock seconded. and Mr. Thomas Dale supported. The resolution was carried unani- mously. Councillor Peacock expressed his sincere thanks for the genial manner in which the reso- lution had been .proposed and adopted., He would try his best to live up to the expecta- tions they had formed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>CONGRATULATIONS FRONI ALL QUARTERS.</head>
        <p>CONGRATULATIONS FRONI ALL QUARTERS.</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR PEACOCK'S MANY-SIDED</p>
        <p>QUALITIES.</p>
        <p>As will be seen from our brief review of the career of Councillor Peacock, the Mayor-elect has many and varied interests in life, and his popularity has been reflected by the number of congratulations that have reached him upon the attainment of this high office. These con- gratulations have flowed in from the various Courts of the Foresters and many other friendly societies and similar organisations with which he is associated. The managers of several railway companies have addressed him con- gratulatory letters, and a cable came all the way from Chicago, from ex-Councillor Owen Toole, as follows :</p>
        <p>Heartiest Congratulations from Toole.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>MR. CROSFIELD AND THE SELECTION. " OUIt GOOD OLD FRIEND."</head>
        <p>MR. CROSFIELD AND THE SELECTION. " OUIt GOOD OLD FRIEND."</p>
        <p>Mr. Crosfield, While speaking to the Women Liberals at Belle Vue on Monday, was handed a telegram by Mr. E. A: Sanders.</p>
        <p>"I have just received," said Mr. Crosfield, "a piece of good news, which I make known to you with great joy. (A Voice: "Peacock's Mayor.") Yes! (Loud and continued cheering.) Yes, our valued and indefatigable worker and our very able member of the Liberal party in Warring- ton, a friend whom we all respect and whose works we all deeply admire, our ..good, old friend, Mr. Peacock, is chosen to be Mayor." (Renewed cheering.) •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>WOMEN LIBERALS' MESSAGE.</head>
        <p>WOMEN LIBERALS' MESSAGE.</p>
        <p>On the proposition of Mrs. Crosfleld, and amid much enthusiasm, it was decided to send the following telegram to Councillor Peacock in Hie name of the Association : Women's Liberal Association Send Heartiest Congratulations.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>MR CROSFIELD'S PERSONAL LETTER.</head>
        <p>MR CROSFIELD'S PERSONAL LETTER.</p>
        <p>On returning to Warrington, Mr. Crosficicl, writing from his home in Bold-street, sent the Mayor-elect the followingtletter, dated Septem- ber 29:</p>
        <p>' "Dear Mr. Peacock,—Just a few lines to let you know that the joyful news announced In the telegram, when I read it out to our great gather- ing of Warrington Women Liberals at Belle Vue, was received with the utmost enthusiasm.</p>
        <p>"On behalf of Mrs. Crosfield and iny sister, as well as on my own account, I send you warmest, heartiest, most sincere congratula- tions.</p>
        <p>"I remain, "Ever faithfully yours, "ARTHUR H. CROSFIELD."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>FROM THE WARDS. HOWLEY.</head>
        <p>FROM THE WARDS. HOWLEY.</p>
        <p>At the monthly meeting of the Howley Ward Liberal Comniittee on Tuesday evening, Mr. William Clegg proposed, and Mr. W. Vose seconded, the following resolution, which was adopted unanimously : "At a meeting of the Howley Ward Com.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>BEWSEY.</head>
        <p>BEWSEY.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Bewsey Ward Liberal Executive Committee on Thursday evening, a vote of congratulation to Councillor Peacock, on his attaining to the high office of Mayor of the borough, was moved by Councilor Gough, seconded by Mr. C. E. W. Nurse, and supported by Councillors Wilkinson, Skelland and Dicken- son. ,</p>
        <p>The resolution was carrieiTunanimously, and Councillor Peacock suably replied.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>TOWN HALL.</head>
        <p>TOWN HALL.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Town Hall *ard Liberal Committee last evening, Mr. H. Halford moved a resolution congratulating Councillor Peacock on his selection as Mayor.</p>
        <p>- - ln hini, he said, we shall have an ideal Mayor. Our sympathies are with him in any loss he may sustain to enable him to be our, Chief Magistrate, but I am sure he is strong enough to stand anything in that direction.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. Colley seconded, Councillor Lindley supported, anu the resolution was carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>MAYOR-ELECT.</head>
        <p>THE MAYOR-ELECT.</p>
        <p>A STRENUOUS LIFE AND AN INTERESTING CAREER.</p>
        <p>Councillor Peter Peacock is, as he informed the Council on Monday, an ardent politician— on the Liberal side„ of course. At the present moment he is chtainan of the Executive Com- mittee of the Liberal-Association, to which office lie succeeded Councillor Wilkinson, in March, 1910. Prior to that he had. been deputy-chair- man for several years. Ile has been very active in ward politics. After having been secretary of the Orford Ward for eight years, and vice-chairman of the Orford and Bewsey Wards for seven years, he became chairman of the St. John's Ward four years ago. He has served on the Finance and Executive Commit- tees and General Council of the Liberal Asso- ciation for over twelve years. He has given great encouragement to the development of the game of bowls among the various Ward Com- mittees, and the " Peacock " Cup has done much to keep interest alive through the summer months.</p>
        <p>Mr. Peacock was, born in Silsoe, Bedford- shire, in 1870, but his father, Jesse, who, by the way, was a strong Conservative,Vas transferred to Warrington by the Midland Railway Co., by whom he was employed, when the future Mayor was but a year old. As Master Peter grew up he was educated at Wycliffe School. When fourteen years of age he entered the service of the Cheshire Lines Committee and remained with them, at Warrington and Liver- pool, for ten year. He then became the-first sub-agent for the Great Northern Railway Co. at Warrington. Two years later he entered into private business as a coal merchant at Warrington , and Lancaster, and as a stone- mason and contractor at Warrington. He also established an. accounts checking agency. Ulti- mately he gave up these businesses and for the past fourteen years he has been traffic manager to the firm of Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Ltd., soap and chemiCalmanufacturers, a position in which he has, by general consent, displayed conspicuous ability. • Councillor Peacock is particularly well and favourably known for his friendly society work and charitable interests. • He has been a .mem- ber of the Ancient Order of 'Foresters, the Ancient Order of Shepherds, and the Hearts of Oak Friendly Societies since he was sixteen.</p>
        <p>Ile has served in all the principal offices, and is at present district treasurer of the Ancient</p>
        <p>(Continued in next column.)</p>
        <p>"THE EXAMINEWI</p>
        <p>Mr. Crosfieid's Advice to Warrington People.</p>
        <p>"IN THE FRONT RANK.'</p>
        <p>Mr. A. 11. Crosfleld, addressing the rnernriplies of the Women's Liberal Association at Vue on Monday, paid a glowing tribute to 1" Examiner." aclid "I am," he said, "delighted with the progress your organisation has made. vt me to make one suggestion. Advise all bel°,"lo ing to the working classes in N.N.'arrington, wish to support their own cause, to back ".1`; their own folk, to stand out for their interests, to get the best articles from a vinn!ei-oi and children's point of view, from all Pas. view, who wish to get week by week a ne7r4 paper which is for graphic, varied, interelrti AI and reliable news, sound, enlightened opinion, courage, initiative and. resource in: very front rank of all local journals in the k1117,"0 dom—advise all these people, l' say, to sticl` the "Warrington Examiner."</p>
      </div>
