      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <p>. •</p>
        <p>Stone-laying Ceremony.</p>
        <p>Mrs. W. T. Williams, of Warrington, laid the foundation-stone of a new Welsh Calvinistic Methodist (Presbyterian) Chapel, Daisy Bank- road, Victoria Park, Manchester, on Saturday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>Anti-Vivisectionists.</head>
        <p>Anti-Vivisectionists.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Anti-Vivisection Society, held in the Cocoa Rooms, Horsemarket:street, to discuss the future policy of the Warrington Society, there was only a moderate attendance, and the meeting was postponed for a month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>Death-rate.</head>
        <p>The Death-rate.</p>
        <p>There were 27 deaths in Warrington last week, 14 males and p females, giving a death-rate of 18.9, as against 18 deaths (10 males and 8 females) giving a rate of 12.7 for the corresponding week in 1912. There were nine deaths from diarrhea.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>Local Birth-rate.</head>
        <p>Local Birth-rate.</p>
        <p>In Warrington last week there were 39 births (12 males and 27 females), giving a birth-rate of 27.4. For the corresponding week last year the births were 33 (17 males and 16 females), with a birth-rate of 24.1.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>Warrington Market Prices.</head>
        <p>Warrington Market Prices.</p>
        <p>The Warrington market prices on Wednesday were: Wheat, 4s. qd. to 4s. 6d. per 701 b.; oats, 2s. sd. to 2s. 64d. per 451 b.; wheat straw, 255. per ton; oat straw, 255. to 26s 6d. per ton; XX flour, 235. 9d. per 2401 h.; bran, Is. id. per 201 b.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. IL Crosfield and Billiard Players.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. H. Crosfield has presented for com- petition amongst the members of the Warring- ton and District Billiard League a handsome challenge shield. There are now 21 clubs in the two divisions of the League.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>Tradesmen's Complaints Removed.</head>
        <p>Tradesmen's Complaints Removed.</p>
        <p>The Warrington tradesmen sent a complaint to the Runcorn District Council that the un- named roads in Grappenhall and Latchford Without caused them inconvenience. The Clerk has replied that the roads will be named at once.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>Licensed Trade Conference.</head>
        <p>Licensed Trade Conference.</p>
        <p>At the conference of the Northern District League of Beer, Wine and Spirit Trades Defence Associations, held at Farnworth, Bolton, on Thursday, the Warrington Association was represented by Messrs, W. C.. Brooks (chairman) and W. J. Shaw tsecretary).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>Death of the Rev. Thomas Rfppon.</head>
        <p>Death of the Rev. Thomas Rfppon.</p>
        <p>The death occurred on. Thursday, at-Hoylake, of the Rev. Thomas Rippon, who laboured as a Wesleyan pastor for some years in Warrington. He was an energetic minister and an eloquent preacher, and his sojourn in Warrington is still remembered by many of our local Wesleyan&amp;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>Crosfield's Band Fourth.</head>
        <p>Crosfield's Band Fourth.</p>
        <p>Crosfield's Soap Works Band was awarded fourth place in the National brass band contest at the Crystal Palace on Saturday. For the third time Irwell Springs Band have secured the title of Champions of Great Britain and the Colonies, along with the thousand guinea trophy. St. Hilda's Colliery were second, and Black Dyke third. •</p>
        <p>Band of Hope Jubilee. There was a great day on Saturday In Band of Hope circles, for the jubilee of the Lancashire and Cheshire Band of Hope and Temperance Union, which event was celebrated at the Exhi- bition Hall. Rusholme, Manchester. Amongst others who attended from the Warrington Band of Hope Union were Mrs. James Dodds, the president; Mr. Thomas Dale, chairman of the Executive Committee; Mr. Frederick Gandy, honorary secretary; and Mr. Ralph Fellows, ?.ssistant secretary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>Dolls Aid a Memorial.</head>
        <p>Dolls Aid a Memorial.</p>
        <p>r In order to aid in raising a fund to perpetu- ate the memory of the late Mr. Walter McLaren, ' M.P. the Warrington Women's Liberal Associa- tion held a draw for dolls last evening. The winners were: Miss Hilson, Ellesmere-street; Mrs. Hardman, 19, Villar-street ; Miss Vera Wil- lett, 5, Hume-street; Mrs. Hinds, 451, Wellfield- street ; Mrs. Catterall, 5, Froghall-lane ; Mrs. Marlh, 2, Bean-street. Mrs. Clarke, Orford, on the doll specially given for that ward by Mrs. P. H. Brown. ,</p>
        <p>Appointment for Mr. R. B. Roberts. Mr. R. B. Roberts, who was appointed Assistant Director of Education by the Warrington Edu- cation Committee in 1903, has been appointed to a local secretaryship at Woking by the Surrey Education Committee. Mr. Roberts was edu- cated at the Liverpool Institute and prior to taking up his duties at Warrington was for 11 years in the employ of the Liverpool School Board, where he •went through the various departments. He is a native of Liverpool, and will commence his secretaryship at Woking early in November.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>Cult of the Chess Board.</head>
        <p>Cult of the Chess Board.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Warrington Chess Club was held at Prince's Cafe on Thursday, Mr. J. Newcombe presiding. Mr. F. Dunne • was elected president and captain, and Mr. J. New- combe vice-president. It was resolved that, in addition to running a competition for the club's silver trophy, a second-class competition should be held for players who are not strong enough to compete for the trophy. A handicap competi- tion will also be arranged. A number of new members joined the club.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>Co-operative Conference.</head>
        <p>A Co-operative Conference.</p>
        <p>A conference of Co-operative Committees and employes was held in Warrington on Wednesday on Wages Boards.—Mr. L. Lumby (senior organ- iser of the Amalgamated Union of -Co-operative Employes) said Wages Boards had already been formed in connection with some societies, but many of them were not Wages Boards at all, but simply committees on which employes had no representation whatever. To his mind, what- ever the so-called Wages Boards might decide, they would be ignored altogether if they wasted time attempting to modify the present minimum rates laid down by the union ill any particular whatsoever.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>Death of the Rev. E. Lloyd Jones.</head>
        <p>Death of the Rev. E. Lloyd Jones.</p>
        <p>The Rev. E. Lloyd Jones, a Wesleyan minister, who was stationed at Warringto'n some years ago, died in Manchester on Saturday. Mr. Lloyd Jones was a native of North Wales, and in his Sunday School days at Cefn Mawr his genius began to show itself.. A discussion had been opened in the class on the subject of the happi- ness of the departed. Young Lloyd Jones took part, and his views made such a striking impres- sion that the discussion was continued for 13 Sundays, and ultimately all had come round to his opinion. Of course, he soon began to preach, but when he was proposed as a candidate for the ministry he was rejected by the quarterly meet- ing as unorthodox, and returned to his draper's counter. Next year, however, the quarterlymeeting changed its views, and passed him un- animously. It was Mr. Jones who created such a sensation amongst the Bewsey-road congrega- tion by solemnly announcing the occurrence of the terrible murders in the Phcenix Park, Dub- lin, in the early "eighties." He was a skilled lecturer and his favourite theme wae "The Progrese,l4</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>Co-operative Exhibition.</head>
        <p>Co-operative Exhibition.</p>
        <p>An interesting exhibition of Co-operative pro- ductions was opened in the Empire Skating Rink on Saturday. At the opening ceremony Mr. J. Jarman, the secretary of the local branch, pre- sided. He had to apologise for the absence-of the Mayor, for the exhibition was not an ordin- ary trade exhibition, and on that account they thought they might have welcomed the Mayor that afternoon. There was a Proposal at the last quarterly meeting that the Warrington Society's Committee should consider the estab- lishment of a new productive department. With- out criticising that proposition, he would like to ask if the members were at present support- ing to the utmost extent the productive depart- ments which the society already possessed? They were anxious to improve the lot of the workers. By helping forward the co-operative movement they would be doing more than in any other way to uplift the condition of the workers of the town and Country.--A vote of thanks to Mr. Gregory for opening the exhibition was prol. posed by Ir. A. Mann (secretary of the Labour 'Ci3-partnership Association), who presented Mr. Gregory with a beautiful copy of "Industrial to-operation," by Miss Catherine Webb.—To-day there will be a grand choir contest at three o'clock in the Parr ,Hall, with; an evening con- cert at 6.30. The exhibition will be crowded to-day, for we understand that a lajge number are coming over from St. Helens and elsewhere.</p>
        <p>PEVERIL FIRE GRATES never fail to please.— Sole agent, J. S. GARLAND, Ironmonger, 51 to 55, Bridge-street, Warrington. N. },•, • 4 , '</p>
        <p>In studying the Medical Officer's report, sani- tary section', for the last seven years, we are impressed with the inadequacy of the reports on the important subject of smoke control. The smoke section, as compiled, viiries from year to year in its formation and terms. This makes it impossible to form any estimate of progress in smoke abatement, or to put together in tabular form anything comparable with the information supplied in other towns. Here is a specimen of the casual way in which the' Health .Committee have dealt with the serious nuisance SMOKE INSPECTION. . .</p>
        <p>inTchaisretsywonbesreervnaf ft ei onne as she e been been teaekn le inn , d tatne d the offenders have been informed, and in eachatt e attention. as the they have promised to give the m This and nothing more. The offenders have left out. -How different from other towns. We tphr eoym ias be ad t e dt o th givee the m nuisance?att e een tvi ot vital al butpo inhta vie s have before us the health report of such towns as Manchester, Glasgow, Leicester, Liverpool, Bolton, Sheffield, and others, in which pages of interesting details are given. Of course, in a town where practically nothing is done, it can- not be expected that a sanitary inspector can have much to say; nothing can be produced from nothing. It is true, in other years there appears to be some evidence of record-keeping. In the years 1908, 1909 and 1910 we have respec- tively 11, 16, and 10 offences, in which warning was given, and the addition of the fact that on second observations, improvement had been effected, with a few details. Any stranger reading these brief, and, as far as they go, satisfactory, reports, would come to the con- clusion that the Health Committee had simply to Warn the manufacturers, and forthwith an improvement would be effected, and no more offences committed. He would rise from the study of these reports with the idea that as far as smoke was concerned, Warrington was an Ideal place, with an ideal Health Committee; that manufacturers were ready, nay, eager, to heed the warning and amend tile faults. If this same stranger, having arrived at this conclusion, were to walk about Warrington to admire these ideal conditions, he would be as- tounded to find the facts in no way accorded with the impressions conveyed by the Tibalth reports. He might come across the .report recently published of a Competent and inde- pendent inspector from Manchester, but this would be scarcely necessary if he had a pair of eyes and a six-shilling Waterbury welch, and he would wonder whefrier the report he had read belongea to Warrington. He might conclude he had got hold of the wrong reports, got into the wrong town, got mixed up some- how.</p>
        <p>In the 1907 report we find "19 cases where black smoke was emitted from the chimneys for more than three minutes, out of thirty, and were reported to the committee, who instructed me [the sanitary inspector] to obtain informa- tion as to the time limit in force in other towns, and to take a series of observations from chim- neys in our own town with a view to ascertain- ing whether' the time limit in force in this borough is a reasonable one?' Note one factor to be used GO determine whether the time limit was reasonable was observations of Warring- ton chimneys; in other words, the manufac- turer? hahltS were to help tO determine the time limit.</p>
        <p>THE TIME LIMITS. In 1908 the required information appears to have been obtained, and we have the foliow- ing:— •</p>
        <p>This question has received special atten- tion during the past year, and after making inquiries as to the time limits enforced in .'other manufacturing towns; the Health, )Committee decided to fix the following as the standard, viz., that three consecutive minutes, or a total of five minutes in a thirty minutes' observation, be allowed for the emission of black smoke. • It would be interesting to know why the Health Committee altered their rule from three minutes, which was about an average of other towns, to five minutes, which was practically the extreme limit in use. Probably it was the second consideration, the manufacturers' habits, more than the first which afforded reason for the unusual extension. At this time Bolton reduced its limit from two and a half to two minutes, Manchester stood at two minutes, Salford three, Oldham two. But, letting this pass, the Health Committee should tell us why, after fixing the five minutes' limit, they have not enforced it? There is not a week, not a day, scarcely an hour of the day, that the by-law is not broken. _ _</p>
        <p>11.1 1911 we have the information given us that in suitable weather two inspectors are em- ployed two half-days a week in taking smoke observations. This is equivalent to one whole day per week for one inspector, or fifty-two full days in the year. In the same year this fact is given the number of offences is recorded as ten for the year, working under the extended time limit. Now here is a point of some seriousness. From private observations taken and confirmed by the Manchester Inspector's report, the num- ber of offences at the present time is more like ten a day than ten a year. There are three possibilities open.</p>
        <p>Ist. That the appointed inspectors have SO neglected their duties as to discover or to report only the ten offences in the year. 2nd. That there has been such a marvellous change for the worse in the emission of exces- sive smoke in the two years that the number of observed and reported offences in 1911 was correct, viz., 11.</p>
        <p>3rd. That the Health Committee in their report has for some unknown reason sup- pressed the reports sent in as a whole, and only reported 11 to the public. •</p>
        <p>WILL THE HEALTH COMMITTEE EXPLAIN?</p>
        <p>The first supposition is so unlikely as to be almost unthinkable. There is nothing to suggest that our local sanitary inspectors are not men of diligence and integrity, and to suppdss evidence of their activity would be against their own interest. At the same time, as a general rule, if it is known that a committee is indifferent about' any department of their work this fact is liable to create a slackness. If, for example, in this case reports are ignored the officials become disheartened and their work becomes Rerfunctory,, But the blame is at the door of the committee.</p>
        <p>The second possibility iS equally untenable because while the smoke conditions are cer- tainly not improving there is no evidence of any marked change for the worse during the last two years.</p>
        <p>We are thus left with the third alternative. We do not at present urge the serious inference which this suggests. We desire to keep an open mind to treat the Health Committee with all fairness, but an explanation is called for from the committee Which WiWmake plain how, in recent years, with two inspectors each taking observations a half-day a week, they, have reported according to the average yearly record under one offence per month: We propose to let the matter rest for a week or two, to allow the committee opportunity to offer any defence they can make. If no ex- planation is forthcoming, The public, whose interests they are elected to protect, will draw the natural conclusion, and we will then return to the subject. •</p>
        <p>Our sole (Med is to effect an improvement,</p>
        <p>to prevent unnecessary smoke, not to discredit the Health Committee, but it is impossible— at any rate undesirable, unless other means fail—to effect this improvement except through the responsible parties, and this can only be done by showing how, through ignorance, or some other cause, the Health Committee have in the phst failed in their duty as guardians of .the 'public rights. •</p>
        <p>Remarkable success attended the demonstration and picnic of the Warrington Women's Liberal Association, held at the famous Belle Vue Gardens, Manchester, on Monday. The party numbered ever 600, and a merry throng they were. In the Gardens they found much to interest and entertain them for several hours. Speechifying occupied a_ short time after tea, In the spacious tea-room, and deeply interest- ing addresses were given by Mr. and Mrs. A. H. Crisfield. The prospective Liberal candidate for Warring- ton made merry over the wooden guns, and ,the "wooden heads," that, he said, were behind the wooden guns in the threatened war in Ulster. Later the company witnessed the mimic battle, an incident in the Turco-Bulgarian War. In this wooden guns and wooden off icers.played a conspicuous,' part ' •</p>
        <p>. It was "Warrington Day" at the Belle Vue Gardens, Mailehestef.-,*-',Monday. • Warring-. ton folk were to be. inet Willi at every tiirriing. The Women's Liberal ASSOciation were there for their annual pievic and demonstration, and a merry company they -provided: - Even the animals in the 4.0 were reported to have en- tered into the joys of the'dliy's outing, and, as a waggish keeper put it, they became "wire- pullers" for the day. The company travelled from Warrington in two special trains, which made expeditious journeys, and they numbered upwards-of 600. At the Gardens, the early .part of the afternoon was devoted to sight-seeing. Later on the com- pany assembled for tea in the spacious tea- room, and after tea there', were two brief speeches. Mrs. A. H. Crosfield presided in her capacity of president. She was supported by Mr. A. H. Crosfield, Miss Truda Crosfield, the chairman of the executive; Mrs. Osborne, the hon. secre- tary, and other office-bearers of this thriving Association.</p>
        <p>When Mr. and Mrs. Crosfield entered the hall, the ladies rose en masse and gave the worthy couple a most enthusiastic welcome.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
