      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>WOMEN LIBERAL STALWARTS,</head>
        <p>WOMEN LIBERAL STALWARTS,</p>
        <p>Left to right—Back row: Mr. Weston, Mrs. Weston, Mrs. PerCival, Mrs. Sanders, Mrs. Bunch, Mr. Sanders, Miss Booth, Mrs. Halliwell, Mrs. Pendlebury, Miss Wilkinson, Mrs. T. Locker, Mrs. 3. Booth. Front row: Mrs- Hassall, Mrs. Hawthorn, Miss , Le Thicke, Mrs. Dutton, Miss Crosfield, Mrs.. A.H. u.osfleld, Mr. A. 11. C.rosfield, Mrs. Osborne. Mrs. J. T. Locker, Mrs. Shaw, Mrs. Lindley.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>PRESIDENT'S WELCOME.</head>
        <p>THE PRESIDENT'S WELCOME.</p>
        <p>THE TORY ULSTER TRICK.</p>
        <p>llrs. CROSFIELD was enthusiastically cheered when she rose to open the meeting. She said they were to be congratulated on the ideal weather they were having, sbut the Clerk of the weather had evidently heard that the Women's Liberal Association were holding their annual picnic, and he wished to give them a proof of his liberality,. (Hear, hear.) She had jUst re- turned from ao•trip to Germany, which was a wonderful counlry in Some respects, but she was glad to get back to the beauty of old England and to its evidences of comparative prosperity. It was also such a ,relief to get English food and English bread and English meat again. This prosperity in England was largely owing to Free Trade, and to social reform which had been introduced since the Liberal Government came into power in 1906. (A Voice: "And cheers for Lloyd George.") And what did the Tories say about social ,reform? It threw a light on their character. They said it had been brought in with a rush. Thousands of men, women and children succumbed to illness which could be cured and prevented, they toiled in Misery and squalor, and many lived in daily dread of that most terrible of all evils, a pauper's grave. And yet when the Liberal Government , brought in measures to alleviate this suffering, the Tories complained that they have been rushed. Was it not a proof of their utter sel- fishness? Thank God social reform had come with a rush. What about the Ulster farce? Why were the Tories insisting on stirring up strife amongst the Irish? Simply because they thought this was the easiest manner of ousting the Liberal Government and getting themselves into office. They had neither Sympathy nor in- terest in the Ulster people,- and were simply making them dupes. It reminded her of the fable of the old tabby cat and the mice. The two mice quarrelled as to which had the more ancient right to a certain . comfortable hole, and it was decided to refer the dispute to an old tabby cat, which was said to have wis- dom, and to be the judge of the neighbourhood. They went to the cat, but tabby.pretended she could neither see nor hear, and asked the two mice to ,come nearer and nearer. When they did so she suddenly grabbed them one in each paW, and they were not heard of again. That was exactly what the Tory party were trying to do in Ulster. They were using Ulster for , their own selfish ends. (Cheers.) rsuppose all of you saw the excerpt from two speeches in "The Examiner " ? One excerpt was from the speech of a certain Tory lady you all know---(laughter)---and the other excerpt was from an admirable speech by Mrs. Osborne. (Lo_nd applause.) Her you know still 'better. (Applause.) The excerptswere printed side by side in "The Examiner," withobt any comment, and I do not think any comment was needed. His always the strongest party that can afford to be magnanimous, and dignified, and that is the attitude we must maintain if. we Wish our</p>
        <p>Association to continue popular and peaceful (Loud applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>MR. ,CROSFIELD'S SPEECE.</head>
        <p>MR. ,CROSFIELD'S SPEECE.</p>
        <p>FREE TRADE A WOMAN'S QUESTION.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. CROSFIELD., who met with an enthusiastic ovation, expressed his thanks for the oppor- tunity of addressing the members of the Association, who had made his wife president, and his sister chairman of the executive. He also congratulated them upon having such a band of leaders in the executive of the Associa- tion and such an excellent and indefatigable secretary as Mrs. Osborne. (Loud applause.) I have said more than once, he added, if it was only in defence of Free Trade and in for- bidding a few wealthy -people, who by Protec- tion, would be receiving an artificial and un- fairly enhanced *price at the 'expense of the , whole mass of our people, from. in that way ex- ploiting the nation, the existence of the Women's Liberal Association is abundantly justified. We have again and 'again riddled the argument of Protection, which the Tories have tried to weave round us. 'Why the *hold lambi* of 'Profile- ' Lion is so transparent that it has driven men out cif public life, and it has broken up dose family relations.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>" WAS SO SCANDALOUS."</head>
        <p>" WAS SO SCANDALOUS."</p>
        <p>This preposterous proposal to foist Protec- tion on our people was so 3eandalous that In the whole' of Mr. palfour's Cabinet there was not one statesman of real weight, standing and responsibility, who did not find it more than he could swallow. These illustrious statesmen were driven out of the Cabinet, and even Mr. Balfour never really had his heart in the Bir-</p>
        <p>ment, and so is the Insurance Act, What to say about the people who tried to defeat and ruin these great measures, I know not. The less said the better about those ghoulish and selfish efforts made to impede and discourage the men working out these great measures. In addressing a gathering of Liberal women in Warrington, some years ago, I remember I said tiled we glorified in our splendid positive policy, and every year that has passed you have seen, that policy expand arid you, have seen practical results from it. I do say that now you have entered the period of constructive work in polities, social reform comes home to you all as politics never came home before. Why does it come home especially to an association like yours?, It is for this simple reason. Social reform is neither more nor less than the politics of the home. (Cheers.) But, friends, happily the men who have this work in hand were not to be impeded or deterred from going through with it, for at-the back of all this wonderful work of social reform we have strong men who will continue to develop it in the int&amp;ests of our whole ileople. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>SHORTER WORKING HOURS.</head>
        <p>SHORTER WORKING HOURS.</p>
        <p>• I glory in the work the Government has done in the economic interests of the nation, but I feel, while we have made great progress on the economic side, we have pressing work before us in connection with the hours and conditions of labour. Through an International Associa- tion, in the work of which I am now taking an active ;wt. we are endeavouring to bring about by International co-operation the introduction of eight-h Our shifts instead of twelve-hour shifts</p>
      </div>
