      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>"SHALL WE GO ON THE' FIGURE 8?-"</head>
        <p>"SHALL WE GO ON THE' FIGURE 8?-"</p>
        <p>mingham programme, if he did, not thoroughly detest it. (Laughter and cheers.)</p>
        <p>And :low what do we see after all the Tariff Reform agitation in Warrington and elsewhere? Has this raging, tearing propaganda led to any useful iand substantial result? Certainly not. Why, the Protectionists themselves are turning tail; they have already thrown over the most important part of their own policy:, We have now got them fairly on the run, and, with your help, we will take precious good care in War- rington to keep them on the run. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>for all .men working in. continuous industries. There is an overwhelming case fOr this change, and our object is to 'bring pressure to bear on the Governments, of all industrial countries. To keep our reform within manageable limits We are, first of all, concentrating our energies on the iron and steel trade—a trade that much con- cerns Warrington. (Hear, hear.) Once we get the eight-hour shifts established throughout. that great trade, Then other continuous industries will follow suit. 4..ast, but not least, the bene- ficial effect of that reform will be felt in the introduction of more rational hours of work throughout the whole world of industry. If this is a natter of great, importance to the men of Britain, I do not consider it of less impor- tance to the women, and to tens of thousands of households in England, (Hear, hear.) If one follows this work as of supreme importance from moral, mental, physical and social points of view, I also for one set great store by the opportunity it will give as time goes on to en- courage through international cooperation better international relations. It is work of this character ,which in the long run will more and more help to promote, International peace. But peace, like charity, begins at home—(hear, hear)—and I have a Word to say about those who have been striving, to stir up old, out-of- date, discreditable rellgious animosity. I say that recent proceedings in Ulster have been nothing less than scandalous. No doubt such demonstrations as we have seen in Ulster, and not so very long since in Warrington, may have a comical side to them—these demonstrations with their wooden cannons and wooden guns, with, I fear. some very wooden heads behind them.' (Loud laughter and applause.) I must say, too, there is something quaint and ludicrous in the idea of the Member for Warrington— (laughter)—entering into the service of Mr. Cook in order personally In conduct a tour of, I am sure, very worthy ladies. from Ulster. (Laugh- ter.) I -do not know how many of you here have been getting your best bedrooms ready— (A Voice: "None of us," and laughter)—for their accommodation. It has struck me that the Member for Warrington,will have his hands</p>
        <p>pretty full with the job. (Laughter.) t sup- pose they are going to make ready for a con- siderable instalment of the members of the per- sonally conducted tour from Ulster at Walton Hall. (Loud laughter.) All this sort of thing' has its humorous side. If you want something in the way of a political farce to serve as a turn on the stage of a second-class music hall, this sort of thing is all right. (Laughter, and a Voice: "They're not coming.")</p>
        <p>My friend- tells me the Ulster women are not coming. Perhaps they want to stay at home with their husbands. (A Voice, ironically : I don't blame them; they'll be better of than in Warrington," and laughter.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>"SCOTCHED THE SNAKE."</head>
        <p>"SCOTCHED THE SNAKE."</p>
        <p>We have scotched the snake ;.we have got to kill it yet, and the defence of Free Trade is still a noble, a practical, and, even say in all sin- cerity; a Saered duty, that rests upon the Liberal women of England, and therefore, in a special degree, upon you, the members of this Associa- tion. But we Liberals do not stand for any negative policy. Not for one moment, since we came into power in 1906, have we been content in our home politics with the defence of Free Trade. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>DWELLS 1.75,1 THE COTTAGE.</head>
        <p>DWELLS 1.75,1 THE COTTAGE.</p>
        <p>That great, noble and unselfish Statesman, John Bright, in one of his speeches, made this remark :</p>
        <p>_ "The nation in every country dwells in the cottage, and unless the light of your constitu- tion can shine there, unless the object of your legislation and the excellence of your states- manship are impressed there, on the feelings and condition,of the people, rely upon it, you have yet to learn the duties of government." What I submit to you .now is, that the light of our constitutiOn has begun to shine into the cots, that the excellence of our statesmanship has now been impressed on the feelings and condition of the people, and, therefore, that we, as an English nation, have learned in the fullest measure, the real and full and complete. duties of government. (Loud applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>GHOULISH kND SELFISH.</head>
        <p>GHOULISH kND SELFISH.</p>
        <p>014-age pensions are an example of really great statesma.nship, and really great govern-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="3" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
