      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>BELFAST TOMFOOLERY.</head>
        <p>BELFAST TOMFOOLERY.</p>
        <p>Well, but ladies, amusing in its way as this sort of political tomfoolery may be, it may lead to very serious consequences. Just as working men only a few weeks since in Belfast were roasted at the furnace mouth, and beaten in the streets within an inch of their lives, so there may he siqualid tumult and dastardly outrage and bloodshed, and even the loss of the lives of some of their unfortunate dupes; if these poli- tical malefactors and treason-mongers continue their mal-practices. Even.at the eleventh hour one hopes that wiser counsels will prevail; but if, on the contrary, 4his shameful work goes on, and if it leads to deplorable consequences of such a Character, then I can assure you, ladies, there will be short shrift for the chief conspira- tors in this campaign against law and order, and for these barefaced traffickers in treason. (Loud cheers.) Only we will choose our own time, (Hear, hear.) At the moment we do not intend to turn King. CarsOn into Saint Carson —(laughter)----nor his office boys into acolytes. (Rene Wed laughter.) Neither will we make a present of an imitation halo to' every mediocre politician Who is aspiring for a little notoriety. (Laughter and applause.) If the time comes when there is serious trouble and disorder, that trouble and disorder, depend. upon it, will be put down with a firm hand.</p>
        <p>I saw a picture in a paper this morning of a mounted man in uniform, who seemed familiar, and when I read the lines below it, I found this officer was none other than " F.E." (A Voice: "Smith," and loud laughter.) I rather think the Member for Warrington has been in the same quarter, and on the same errand, encour- aging the bitter and ignorant prejudice of Protestant against Catholic, and Catholic against Protestant. Remember, ladies, in the rest of Ireland, and in a great part of Ulster, these bitter feuds are a sheer impossibility. If they still disgrace• the North-East corner of Ulster, what a reflectiow on the gospel and the spirit of ascendancy that has pervaded those four counties of Ulster far too long. I subMit to you with confidence, in conclusion, that it Is the duty of every good citizen to set his face and her face against this sort of thing, and to help finally to stamp it out. (Loud applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>AS GOOD AS AN ULSTER BATTLE.</head>
        <p>AS GOOD AS AN ULSTER BATTLE.</p>
        <p>Cheers for Mr Crosfield and Mrs. Crosfleld eonchxled the meeting, and the party then pro- ceeded to the spacious grounds, where they found ample amusement to occupy their atten- tion until the closing'tit-bit of the day's Outing, the fireworks display, illustrating a battle Jr the recent Turco-Bulgarian war. This mimic battle was of much interest lo the Warrington sightseers, and, according to crne, was about as real as anything that will be sees in Ulster. Wooden guns and noisy but harm- less fireworks were the chief implements ern- ployed. ..</p>
        <p>A tired, bnt jolly, company returned home to Warrington about eleven o'clock. The day's outing was voted one of the best that mai have been provided.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS. ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS. ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>'PROOF.".</p>
        <p>An intensely exciting melodrama, Sir FralleiS Burnand's "Proof," has drawn large houses to the R9yal Court Theatre this week. Mr.- and Mrs. William Maclaren.'s_ company make the piece as attractive and emotional as possible. Mr. Ftobert Gilbert's interpretation of Pkl-re Lorance was very convincing:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>"WHEN KNIGHTS WERE BOLD.*</head>
        <p>"WHEN KNIGHTS WERE BOLD.*</p>
        <p>Surely the production of such—an—extiaordin- ary success as " When,Knights Were Bold," at the Royal Court Theatre, Warrington, next week, constitutes a record, and the manage- ment and all concerned are to be congratulated on the fact that it is possible to do this without any 'cuts" or curtailznent whatever. The play, which is well known as Mr. James Welch's greatest success, has enjoyed the long- est run of the century, during which it has been visited three times by Queen Alexandra ; whilst it was the favourite play of the late King Edward. It was an immense success when it last visited Warrington, and it should prove equally popular on this occasion, for the com- pany is substantially the same. There will be a matinee on Monday Afternoon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>"MISS HOOK OF HOLLAND."</head>
        <p>"MISS HOOK OF HOLLAND."</p>
        <p>The plan of the reserved seats for the six per- formances of this musical comedy, the firstopf which will be given by members of the Musical Society on October 13th, is now open at the Royal Court Theatre, and will remain open daily up to the final performance on October 18th, from 10 a.m. to one p.m., from two to three, and from 7.30 to 9.30.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>" The' Bandit " is a highly dramatic playlet, which heads a good bill at the Hippodrome this 'WV. The story of the bandit's thirst for re- venge, and the capture of his enemy's adoPted daughter, whom he discovers is the one he lost years ago, is told in a vivid mariner. Haley's Juveniles present a srrlart and artistic vocal and dancing scena. Rowland Hill is always a popular turn at Warrington, and as "Ye Dame" he again Acores a success. Victoria Campbell, the Scottish nightingale, goddaughter of the late Queen Victoria, pays a welcome 'return visit, and, renders songs in her usual charming man- ner. A refined drawing-room scena is given by Farrow and Fiske.</p>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>A good programme arranged for next week will include: Ethel Ward and Company of 20 artistes, in ,the successful .sketch '"Her Madcap Majesty." . Van Der Koors, " Quack " illusion- ists, and Felix, the mind-reading duck -*;Jimmie Pullin, patter comedian; Schillott Bros., acro- bats; Wylie, iiopular tramp musician; Gray and White, talkative toje tellers; and Rosie Pink, comedienne.</p>
        <p>THE PICTUREDROME.</p>
        <p>TWO STRIKING FILMS.</p>
        <p>Two rOmarlialle films will be shown at the Pic turedrom e, Sankey-street, next week. "Fedora," a sensational social drama, in four parts, will be ruh on the lirst three nights, and from Thursday "The ,Imposter," a wonderful romance of American Society intrigue, will be on view. These will be worth seeing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>THEATRE ROYAL.</head>
        <p>THEATRE ROYAL.</p>
        <p>At this popular theatre an excellent pro- gramme of pictures has delighted the douses each night, and, the thrice weekly change is proving very popular. To-night EN chief film will be "The return of Thundercloud's Spirit."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>" DANTE'S INFERNO."</head>
        <p>" DANTE'S INFERNO."</p>
        <p>Exclusive ,rights for this district have been obtained for exhibiting "Dante's Inferno" next week. This famous production gas created an immense sensation on the Continent and in the United States. It is , described as one of the most serious and ambitious attempts yet made to interpret a classical poet by means of the cinematograph. Large houses are expecteh at the Royal next week to witness this great film, which is 5,000 feet long.</p>
        <p>"DON'T Looß OLD—Keep the ravages of time at bay by darkening the erf y streaks withLocKyym'sSum. Hun }Urn REsToßgn,which deepens to a naturalcolourin a few days Lockyer's maintains a youthful appearance. Large bots- everywhere.Post free 1/6.Bedford Laboratory.London,ft '</p>
      </div>
