      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <p>•</p>
        <p>Writers to tilt bit=</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. THE EDITOR of the "Examiner" invites corre- spondence on 411 subjects of public interest.</p>
        <p>LEITERS should be brief and pointed. Their object must be to impart or seek information, not abuse.</p>
        <p>LETTER'S must in all cases be accompanied by the</p>
        <p>name and address of the writer. - CommsNlat-110Na received early in the week— except irgent matters—can always be given the best attention.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <head>BETWEEN FIFTY AND SEVENTY.</head>
        <p>BETWEEN FIFTY AND SEVENTY.</p>
        <p>Sir,—What is to become of men between 50 and 70 years of age under present conditions of -labour? What can the Labour Exchanges do for such men when an age limit is fixed by SO many employers? It is a fair estimate to say that not more than ten per cent. of these men have ever had wages enough to save a -competency for old, age if they have brought up a family respectably. And here comes an- other question: What reward hive the majority for spending all they Let on .rife and family When they turn against him as soon as he gets • out of work and is dependent upon them? He Is not wanted and his life is, in many cases, a truly miserable one. Frequently he is only -half fed and insufficiently clad. He tramps -aimlessly about for hours and is then thankful for the Library Reading Room. He can read of boundless wealth and luxury, of sympathy and kindness, but for him it is a horrible mockery. Of course there are some lazy wastrels, but many- decent, worthy men amongst them. Will you ask your readers if they think it is JUSTICE?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
        <head>MONEY-LENDING AND OTHER THINGS.</head>
        <p>MONEY-LENDING AND OTHER THINGS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Your startling revelations about money- lending in this bad old town ought to stir more than a passing Interest. It would be easy to enlarge upon the stupidity of the borrowers and the cupidity of the lenders. But after all, the whole sorry business is but a symptom of a good deal that is rotten in Warrington.</p>
        <p>Drastic legislation and administration are Very good in their way, but at bottom What is needed is a•public opinion that is enlightened, and a public spirit that will refuse to tolerate preventable evils. It is no use mincing mailers. The tone of the town is low. Very little progress can be made until the people burn with shame at the exces- sive drinking, extensive gambling, and squalid immorality, to say nothing of the chimney- belching filthiness of. Warrington. We shall waste time blaming other people. All of us are up to the necks in responsibility for the social conditions around, and, if the decent citizens of the town had grit enough theyr could change the place beyond recognition. Here is a practical piece of constructive work to start upon—counter-attractions to the public- house. ,What kind of rational enjoyment is Open to a local working man after an exhaust- ing day's work? The moneylending evil will cure itself when men have got a thirst for some- thing better than beer. SPADES ARE. TRUMPS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
        <head>HATPIN DANGERS.</head>
        <p>HATPIN DANGERS.</p>
        <p>should like to draw the .attention of your readers, and of the authorities Of the town (who can, no doubt, remedy the Inatter) to the ' dangers of the protruding hatpin. I read in the daily paper at tile-beginning of the week of a woman in Blackburn who had her eye pierced with a hatpin, and only last week a young womattin this town lost ohe eye, due to the same cause. I think it is about time something was done to prevent thoughtless ladie•S Jroni being a source Of danger to the community at large. Perhaps someone may be tempted to cry out about interference with the liberty_of the public, but I think that when a little- May like this is a source of positive danger to men, as well as women, it is high time to stop it. I think we should cry out.pretty quick if men hail two or. three inches of sharp steel jutting out from each side of their-hats ! - I have noticed many times in the cars how thoughtless mothers nursing their own restless. children, will have these' dangerous spikes sticking out of their headgear, and I have, been in trepidation and trembling all the time for fear one would pene- trate the, tiny face. How CAN women be so thoughtless? What would one of them say if her husband lost tffe sight of his eyes through a simple hatpin? One shudders to think of .such things, and especially if the husband be an ordinary workman, with a family. Eyes are so precious and can never be replaced. And so, r. Editor, I think something could be done in Warrington to put an end to sudh a danger. I</p>
        <p>know Other pla-ces have taken up arms against it with success. We know that Germany stopped it, and Birmingham punishes offenders for this terrible practice. Hatpin shields can be bought for one penny, and short hatpins are quite as useful as long ones. I can speak from experience.</p>
        <p>In conclusion, I hope a crusade will spring up in this town against this useless two or three inches of sharp steel. I am hoping that the men of Warrington will speak and act in this matter; the sooner the better, for, indeed, they have more power to do s 6 than women have. I don't say for one moment that a single woman in this town would ever dream that she would injure-anyone, and she would shrink with horror at the idea that she would ever cause any person to lose an eye. But— _</p>
        <p>Evil is .wrought 133-r‘ want of thought-,</p>
        <p>As well as by want of heart</p>
        <p>And so, those who go- along thoughtlessly until some accident happens, must be made to think before it is too late. I sincerely hope that something will be done speedily, in order to prevent any more oi these unfortunate acci- dents. E.W.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0208" n="art0208">
        <head>HOME RULE AND THE EMPIRE.</head>
        <p>HOME RULE AND THE EMPIRE.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l don't think I should have trespassed on your space I this week, but for a remark by Phil Squod " in your issue of September 27th, where he infers that probably I would agree witti him "That Home Rule was inimical, poli- tically and economically; to the interests of the Empire." I wish to assure him in the friendliest manner possible that ever since I have been able to take an interest in politics until the present day I have been, and am still, an. unwavering Home Ruler, fully believing it is for the ultimate bet- terment and strengtho‘the Empire. May I add I consider the present prosperous cOndition of Irelaild, to which he refers, is principally due to the passing of Land Bills, on,pPie lines advo- cated by Nationalists for years. And so it has been. Nationalists have been the truest indica- tors of Irish requirements; and when their ad- -vice has been followed the best results have followed. When "Phil Squod " introduced the question of Scots' blood into Irish politics, I wondtr if , he had thought of the influence of. Irish blood in Scotland. ',have lately been.reading ,some re- views of a book, or possibly magazine article, , written by the present,Duke of Argyle,'a promi- nent Scottish peer of literary ability, in which he is proving that the great Highland clans are really descendants of Irish emigrants to Scot- land. I think Sir Janes Barr, ,another very distinguished Scot, emphasised the same idea at a recent Burns' or Caledonian festival in War- rington. Anyhow, Nationalists, have nothing to complain of in the generous manner with which the Scottish constituencies (as well as our friends in Wales) have returned majorities in favour of Home Rule. And we welcome all good friends, whatever their, blood or creed, or even if they have no creed at all, save a political one.</p>
        <p>Finally, we wish to win the good will and in- fluence of all intelligent and thoughtful men in England, like our friend "Phil Squod," in favour of I-lome Rule, believing when the Bill is passed, the rest of the work win be easier, if supported by their friendship. The work of conciliation is growing slowly but surely in Ulster, thanks mainly to the efforts of the Labour party, who, in that district, at least, are superseding the clerical element in their mis- sion of "Peace and good will" to all men. ,PATRICK O'BRIEN. Wilderspool-road.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0209" n="art0209">
        <head>' ANGLING. Warrington Workingmen's</head>
        <p>' ANGLING. Warrington Workingmen's</p>
        <p>Association. N.A. CENTRE. INDIFFERENT SPORT.</p>
        <p>More than the usual number visited Norton on Sunday last. Some members only caught one or two, others had as many as a dozen sizeable roach. H. Moores now takes second place on the set board, and E. Brookes comes third. These are the only changes as far as the Norton specials go. Mr. J. Cox had a fair catch of roach from here about a week ago, six fish turn- ing the scale at 33/oz. These must be consid- ered fair roach the- way all canals have fished during this summer, although it will be surpris- ing if the present weights stand for the y !ar. They will be the lowest weight ever recor.).i:d for Norton to win the season's specials.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0210" n="art0210">
        <head>GOAN Y.</head>
        <p>THE GOAN Y.</p>
        <p>Mickle Trafford had a fair share of membors last week-end, but the. result was very poor for the number there. The only fish taken was a pike of 61b. Hoz. by R. Clare. It gave him splendid sport while it lasted. He also had a run with another, that, according to his esti- mate; was, about twice as heavy angling as the one he successfully brought to land. Members who were roaching had very poor sport Aleed, not a single roach of decent size being reported. Gudgeosns were plentiful, but no specimens were caught, and one angler states they were a pest to him, spoiling his roach fishing for the greater part of the day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0211" n="art0211">
        <head>PROGRESS.</head>
        <p>PROGRESS.</p>
        <p>We are able to strite that there will be at least one more club to affiliate to the Northern Anglers for next year, and the membership, according to reports, will be more than 250. This is looking well for the N.A.A., and the centre in Warrington will soon pass other centres, who had many years' start of us. With a membership, of say, 300, there is no reason why subscriptions should not be lowered, to give every angler an opportunity to become a' club member.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0212" n="art0212">
        <head>J. DRYERS' FINE RECORD.</head>
        <p>J. DRYERS' FINE RECORD.</p>
        <p>Mr. James Bryers, who has been an angler for 25 years, and has tried his skill in waters abroad, as well as in this country, is considered by the members one of our best specimen hun- ters and workers. As 'a worker for a society, he is considered the best. He is always selected by his cIUbS• tci peg out the lengths where the inatches are to be held. He was the first man to be selected to peg out for the Eng- lish Championship, but when the officials heard of his record from the River bee, he was at once placed on the team, although, as he once stated, he Would have been far happier pegging out, as he cannot settle to contest fishing. Al- though he is never out of the prizes, he has yet to' his first N.A. prize. • As a specimen angler' he stands easy first. His favourite stream is the River Dee, where, in one sitting, he has taken 32 bream, weighing 711 b. On another occasion he took 16 bream, weighing 401 b. Higher up stream at Llangollen his catch of dace was 251 b. in one day, ranging from Boz. to lloz. each. Trout also were taken up to 21b., perch Ilb., and gudgeon from the swims at Chester litoz. The Gowy is his next favoured spot. Here he has the record for a catch of roach; his catch was 18 roach, weigh- ing 151 b., the largest being 11b. 12oz. He has also taken 321 b. of roach from the Gowy in one day's catch, pike up 1.9 81b., trout 21b. 20z., and numerous date over 10oz. From the Severn he has taken bream 41b. 40z., roach 111 b., chub 21b. 40z., pike 91b., and a 21b. catch of roach in one day.</p>
        <p>His angling abroad was at Gibraltar, Malta, Egypt (in'the Nile), Africa, and Channel Islands. At Gibraltar rock fish and sea bream were the species taken ; at Mfilta, mackerel and sea bream. On the Nile, a Class of fish was caught resembling the herring, only harder in the mouth, and many times. when angling in the favoured haunts he was plagued by the young crocodiles (called baby crocks). In Africa he fThhed the Buffalo River- for the water snake, which Is a favourite dish for Kaffirs ; they average a yard long. The Tugela River produces small pike and a kind of fish resem- bling our minnow. On the Sunday River good sport is to be had with jack pike.- In the Chan- nel Islands, conger, codlin and roach fish were taken.</p>
        <p>Coming home again, his favourite bait for the Dee was bread• and 'worm, ground baft, fine bean and soaked crusts; Chester swims, the annato maggot; the Gowy, stone bait and stick bait cannot be beaten for cold weather, "and that is the time you will get your specimens," says Jim. His best year was in 1607, when he was successful in winning every set prize in three separate clubs, with the exception of a gudgeon set, and he tied with the next man for this. His bream fishing, for which he is specially noted, is done during the night, but he never takes other fish during the dark hours. This kind of angling is barred in many societies, but we cannot see where there is anything wrong with it, so long as the angler does not use the side, night line, or trimmer. This kind of angling is not sport, and anything that is unfair would be the last thing that an angler of' Mr. Bryers' calibre would do. He is a member that the society could not well do without, for he has brought more novices to the Northern Anglers than any other three members, and is always on the look-out for fresh members. He has spent many a clay, both at Chester and Mickle Traf- ford, tutoring th 6 novice in the ways of the streams, and he has been selected to give a few of the Northern Anglers"officials a day's fishing at the Gowy. . He *has earned the respect of all Warrington anglers, and 1 a favourite with the younger generation of Izaak Walton. May he enjoy his angling career for many, many years is the wish of all Warrington anglers. A. S.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0213" n="art0213">
        <head>RYLANDS' RECS. SECOND WIN.</head>
        <p>RYLANDS' RECS. SECOND WIN.</p>
        <p>The League Match between "Viands' Second and St. Helens took place at • Gorsey-lane en- closure. Both teams were at full strength. St. Helens kicked off. Even play was the order, Manchester, on the right, was nearly in, but kicked-, over the full-back's head too far. "Viands', who were the lighter lot, began to assert themselves, and were nearly through. Half-time arrived with the score nil.</p>
        <p>'Viands' commenced the second half in grim earnest, and good play by Morris took 'play to the railway end. From a pack, formed close in, Riley scored. Potter failed at goal. At this point the z" Wiremen " were all over their opponents, and Potter, following his kick, got well under, and just touching the ball, made the opening for Ogden to score under the posts, Potter goaling. The final read: Rylands' Recs., one goal two tries (eight points) ; St. Helens Recs., nil.</p>
        <p>Potter, Morris and Btirtmi played well for the Bees._ Manchester and Stott, for Bt. Helens, were the pick.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0214" n="art0214">
        <head>11)1ands' Teams for To-day.</head>
        <p>11)1ands' Teams for To-day.</p>
        <p>Rylands' (v. Cadishead, at Gorsey-lane) : F. Horrocks ; Griffiths, Bramhall, A. Horrocks and G. Warren; Floyd and Gallop.; Brandwood; Mason, Slater, Whittaker, Buckler and Ogden. Rylands' 2nd (v. Warrington White Rose, away): Potter; Scarisbriek, Morris, Barton and Cooper; Riley and Backhouse ; Welsby, Bibby, Cooper, Weldon, Myles and Hansbury.</p>
        <p>Most Remarkable. "He's a perfectly devoted, father I" , "Is that so?" " Yes ; if their /new baby keeps him awake a: night, he never scolds his wife about it."</p>
        <p>The most imitative thing in all the world is to try to be original.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0215" n="art0215">
        <head>arAz-</head>
        <p>arAz-</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 4, 1913.</p>
        <p>icT</p>
        <p>LYMM.</p>
        <p>LINES FROM LYMM.</p>
        <p>The Liberal ladies had. a great day. They painted Belle Vue fed. • It was a splendid outing. The visitors thoroughly enjoyed it. My word, didn't they like the figure eight! Is it true that one couple did .six rounds? Who was it that bought a lot of toffee? This was to feed the monkeys. The apes found it a bit too sticky. After the sumptuous tea the fun began. How daintily two Lymm ladies waltzed. They 'simply could not resist the temptation. The music was so absolutely fascinating. The May Queen tea was a great success« How the youngsters punished the good things. They thoroughly enjoyed the after concert. Well, they might, for it wag excellent.</p>
        <p>ay Queen Tea.—One of the desires of the Lymm May Queen Festival Committee, namely, that of providing a free tea for the children, was realised on Saturday evening: Undaunted by the present adverse balance, the committee and their friends' generously gave a large share of the "wherewithall " to provide the children who took part in the last festival with a jolly time, and right successful were their efforts. It was an eager and expectant crowd of young people who assembled at the Wesleyan School- room, and by the hearty manner in which they demolished the tasty 'provisions at tfie tea- tables and the spirit with which they entered</p>
        <p>the preacher at the morning service was the Rev. G. C. Briggs, Latchford. In the afternoon a children's fruit and flower service took place, at which the Rev. G. H. Garnett, St. Peter's, Warrington, delivered an appropriate address. The Rev. C. H. Conybeare, Rostherne, preached in the evening. Under the conductorship of Mr. W. Cooke, the choir gave an admirable render- ing of Macfarren's 'God said, 'Behold I have given you every herb bearing seed,'" soloist, H. Clare% The offertories, which were for tho Choir Fund, amounted to £l4. An effective scheme of decoration was accomplished by the following ladies :—East end, Mrs. Newmarch 'and Miss W. Norris; pulpit, Mrs. Callender; tont, Miss D. Norris; screen, Mrs. Burrows and Mrs. Watkin ; windows, Mesdames T. Dixon, A. H. Evans, Hughes, Johnson, Molesworth,' Palmer and D. Stewart-Smith, and the Misses Boumphrey, Hatton, Hubback and Rawlins.</p>
        <p>Cpngregational Harvest Thanksgiving.—The Congregational Church was the scene of special harvest thanksgiving services orSunday: The Rev. J. A. Penaliggon occupied the pulpit, and his themes at the morning and evening services. were "The growth of the seed" and "No man,. having put his han-I to the plough, and, looking back, is fit for the Kingdom of God." In the afternoon a children's serv;ce was held, at which addresles vere given by the Rev. J. A. Penalig- gon and Mr. J. IL Holt. During the day, the scholars sang several anpronriate-hymns, and in the evening the choir rendered the anthem. "They that wait upon the Lord," the solo t•eing taken by Mr. J. Ellis. and the quartette by Mrs. Lewis, and Messrs. W. Myatt, C. Leather and F. Guest. There were good congregations at each of the services. The lady members of the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0216" n="art0216">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>THE ROAD TO FIDDLER'S FERRY. Evidence in Right of Way Case. _ _</p>
        <p>On the request of the Warrington Rural Dis- trict Council, Mr. Woodward, Penketh, attended the meeting held in Bewsey Chambers, Warring- ton, on Wednesday afternoon, to lay his views about the right of way over the road leading to Fiddler's Ferry. Mr. Woodward protested against the remark made at a previous meeting of the Council that the road was a path which...led from nowhere to nowhere. It was a path which led from a defi- nite somewhere to an equally definite some- where. He had evidence that the road had been used for a long time by the public. Councillor Furness said from the mass of evi- dence it seemed to have been in use from time immemorial. The Chairman (Councillor Mather) : Has it ever been dedicated to the public? Councillor Knowles: Are there any maps to show it was used before the Highway Act of 1835? Councillor Garnett: It goes pack to the Nor- man Conquest. (Laughter.) Many Aged Witnesses. Mr. Woodward said he knew of no map. There ' had never been previously any attempt to pre- vent the public using the path, and no locked gate had been placed on it until the dispute arose. He had two persons who could go back seventy years, two who could go back sixty, and two who could go back fifty years to prove that it was a public right of way. There was never any other way to Fiddler's Ferry. Mr. Sutton (the clerk) : The .point is—ls this path worth fighting for. Can you not get an- other way? Mr. Woodward: There are two other ways, but they are absolutely private roads. This is the only public way. After some further discussion the Clerk pointed out that it might cost the Council. £4OO at least to fight the case in the Law Courts. Wm</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0217" n="art0217">
        <head>LY:t* M WOMEN LIBERALS AT BELLE VUE,</head>
        <p>LY:t* M WOMEN LIBERALS AT BELLE VUE,</p>
        <p>A Happy Bay With Warrington Friends.</p>
        <p>Photograph by Burren.</p>
        <p>SOME OF THE LYMNI CONTINGENT. From left to right—Back row: Mesdames Jones, Bell, Barber, Guest, Wright, F. Guest, Miss Mcltityre, Mrs. Daniels, Mrs. Barnes, Miss Mer cer, Miss F. Mercer. Front row: Mesdames Johnson, Driver, Harrison, Lord, Radcliffe, Wyatt, Edwards, Mclntyre, •Seymour and Bell.</p>
        <p>A contingent of the Lymm Ladies' Liberal Association visited Belle Vue Gardens, Man- chester, on Monday, and there they spent an enjoyable time. The early pert of the afternoon was spent in " doing " the zoological collection and visiting other features so profusely provided. The Figure Eight Railway was liberally patron- ised by the Liberal ladies. Prior to tea they were speeiltlly photographed by the "Examiner" representative. At tea the Lymm ladies joined those from Warrington and enjoyed a sumptuous repast in the spacious dining hall. Amongst others present at that function were: Mesdames</p>
        <p>Barber, Barnes, Isaac Bell, John Bell, Daniels, Driver, Edwards, F. Guest, Guest, Harrison, Johnsou, Jones, Lord, Mclntyre, Radcliffe, Sey- mour, Wright and Wyatt; the Misses C. Mercer, F. Mercer and Mclntyre. They participated .after tea in the demonstra- tion organised• by the Warrington Association, and enjoyed the speeches by Mrs. A. H. Cros- field, the president of the Warrington Women's Liberal Association, and Mr. A. H. Crosfield, full reports of which will be found in another column.</p>
        <p>Lymm Women's Association, and in doing so said : I do not want to sit down before I extend a most hearty welcome to the members of the Lyntim Women's Liberal Association, now with us. I am sure we are all delighted to have the Lymm ladies present, and if this is the first time we have met at such a pleasant function, I hope it will not be the last occasion upon which we shall meet at similar functions. (Loud ap- plause.) The rest of the evening was spent in taking part in the numerous amusements and in wit- nessing the Battle of Lulu Bruges, a splendid scenic firework display. The .p ulwas regarded as one of the most enjoyable ever held.</p>
