::..:::;:;;;:::::•:::::::::":•.:::::::ii.::•!!':::::!•.:!.:Ii:::::.::::::::i::::::•!"•:i:*.!!!!::7::**.:i':*:::1::*::.::::'::::**:::'''." .: . ...,:•: l'...;?:::::::-.:;::::fe.:i.?:,.*::.:•'::::::::::::...?.:::::,.:::::::-.....:•:::M..::::..?:...,......:.......-...•-•.:- ...:.....: ........-.....-. ..,.... - ... , :....:::::•::*:::::i.:::::"::?;:i:i:f...:: ~ ..,..,... ......., ~-.............2...i.:.:.....:„.„...........,..::::::::*.......:.....:.-.:•., -: ........ -...?..............:......„.., . ........ .. ,f4::-:;:ki,.::::.:;:;::::::::::,::::.,,,,,::,::....., ........,:::::::........:::-...-.::::::::::::, ......,... , .s. • • ::%::,:::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::.::::...:•....:::,:::::.*;::::::, -..-. •. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0218" n="art0218">
        <p>into the concert that was afterwards given; they had a thoroughly enjoyable time, and no doubt wished- the event were a weekly one. The evening's programme consisted of such items as illustrated songs by Mrs. Lewis, illustrated reci- tations.by Miss Rushton, and the exhibition by Messrs. Webb and Hesketh of a large number of lantern slides, comprising pictures of Norway, Lymm May Queen festival, some humorous and movable ones, &amp;c. The enthusiastic band of entertainers was composed of Mesdames' Smith, Brown, Jones, Lewis, Newton, and Pearson; the Misses Hazelhurst, Boond4 Mairs, Pearson, Wragg, E. Winstanley and Wright; and Messrs. Hudson, J. Webb, jun., Brown, Druce„--Edwards, Hesketh, Jones and Newton.</p>
        <p>Miner's Last swim'.—Another drowning case occurred in the Bridgewater Canal, be- tween Lymm and Thelwall. On Sun- day John Keegan, Oxford - street, Leigh, with some friends, was fishing in the canal, but as there- was very little sport, Keegan decided to have a bathe. He divetl, got into difficulties, and sank. Shortly after a boat came along and the boatmen commenced dragging operations. Acting-Sergeant Robin- son arrived just in time to assist in the recovery of the body.—Mr. T. J. Ridgway conducted an inquest at the -Pickering Arms on Tuesday, when a verdict of "Accidentally drowned" was returned.</p>
        <p>A Broom Edge Festival.—The harvest festival in connection with ,the Broom Edge Primitive Methodist Church took place on Sunday. Mr. Schofield, Manchester, was the Special preacher. At the afternoon meeting the scholars rendered several special hymns. On Monday: night, the Lynktn Primitive Methodist Choir gave an en- joyable interpretation of "The Tramp's Testa- ment," Mr. W. T: Moss supplying the narrative, whilst the Rev. R. W. Ferguson conducted the devotional part of the gathering. , At the con- clusion of the service of song the fruit and vegetables, were disposed of by sale, Mr. Blease being an amusing and successful "auctioneer." The work of decorating the church was under- taken by Mesdames Ford, Moss, and Reid; the Misses Amy and Alice Priestner, Perrin, Mary. Emily and Jane Blease, L. Lea, M. Moss and G. Tktlbot; and Messrs. IT. Perrin and Joe Blease.</p>
        <p>King'sMessengers.—The Lyiam and Statham branehes of. the Children's Union and King's Messengers held a sale of work in the Drill Hall on Wednesday. The display of articles, made last winter, was most praiseworthy. The fancy work and embroidery on the Lytam stall and the various achievements of the Statham stall were especially noticeable. The following is a list of the stalls, teachers, and helpers: Woodcarving, brasswork and basket-making, Misses Dalton Duncan, Norris and Rawlins; fancy work, Mrs. Cooke and Miss Roach; embroidery and plain sewing, the Misses Hankinson'eand Saville; re- freshments, Mrs. Dr. Burrows, and the Misses Molesworth, -Norris, and Watkin ; . fruit and flowers, Mrs. Newmarch, Mrs. and Miss Callen- der; Statham stall, Mrs. Watson, and the Misses Dalton, Hunt, and Wright; shooting,. gallery, Mrs. Ditchfield and Mr. T. Hatton; the village pump, Masters Tom Cooke and Harry Worth- ington.</p>
        <p>Parish Church Festival.—The harvest festival at the Parish Church was held on Sunday, when</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0219" n="art0219">
        <p>church decorated the sacred edifice with flowers, cereals and plants. The sale of fruit, &amp;c., which was held in the Schoolroom on Monday night, realised ..t1 6s. 10d. for the Poor Fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0220" n="art0220">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>Harvest Festival.—The harvest thanksgiving services were held in the Wesleyan Church on Sunday. The ladies of the congregation had, as usual, decorated the interior of the church with fruit, corn, flowers and vegetables. The morning preacher was the Rev. W. A. Beckett, and in the evening the pulpit was occupied by Mr. F. Wright. The anthem "Ye shall dwell in the land" was rendered by the choir during the evening service, the soloists being Miss Ward and Mr. W. Bate. Mr. Weaver officiated as organist with his accustomed, ability.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Guild Social.—The opening meeting of the winter session of the Guild was held on Monday evening, and, as usual, took the form of a social. The Rev. E. Morgan presided over a large assembly. Mr. William Seed was re- sponsible for a well-executed pianoforte solo. Mrs. Angus contributed the well-known song " Daddy " in pleasing style, and an enjoyable and somewhat unique feature of tho proceedings was a musical monologue, "Johnnie, Me and You," by Mrs. W. Bate. There were competi- tions in guessing the names of songs and the titles of 'celebrated pictures. Games, including "How do you like your neighbours?" "The Derby race," and "Swiss chairs," were freely indulged in, and the free-and-easy character of the evening was commented upon favourably by those present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0221" n="art0221">
        <head>GREAT SAN KEY.</head>
        <p>GREAT SAN KEY.</p>
        <p>St. Mary's Church.—The harvest festival was held on Sunday. he Rev. Isaiah Switer, of St. Ambrose's, Widnes, occupied the pulpit. A flower service was held in the afternoon, when the Vicar (Rev. J. R. Jones) gave an address.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0222" n="art0222">
        <head>CHURCH CONGRESS.</head>
        <p>CHURCH CONGRESS.</p>
        <p>The subject for discussion at the Church Con- gress at ,Southampton' on Tuesday was "The Kingdom of God and the Baces." The relations between backward and civilised races were first considered, aild afterwards those between civilised 'nations. At a inceting in the after- noon a resolution protesting against the Welsh DiseStablishment Bill was passed.</p>
        <p>Goodness, Yes. \ "Did you ever tell that young man that late hours were bad for One?" , asked the father at the breakfast table.</p>
        <p>"Well, father," replied the wise daughter, "late hours may be bad for one, but they're all right for two."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0223" n="art0223">
        <p>the matter worth going to litigation about? The parish would not have to pay the cost, and that would have to be borne by the whole area of the Council. It was finally decided, on the motion of Coun- cillor Furness, seconded by Councillor Knowles, that the Council act as a committee to ascertain the evidence available and report later.</p>
        <p>Accident at the Tannery.—Mr. B. Ireland met with an accident at the Penketh Tanning Com- pany works on Saturday. He received a com- pound fracture of the arm. Dr. Murray was called in, and ordered him to be taken to the Warrington Infirmary.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Church Festival.—The festival was held on Sunday. The preachers. were the Revs. H. Williamson and If. Middlewood. The church was tastefully decorated by ,the members and friends. The choir rendered special music.</p>
        <p>St. Mary's M ission.—On Wednesday the har- vest service was held. The decorations were creditably executed by Miss Ansdell, Miss A. P. Jones, Miss Owen, the Misses Manning, and Mrs. E. Dumbill.. The Rey. A. M. Mitchell preached.</p>
        <p>Death of an Old Resident: —There passed away at Greenfield House on Thursday, Maria Pusill, at 72 years of age. The deceased was one of the oldest residents in the village. Born at Sandy Nook, close to Heath-road, she lived all her life in the parish. She had made many friends, and was respected in the neighbourhood. 94 • ,</p>
        <p>. Eight .in Two Rooms.—lt was 'reported at a meeting of the Rural District Council on Wed- nesday that a case of alleged overcrowding had been discovered. It was alleged that eight per- sons, consisting of a man, his wife, four, children and two lodgers were occupying two bedrooms.— The Rev. Councillor Mitchell: A mild case com- pared with those in Burtonwood.—Councillor Mather (chairman) : According to you. Burton- wood is a terrible place.—Councillor Mitchell: You cannot turn them out if they have no other house to live in.—Councillor Longton : You should not make Penketh so attractive.— Councillor Knowles: What is the attraction—. the brewery or the church? (Laughter.)—lt was decided to serve 14, days' notice.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0224" n="art0224">
        <head>EVENING SCHOOLS.</head>
        <p>EVENING SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>Lord Shuttleworth and Sir WilHain Mather, speaking-at 'Burnley at the annual meeting of the Union of Lancashire and Cheshire Institutes,, emphasised the need of a deVelopment of even- ing continuation schools to continue, the educa- tional work done in the elementary schools...</p>
        <p>A Hustling Newsboy. Newsboy: Great mystery. Fifty victims! Paper, mister? Passer-by: Here, boy, I'll takl one, (After reading a moment.) • Say, boy, there's nothing of the kind in this paper. Where is it? Newsboy: That's the mystery, guvnor. You're the 51st victim.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0225" n="art0225">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0226" n="art0226">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH</p>
        <p>TOWNSHIP TITTLE-TATTLE.</p>
        <p>The sleepless man can now sleep.</p>
        <p>The sirens have restarted on the canal.</p>
        <p>This "Heathen" finds them a lullaby.</p>
        <p>Pretty " 'Mums" are grown by the Heathens." Illustrated by the Garden Society's Show, Chrysanthemums was the strong card.</p>
        <p>The display was a very pretty Oile Who grew a "Mum" a foot in diameter? Was it a real, ttue chrysanthemum? Not a growth of the imagination, eh! Stockton Heath Wesleyans distinguished ihemselves. • Their, harvest festival was a marked success. The ladies presented a lovely decked church. The congregation were large—quite a record. An angler went to sleep on the canal bank. Before he slept he had a basket of ten fish. In his creel he had a loaf of food.</p>
        <p>When he woke up the contents had vanished. fie wants to know what became of the loaf and fishes. . Some two-Tooted sharks must have been about The angler is now anxious to hook that species of shark.</p>
        <p>The Heath had reason to feel proud this week. A real, live Government Inspector visited the ;Ownship. _ _</p>
        <p>The representative of John Burns had a Simple ob.</p>
        <p>It was to consider whether money should be borrowed.</p>
        <p>This, a sum of £2,250, is to be spent on new roads.</p>
        <p>Whether the money can be borrowed we shall know later.</p>
        <p>Firemen at Practice.—The members of the Stockton Heath Fire Brigade, having come to terms with the Fire Brigade Committee, were out for practice on Saturday. They did a con- siderable amount of hose drill and gave pro- mise, proVided there is a sufficient water pres- sure, of, doing useful work in the event of a future outbreak of fire.</p>
        <p>A Membership of 700.—There are at least 700 recognised gardeners in 'Stockton Heath, but this does not exhaust the number of those who follow the cult of flower rearing. The 700 are members of the Stockton .Heath Garden Society, and as there are still about 300 outside the pale of the society an effort is to be made during the winter to gather these absentees into the society's fold.</p>
        <p>A Destructive " Goal"—A destructive "goal " was scored during the ,match between Stockton Heath Association team and Lymm Amateurs. The Stockton Heath men brought the ball to the Amateurs' goal, where Saville, the Lymm custodian, indulged in a ponderous kick. He sent the ball out of the ground and across the street. The leather finally found the "-goal" by plunging through the window of a house, smash- ing the glass. It might be called a " pane-alty goal."</p>
        <p>A Motor Fire Engine.—Now the Fire Brigade at the Wilderspool Brewery have been engaged to fight Stockton Heath fires, and generally play the useful part of firefighters to the immediate district, it is reported that the firm have under consideration the securing of a motor fire engine to replace the present horse.drawn vehicle. As is well known, motor vehicles are rapidly dis- placing horse labour for brewery purposes, and in the course of time few horses will be available at the brewery, hence the consideration of the need for the provision of a motor fire ejngine. Nothing, of course, has been done in the Matter as yet, beyond the subject ha.N\ing been mooted, but it is a question which will have to be faced in the future.</p>
        <p>Band of Hope.—The opening meeting of St. Thomas' Band of Hope was held on Thursday, when the Vicar (Rey. J. Collins) gave a very in- teresting address on duty. There was a large number present, afid the Secretary, with the workers, were kept busy all die evening enrol- ling new members. Mr. J. Claytiin kindly gave a few selections on the gramophone. During the evening the Vicar, on behalf of the committee, and worker's, pfeSented Mr. W. Mather, who, is leaving for Canada, with a teacher's Bible and a Church service. The Vicar, while regretting his leaving them, wished him every success in the future. After a few parting words from the Secretary; Mr. Stannard, Mr. Whiteman, Mr. C. Hallows, and the leader, Mr. Hallows, Mr. Mather suitably responded, and in thanking them for their handsome presents, 'said they would serve to 'remind him of many happy and useful hours spent' in the dear old Band of Hope, which he hoped would go on and prosper 3till more.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Harvest F esti val.—Most successful harvest festival services were held at the Wes- leyan Church, Walton-road, Stockton Heath, on Sunday, and there were record attendances. The ladies showed remarkable taste in the decoration of the edifice, chrysanthemums, gladioli, lilies and other fine bloom entering largely into the scheme of decoration, while there was also a fine assortment of fruit, &amp;C. The preacher should have been Mr. J. W. Deakin, of North- wich, but at the last moment he notified the officers that he could not attend, and a young student from the Didsbury Wesleyan College was impressed into the service. He preached two most interesting and appropriate sermons. The choir distinguished themselves by the way they rendered special anthems. They were heard at their best in "The wilderness" and "0 Lord, how manifold are Thy works," which they gave in the evening to a crowded congrega- tion. The .young people's serbice in the after- noon was a great success, appropriate hymns and recitations being given by the youthful members. A pleasant fruit banquet on Mon- day ended a memorable festival.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0227" n="art0227">
        <head>SHOW OF CHRYSANTHEMUMS.</head>
        <p>SHOW OF CHRYSANTHEMUMS.</p>
        <p>A Remarkably Fine Exhibition. Chrysanthemums bulked largely in the autumn exhibition of the Stockton Heath Gar- den Society, held in the Institute on Saturday, possibly because it was the leasonal flower. Dahlias were also there, and some fine rose bloom was on view. The show, on the whole, presented a remarkably pretty spectacle. So' close in point of merit were some of the exhibits that it was no easy matter for the judges to make their adjudications. The following were the awards. : Outdoor Single C h rysantherniams.—Three varieties, in sprays: 1, E. W. Whitehouse; 2, A. J. Mottram ;3, W. Lockett. ' Indoor Japanese.—Two plants, grown in pots: 1, J. Colley. Three varieties, single blo ms :2, A. Shepherd. Outdoor Japanese.—Six varieties, in spays : 1, A. J. Mottram ; 2, E. W. Whitehouse; 3, E. Mills. Three varieties, in sprays : 1, W. Lockett ;•2, H. Mottram; 3, G. Williams. One spray or stem, not containing more than nine fully-developed blooms, any variety: 1, A. J. Mottram ; 2, J. Col- ley; 3, W. Lockett ;m, E. Mills. Six varieties, single blooms: 1, J. Colley; 2, E. W. White- house; 3, A. J. Mottram. Three varieties, single blooms: 1, A. Shepherd; 2, J. 11. Fergusson; 3, Mottram. Two•plants, in bloom, two varieties: 2, G. Williams. One plant, in bloom: 1, G. Wil- liams. _ _</p>
        <p>Cut Blocm.—Bowl or vase of one variety of chrysanthemum, arranged ,for effect: li. A. J. Mottram; 2 Mrs. Hargreaves; 3, A. Shepherd; m, E. W. Whitehouse. Bowl or vase, arranged for -effect, containing two or more varieties: 1, Mrs. Hargreqes; 3, E. Taylor; m, A. Shepherd. Epergne, arranged for effect, containing any number of varieties: 1, Mrs. Hargreaves; 2, A. J. Mottram.</p>
        <p>DaMias.—Six dahlias, shown in , stand: 1, F. Kippax; 2, E. W. Whitehouse; 3, A. J. Mottram. Six dahlias, pompone, shown in stands: 1, F. Kippax; 2, E Taylor. Eighteen single dahlias: 1, A. J. Mottram; 2, E. Taylor; 3, E. W. White- house. Collection of outdoor blooms, arranged in vases: 1, E. W. Whitehouse; 2, A. J. Mottram; 3, F. Kippax. Three vases of cactus dahlias, arranged for effect: 1, F. Rippax; 2, E. W. Whitehouse; 3, A. J. Mottram. Three vases of pompone dahlias, arranged for effect: 1, F. Kippax; 2, E. Taylor. Roses.--Three vases of roses, arranged for effect: 1, J. B. Baxter; 2, F. Broady; 3, A. J. Mottram.</p>
        <p>Vegetables.—Collection of six kinds, excluding tomatoes amid cucumbers : 1, F. Kippax ; 2, A. Owen ; 3, E. Taylor; he, E. Mills. General.—Collection of six species of cut flowers: 1, A. J. Mottram ; 2, E. W. Whitehouse; 3, E. Taylor; he, Mrs. Langley. Aster (Michael- mas Daisy), four varieties: 1, E. Taylor; 2, W.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0228" n="art0228">
        <p>e1m.••0.__._..,••••""</p>
        <p>Skelton; 3, Mrs. Hargreaves; he, E. W. W/lite- house. Children's Competition:—Basket of autul berries and foliage, arranged for effect: 1, 5 Broady; 2, Lily Melling; J, E. Hughes. The opening ceremony was performed by W. H. Woodcock, the chairman of the eeDl- -</p>
        <p>LOCAL STREET IMPROVEMENTS. . --Council 'Ask for £2,520. Major J. Stewart, the Local Government Bora Inspector, attended at the Hall Cafe, Stockton Heath; on Thursday, to inquire into the Butlt; corn Rural District Council's application borrow A:2,520 for works of private street provement for the parish of Stockton Councillors J. Whitley, G. Pilling and F. Pea'; son also attended, with Mr. G. F. Ashton (clerk) and Mr. R. Garnett (the District Council Or</p>
