        <p>Mr. Whitley's Objection. if of Mr. Whitley said he objected, on beha—oe himself and the members of his family, t°,:ticl treatment of passages 12 to 14, Chapel-lane. passages from 42 to 51, Walton-'road. t 66f • first ease there were two small cul-de-sacs, and 89ft. , There was no traffic over them, to on the opposite side was a piece of land t could not be utilised for building purpose°'„el- cause the by-laws would not allow. In ChaF„6t lane there was a fence for the macadam to 1;c:od against, and in Walton-road there Was a g. galvanised fence set on a brick founds le There was no necessity for skirting qere• de, desired that all the passages should be 1119ing but he objected to some of the changes as be ri; unnecessary. He could get some of the ‘4l3te done cheaper than the Surveyor's estiraa stated. 8 ft Mr. Garnett said that although there I`,ll be galvanised fence there, a flag skirting woiliu better. Mr. Whitley: We think not. -•, let Mr. Garnett: I suggest that, the Councilco Mr. Whitley get the work- done if he says be la get it done cheaper. The work to be done satisfaction. was</p>
