      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0234" n="art0234">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 4, 1913.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 4, 1913.</p>
        <p>'THE HOME Of JEREMIAH HORROX</p>
        <p>The itstronomer-Gurate oho First Observed the Transit of Venus.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0235" n="art0235">
        <head>h HiLGREVIAGE TO RUE HOOLE.</head>
        <p>h HiLGREVIAGE TO RUE HOOLE.</p>
        <p>I have always been fascinated by the stars, and always' (possibly. in consequence) revered the astronomers.</p>
        <p>And in astronomy, as in most things, the pioneers are the most attractive people. Much more dear than the modern observer with his colossal telescope and photographic plates, is the memory of men who, by sheer observation and the, (horrible) science of mathematics, found their way to big discoveries. Thus much in explanation of a pilgrimage to Much Lloole. I suppose that, even the name of the. village, is foreign to the average man, aid. that few would know where to look for it upon the map. .pilgrim need expect no beauty. The Indeed, unless he have a decided strain of hero-worship, he May be disappointed at what he will find. For myself I was abundantly content. It vv. as enough that I had seen they modest home of a pioneer', had seen the church where lie minis- tered,.and read with fresh zest the modest lines in which he described. the Supreme Moment of his life. -</p>
        <p>Lions. I then beheld a most agreeable spectacle. the object of my sanguine wishes—a spot of a perfectly circular :shape, which had,. already fully entered upon the sun's,disc on the left, so that the limbs of the Sun and Venus precisely coincided, forming an angle of contact. Not doubting that .this was really the shadow of the planet, I imniediately applied myself sedulously to observe it."</p>
        <p>_ • _ This he did.until sunset, when the momentous watch was ended by the darlinesS. •</p>
        <p>A FELLOW-STUDENT.</p>
        <p>Behind this triumph of calculation lay much research and patient thinking. In this Jeremiah Horrox did not toil alone, and, .to make our story complete, N've must introduce , his feridw labourer, !William Crabtree. The loneliness of Horrox's life was cheered by a fine friend- ship. A fellow both in humility and obscurity, yet a fellow in rich enthusiasm was this 'Wil- liam Crabtree, a. draper, of Broughton, Salfol.d, With a genius for mathematics he showed that books of tables then in use were inaccurate, and greatly assisted Horrox in the intricate calcula- tions he had to make. It is, indeed, to be doubted whether the transit of Venus would ever have been observed had it not been for this skilful mathematical help. ! The calculations were made and they proved correct. As the day of the expected transit of Venus was a Sunday, .Horrox was in fear that his duties at church would, interfere with. his observing at the right moment, and asked Crab- tree to watch also.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0236" n="art0236">
        <head>QUAINT COUNTRYSIDE.</head>
        <p>A QUAINT COUNTRYSIDE.</p>
        <p>Much Hole lies a few miles. to the south-west of Preston, upon the Ormskirk road. There is Hoole, Little HoOle, and Much Hoole, the last- named a quiet and old-world place that does not seem to take very kiftdly•to the motor cars that whirl Ahrough. it. It has a picturesque little church, a building of red brick with a stone tower, alterdd and enlarged in recent days, bit pleasant and redolent of the past. To this church came Jeremiah Horrox as - curate in the year 1639. The little that we know of him can be easily set forth, and we would very gladly, if it were -possible, increase our knowledge. He began his brief ,carger at Toxteth, Liver- pool, in 1619. So,. at least, it would seem, but .no record of his birth hqs-been preserved. Upon May 18th, 1632, he is, recorded as having enterefl Emmanuel College, Cambridge, and seven years later, as a- young man of twenty years, he appeprs as the curate of the little church at Much Hoole. What of study and achievement lies within those years Ave know little or nothing. From his correspondence he is discovered to us as hav- ing been full of scientific knowledge and rare insight, and in the year of his settlement at Hole made the observation which has given him a lasting place in the annals or astronomical science. • -</p>
        <p>WHAT WE OWE TO HORROX.</p>
        <p>His genius has been, generally acknowledged. He was ,:the fit'st to observe the transit of Venus across the face of the sun, and his writings, though slight, have been given a permanent place. Newton, in his " Principia," bears testimony to the value of his work especially commending his lunar theory as the most ingenious yet put forward. ,Says the learned Doctor, "If I mistake not, the most accurate of all." Two years after hjs settlement at Much Hoole the zealous young scientist had passed suddenly away, leaving behind him a record of observa- tion and labour which any learned and aged scientist might envy.</p>
        <p>This young man of two-and-twenty had added to our knowledge of the physical cause of celes- tial movements, had deduced, the solar parallax, Corrected the solar diameter, and made extensive tid4l observations. No wonder the famous author; of the , " Principia " was stirred to admiration.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0237" n="art0237">
        <head>CARR HOUSE.</head>
        <p>CARR HOUSE.</p>
        <p>And now lot us pictUre., him at the place and year of his famous discovery. About half a mile from the church, is Carr House, now a ruin, where liorrox made his lodging. So, at least, says a reliable tradition, and here on November 24th, 1639.(01d style), our December 6th, he mS.de the famous observation of Venus.</p>
        <p>The room he is supposed to have used is the one on the third floor over the doorway. It is quite probable that tradition speaks correctly hero, for the sun would be low in the heavens on that December day, and the high window facing west would give the best point of vantage.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0238" n="art0238">
        <head>MODEST RECORD.</head>
        <p>A MODEST RECORD.</p>
        <p>Here is the curate's simple record of what happened on that memorable Sunday: "When the time of observation approached I retired to my apartment, and having closed the windows against the light I directed my telescope, pre- viously adjusted to a focus, through the aper- hue towards the sun and received his rays et right *angles upon a circular piece of paper. The sun's image exactly filled the circle. . j watched carefully on the 21th from sunrise -to nine o'clock, and from a Mild before, ten until noon, and at one in the afternoon; being called away in the interval by business of the highest importance, which for these ornamental pur- suits I could not with propriety neglect. But during all this time I saw nothing in th?. sun except a small and common spot, consisting, as it were, of three points at a distance from the centre towards the left, which I noticed I\m the preceding and following days. This had evi- dently nothing to do with Venus. . About fifteen minutes past three in the afternoon when I as again at liberty to continue my labours, the clouds, as if by Divine interposition, were en- tirely; dispersed, and I was, once more invited to the grateful task of repeating my obsrva-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0239" n="art0239">
        <head>GROCERS AND CIVILISATION.</head>
        <p>GROCERS AND CIVILISATION.</p>
        <p>.At the invitation of Messrs. Bovril (Limited) the, members of the Institute of Certificated Grocers dined together in the Trocadero, Sir W. R. Anson,'M.P., the president, in the chair. Sir J. t"richton Browne; proposing the toast of "The Institute or Certificated Grocers," said an interesting chapter might be written on the part played by groceries in the progress of civilisa- tion. It was certain that the introduction of many of the articles in which they dealt had marked steps in. the ascent of man, and that their distribution throughout the earth had corresponded with the spread of humanising influence. Grocers, although perhaps they did not realise it, had been potent agents in devolu- tion. Mr. Page, the American Ambassador, in inaugurating the monument to the Pilgrim Fathers at Southampton six weeks ago, said they had no forks, no tin or china dishes, and, worst of all, no tea or coffee—(laughter)—and that, he thought, accounted for a certain acerbity that characterised them. If they had had some soothing Souchong or Darjeeling Pekoe they might never have persecuted the Quakers— (laughter)—if they had had some fragrant Mocha they might have refrained from burning witches. (Renewed laughter.) Why, it was tea that was at the bottom of the War of Inde- pendence, for hostilities commenced in 1774 with the throwing of some cargoes of tea into Boston Trarbour, and it was permissible to speculate that, had the tea been of good quality, and had there been, in addition, b. n abundant supply of cocoa and of Bovril—(laughter)—George Wash- ington might have been unnecessary, and the American Colonies might not have severed their connection with the Mother Country. •</p>
        <p>THE BEST PROOF of genuine value is an increasing sale. Mothers, daughters and grand- daughters have used Steedman's Powders, which still stand as an unrivalled medicine for children. Observe the EE in Steedman. 4These powders contain no poison.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0240" n="art0240">
        <head>DRAPER'S ATTIC AS OBSERVATORY.</head>
        <p>DRAPER'S ATTIC AS OBSERVATORY.</p>
        <p>All through the daylight, of that cold Sunday .he watched in the, attic above his shop. Like Horrox, he Iliad fixed a paper disc, on which the image of the sun appeared through his telescope. It was. a cloudy day, and he. watched in vain, until suddenly, about four o'clock; a gleam of sunshine revealed the small figure of the planet erOssing the disc of his paper diagram. He was so excited, that he failed, during the first few minutes, to take an. exqct observation. When painting the fine frescoes in the Man- chester Town Hall, Ford Maddox .Brown chose Crabtree's observation as one ,of his subjects, and depicts him in the moment of Orturbation. The draper's shop, the frosted windows, the curious attitudes of his wife and bairns as they watch the uncanny spot creep across the sun's face, are admirably drawn. ",.Rapt in contem- plation," says the description, "he stood. for some time motionless, scarcely trusting his own senses through exce.ss of .joy." With similar disturbed feelings, but with greater coolness, Jeremiah Horrox was observ- ing his paper disc at Hoole. And science was the richer for his patience and lab Ours.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0241" n="art0241">
        <head>MEMORABLE SUNDAY.</head>
        <p>THE MEMORABLE SUNDAY.</p>
        <p>Standing in the village, we may picture the curate on that famous Sunday. He must be at church and fulfil his duties, the business of the highest importance" to which he makes affectionate reference in the account we have already quoted.</p>
        <p>The disc is carefully splaced and he watches till the tolling of the bell calls him away. Did he scramble the prayers that day in his haute to be ,back in his romp? And was the sermon unduly short? Did the congregation marvel at the hasty departure of their shepherd, as he set out tq stride that half-mile and never found it so long before? Honour to the two humble astronomers whose friendship was as beautiful as theinachievement was great. And all honour to their fine enthu- siasm.</p>
        <p>Possibly his conscience troubled him after- wards at the thought of the record he made that day, when Morning Prayer, a Sermon and a mile of walking were done between nine o'clock and "a little before ten."' But posterity has abundantly forgiven him.</p>
        <p>Alas, that the end Came so .soOn ! Jeremiah Horrox died suddenly on January 3rd, 1641. He had planned a yisit to his friend and was to ,bestride his horse next day and ride to Man- chester, but death intervened. His little boOk, in which the story of the obser- .vation of Venus is told, was published in Ger- many in .1662. Curiously, enough, it did not appear in England until 1859. Its title was "Venus in sole visa." •</p>
        <p>His papers Must have been carried to Ger- many, from some reason which we have been unable to discover, for his diary was discovered there about a century ago. It is from this un- assuming record that we have copied the words relating to the famous winter Sunday of 1639.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0242" n="art0242">
        <head>SOME MEMORIAL§.</head>
        <p>SOME MEMORIAL§.</p>
        <p>It is pleasant to find that the memory of Jeremiah Horrox is cherished in the village. Upon the church tower is a • clock placed to his memory, and in the chancel a stained-glass window illustrates the Transit of Venus. Upon the north wall is a tablet to his memory, which reads :</p>
        <p>In memory of one so young and yet so learned—so learned and yet so pious— this church, in which he officiated, has been enlarged and beautified.</p>
        <p>And we remember with pleasure that Jeremiah Horrox has a finer memorial than this: Cam- bridge hOnours him as one of her choice sons: Best of all; his name is carved in Westminsler Abbey. A. M.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0243" n="art0243">
        <head>FACTORIES IN ONTARIO.</head>
        <p>FACTORIES IN ONTARIO.</p>
        <p>The number Of British manufacturers and investors who have taken a trip to Ontario, wit 4 the object of acquiring opinion with reference to the establishment of factories there, has been extraordinarily large during the present year. The rapidly growing markets of the Dominion and the advantages of a "made in Canada" label are becoming more widely recognised,, as well as the fact that if the British manufacturer is to take his proper share of the increasing trade he must be on the spot. It is question- able, however, if the Majority of the manufac- turers of Great Britain fully realise how much practical experience is given to them by the various municipalities of Ontario. Most of the cities and towns of the province will extend some attraction in the way of a site free, or at nom- inal cost, fixed assessment, or even freedom from taxation for a number of years (always excepting school tax), free water, and electric power at low prices. A good index of the value of the Canadian market is the rapid influx of the American manufacturer, not only to estab- lish a market in the Dominion, but to establish factories. This does not apply only to the agri- cultural implement manufacturer, but to almost every business. These men have watched the development of the United States and taken part in that development; consequently they are not making a rash move and their decision is the result of a great deal of consideration and experience.</p>
        <p>The sudden death occurred at his residence in Rochdale-road East, Heywood, of Mr. Henry Flowker, registrar of Heywood, who was for- merly an activb Liberal worker.</p>
        <p>A gardener employed by the Huddersfield Lawn Tennis Club on Saturday found near one of the boundary walls a brown paper parcel containing 'the charred body of a newly-born child. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0244" n="art0244">
        <head>TABLE TALK.</head>
        <p>TABLE TALK.</p>
        <p>The World's Tribute of Interesling</p>
        <p>Items of News.</p>
        <p>QUAINT AND AitiUSLG.</p>
        <p>In Java, it is supposed that, if a live sheep is thrown into the water, it. will indicate the posi- tion of a drowned person by sinking near it.' A curious custom is practised in Norway, where those in .search of a droned body tow to and fro with a cock in the boat, fully expecting that the bird will crow when the boat reaches the spot where the corpse lies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0245" n="art0245">
        <head>Woollen Foundations.</head>
        <p>Woollen Foundations.</p>
        <p>At the little town of Wadebridge, Cornwall, there is a bridge of a unique character. Owing to the strength of the current, ordinary stone foundations would not hold, and .numerous devices were tried without success. Evenfa- ally bags of wood were sunk in the scam and the piles driven in, and this strange foundation has proved wonderfully firm and satisfactory.</p>
        <p>Americans and Marriage.</p>
        <p>Statistics published by the American, Equit- able Life Assurance,- Society show them are 17,004,000, unmarried persons in the United States, and that 39 of every 100 male adults are unmarried. The unmat+ied are divided thus: Men aged 20 and upwards, 8i02,000; unmarried women above 15 years, 0,000;000. Of the men 7,226,000 are ,between .20 and -44 years of age, and. 500,000 between 45 and -54 years. The inference drawn-by compilers of these statistics is that .an enormous number of men simply lack the moral fibre and courage to marry and to take a man's part in human affairs.</p>
        <p>Lirkte as Food.</p>
        <p>A teaspoonful of 'chloride of calcium, three times a- day, dissolved in water, is healthful, and nourishing, accdrding to the German professors, Emerich and Loew. Lime, if taken in slight quantities as food, they contend, is a preventiv6 and a cure of many physical ills which now afflict humanity, increasing the vitality of the organism, protecting it from harmful microbes, and curing inflammatory affections.- Instead -of throwing away the shells when one eats eggs, it would be better to eat the shells and discard the yolk and white, according to the personal view of these prOfeSsors.</p>
        <p>Sugar as an Antiseptic.</p>
        <p>It has long beep known that ;agar was a dis- infecting and preservative agent. The fact is even said to have been mentioned, by Galen. But it is rather novel to learn from "La. Revue" Magnus, iogist, that an eminent German surgeon, Dr. Georg of Munich, who is famous as a • recommends it strongly as a dressing for wounds. All saccharine substances are good, but purescane sugar or beet sugar is best. Its disinfecting and sterilising, qualities are excellent. It is not injurious to the blood, as has been wrongly believed, and is, a better preventive of putrefaction and contamination by microbes than ordinary medical antiseptics.</p>
        <p>Coats at Twopence Each.</p>
        <p>An Illinois man has patented a raincoat that can be sold for about fivepence, the cost of pro- duction amounting to merely half that amount. The garments are made. from waterproofed paper, and the modest. claim is 'inade for-them that they can be used on more than one occa- sion. The chief advantage of the novelty ap- pears to be that it can be folded up and carried in one's pocket like a newspaper, though it has no pockets itself. It can be made in all th-optimism of pro- ducers, it may be 'mentioned that patents for the coat have been granted not only in the United States, but also in this country and in Canada, Germany, France, .and Mexico.</p>
        <p>Quaint Duties.</p>
        <p>The late, Lord Dumboyne had many curious duties to perform as King's Bemembrancer, an office he held for some years. ~.One was to receive every year a. number of horse-shoe nails from the tenants of an old tenement in St. Clement Danes called the "" Forge," by which this Crown property was held. He • had also to attend the Law Courts for the pricking of the sheriffs and acknowledging linage from the holders of the Crown lands, and had to be pre- sent .at the swearing-in of .the Lord Mayor. On the King's behalf he had to demand two small faggots, a billhook, and a hatchet, in payment for an estate in Shropshire.</p>
        <p>Machine-made Houses.</p>
        <p>The project of building houses in a day or two by pouring concrete into forms so set up as to make- a mould for the entire building is now being realised, both in the United States and in Europe. These poured concrete houses can be built quickly, the average time for the construction- of each dwelling in one group of forty having been five days. In any locality' where the materials for concrete, are readily available, they cost Jess than houses built by any other substantial method of construction, says "Popular Mechanics." They are fird- proof, have no cracks. or crevices to harbour germs, are easily cleaned removing the,furni- ture and washing down with a hose, and, therefore, more sanitary than buildings con- structed with plaster walls and wooden floors.</p>
        <p>Huge Coal Trucks.</p>
        <p>For heavy railway coal traffic, notwithstand- ing the enormous developments of late years both here and abroad, eight-wheeled bogie cars have hitherto represented the maximum. An American line, the Norfolk and Western, is, however, introducing coal cars mounted on six wheeled 'bogies, in order to convey the enor- mous load of 80 English tons per vehicle. They are the largest coal cars in the World. Length over couplers is 47ft., width is 10ft. 41in., and the top rails are 10ft. 41n. above rail level. These vehicles are adapted to be unloaded at the docks by being bodily lifted and tipped, says the "Railway News." The bogies are .specially designed, largely of cast-steel parts, and with equalising arrangements to meet requirements which differ solnewhat from those associated with twelve-wheeled passenger cars.</p>
        <p>Future Man: Quaint Prediction.</p>
        <p>"I believe," says M. Archdeacon, pursuing' his. speculatioris, "that man of the day after to-morrow will completely modify himself by suppressing in his economy many encumbering and useless accessories. I believe that he will find in ether, in the solar energy, the source of all life- and, movement, the elements neces- sary to combustion and light, and that all this absurd and defective labyrinth of the intestines with the useless appendices will be radically suppressed. He will have no more lei;s. He will have .at his disposal means of transport far more practical than ridiculous Sticks that we now move with canal slowness, difficulty and clumsiness.' On 'the other hand, he will have an enormous brain and probably several aims. Woman will be radically suppressed."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0246" n="art0246">
        <head>Real Golden Wedding. •</head>
        <p>Real Golden Wedding. •</p>
        <p>In the early days of the gold rush of Cali- fornia and Australia one used to hear of extra- vagant excesses on the part of lucky miners. The same thing is taking part in Demerara just now. In a district on the Cuyuni River " fossisker ".,(men who search for gold with spade and bucket) have been finding large quan- titicc. of gold, mostly in nuggets. One of them recently, to celebrate his luck, got married, calling it a " golden wedding." Many of the guests literally blazed with ornaments of raw gold., Across the street was erected a triumphal arch; from the centre of which hung a large nugget. Another miner, who had heen mar- ried only a few years, decided that he .must celebrate a "silver wedding," and so he sent R large sum on silver ware.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0247" n="art0247">
        <head>WORLD OF LETTERS.</head>
        <p>THE WORLD OF LETTERS.</p>
        <p>cylinder rising to a height of about 'five feet above the surface of, the ground. At the same time an automatic gun fixed in the cylinder opens fire with four hundred bul- lets in any required direction.</p>
        <p>The automatic soldiers may .be brought into action by an officer at a distance of five miles from the line of defence. Further, it is pointed out that there is no danger of panic.</p>
        <p>It is claimed that a few hundred of these steel warriors would sUffice to defend a position against the most powerfut infantry attack. To stop the murderous shower of bullets the enemy would have to destroy the steel cylinders one by one, which, of course, in active warfare would be a most hazardous proceeding. Danish officers, who witnessed experiments declare that the automatic soldiers would serve admirably in certain dangerous positions, where they could very well be substituted for troops.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0248" n="art0248">
        <head>New Books.</head>
        <p>New Books.</p>
        <p>The Shorter Poems of Frederick Tennyson. London: Macmillan and Co., Ltd. ss. net.</p>
        <p>Frederick Tennyson is known to most readers only through his relationship to a more illus- trious singer. This is much to be regretted, for his poems have great and unusual merits, and it is a happy thought on the part of Mr. Charles Tennyson to issue a selection from. them. One blemish which must have attracted the attention of all who have any acquaintance with the works is a strange diffuseness, due probably to the fact that the poet did not write with an eye to publicatian. The editor of the present selec- tion has taken the bold course of Cutting out these redunilancies, believing that the action is thoroughly justified by the fact that he has thus been enabled to place before the reader a genu- inely interesting body of work. He has further provided• a brief introduction, containing . a sketch of the poet's life and some jlluminating critic*m of his writings.</p>
        <p>With the Russian Pilgrims to Jerusalem. By *Stephen Graham. London: Macmillan and Co.</p>
        <p>As most readers are aware, Mr. Graham has made a special study of the Russian people. He has travelled through the length and breadth of that vast Empire, travelling not as is the manner of the average globe-trotter in express trains,. but tramping on foot from vil- lage to village, living amOngst the .peaBants as one of themselves, speaking their language and conforMing to their habits and customs. - It is not surprising, therefore, that in his books we get .not so much geographical information, or descriptions of the large towns, as a revelation of the thoughts and feelings, the hopes and fears and desires of the Russian people. In this present book Mr. Graham describes the Russian pilgrimage to Jerusalem. He took part in it disguised as a pilgrim and shared all the privations and discomforts Of the journey by sea .and land: The account of it recalls the story of the late Sir Richard Burton's famous journey to Mecca. Though not so dangerous, it was similar in many respects, and equally successful, as his -disguise was never pene- trated. He made friends with many of . the pilgrims, and this intimacy enables him to pre- sent us with studies of Russian character of the utmost interest.</p>
        <p>This annual pilgrimage. of the Russian peasants to Jerusalem, though an event of the utmost significance, is little 'known Outside the country, or even in the great towns. 'But from every province of the Empire, from the villages in every part of that vast dominion, there come, singly or in groups, the peaSants, whose great ambition in life has been td- make the loirg journey, and at. Easter time to be at the olac-,9 where their Lord was crucified. Their faith is.; sublime in its intensity. It is equalled by the sincerity of their devotioti. The rapture alio enthusiasm, which the first sight of ,the Holy City kindled in the Crusaders, is again repro- duced• in these humble folk. Mr. Graham de scribes these seenes 'with the' utmost sympathy. He has lived so much amongst these peopie that he has probably acquired something of their outlook on life. The descriptions of the various • rites that take Place at Jerusalem at Eastertide are given not from the tour:qt's point of view, but from that of the devout pilgrim, and gain immeasurably thereby. It is a unique story for the tejling of which Mr. Graham has every requisite. Various characters stand out clearly before us'; we see the great religious motive impelling the pil- grims; the incidents of the journey, tragic, pathetic, and sometimes humorous, are bril- liantly described, and no one can read this, book without having a clearer knowledge and a keener appreciation of these simple peasants with whom lies the destiny of the great Russian, Empire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0249" n="art0249">
        <head>Literary Oleanings.</head>
        <p>Literary Oleanings.</p>
        <p>A PEOPLE WITHOUT RIGHTS.</p>
        <p>The Russian Government's traditional policy in the Caucasus, which, in spite of the present Viceroy's broad-minded ideals, is still as effec- tive as ever, consists in treating the native in- habitants of that region as a people practically without any political, and in a ineasure, with- out human rights. The occupation of the coun- try, which was effected many years ago by the most unscrupulous and brutal methods, still bears a military character, and the ruling motive of its administration is to keep the population in the Most rigid and pitiful sub- jection. The police and other officials are re- cruited from the worst elements of Russian society, including a considerable number of convicted criminals, and under them a veritable reign of terror has been instituted.—" Darkest Russia."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0250" n="art0250">
        <head>FOOTBALL IN THE PAST.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL IN THE PAST.</p>
        <p>Now that this pastime is in season it is well to lo 9.1: al its record, as at one time it was held in greardisfavour by our sovereigns. King Ed- ward 111. entirely prohibited football, as men gave up their time instead of working, drilling, or practising archery, and the narrow streets of London were the scenes of many rough tus- sles and brawls over the game.</p>
        <p>Barclay, in his Ship of Fools, fifth eclogue, 1508, writes:</p>
        <p>The stuildie plowman, lustie, strong .and bold, Overcomes the winter with driving the foote ball,</p>
        <p>Forgetting labour, and many a grievous. fall.</p>
        <p>Klng James I took care the football should be banned from the quadrangles of his resi- dences. The Boyal. Ordinance says: "From this: Court I debarrp rough and violent exer- cises:as the foot-ball, meeter for lameing, than making the users thereof." The complaint of Edward 111. as to the game is not unheard to-day, after the lapse of nearly six centuries.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0251" n="art0251">
        <head>FRUITS KNOWN TO FEW.</head>
        <p>FRUITS KNOWN TO FEW.</p>
        <p>Mexico is the fruit paradise. Fruits never heard of by the hundreds of millions who live in the temperate zones grow there in profusion, and Acapulco', on the Pacific slope, is perhaps the gqrden spot of it all. It is noted for the great quantity of indigenous varieties which grow spontaneously. No effort has been made toward extensive- cultivation by the natives, much to the surprise of visitors from the North. With the exception of some indifferent cultiva- tion of cocoanuts and bananas, everything else grows wild and in luxuriance._</p>
        <p>Of the fruits knOwn in the United States this district alone produces bananas, oranges, pine- apples, mangoes„ limes and lemons. And here are a few more of Other varieties never seen above the border line; toronjas, a species of grapefruit and aguacate, papaya, guayabo, chic°, znpote, mainey,„llama, anome, granada, caimita, cartajena, meson, wamuche, jicame, tamarind°, nanche, huicon, icacos, almendra (almond), ciruela • (plum), and undoubtedly. runny others.</p>
        <p>Hut so indifferent are the natives to their pos- sibilities that nothing is exported except the lime and its proclucts.</p>
        <p>MUM %MC ‘ll, SOLDIERS.</p>
        <p>A Danish engineer has invented an automatic soldier, which he claims will do 'away with most of the dangers of War for the army em- ploying his invention.</p>
        <p>It consists of a steel cylinder enclosed within another cylinder which is embedded in the ground. The fighting power is set in motion by means of wireless telegraphy, the inner</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0252" n="art0252">
        <head>WALLS OF JERICHO.</head>
        <p>THE WALLS OF JERICHO.</p>
        <p>In. the "Journal of the German Palestine. Society," Professor Thiersch gives interesting particulars of further excavations carried out on the site of Jericho by Professor Sellin (says the "Daily Graphie"). The chief work of the past year has been the laying bare of the great outer wall of the city, which is described as "something extraordinary, even in its ire- sent reduced stak—something majestic and overwhelming." The excavators fOund proof that this outer wall is Israelitish work.</p>
        <p>The inner wall, which is badly preserved, is the original Canaanitisfi defence, which fell to he blast of Joshua's trumpets. The outer wall Is identified as the Work of Hicl,.whose achieve- ments are described in the First Book of Kings: "In his (Ahab's) days did Hid l the Bethelite build Jericho; he laid the foundation thereof in Abirnarri, his first-born; and set up the gates thereof in his youngest son Segub." "One finds again in this work," says Pro- fessor Thiersch,. "this man of resolute charac- ter, who did not shrink even from the sacri- fice of his own flesh and blood." The fruits of Hiel's resolution are, seen in walls which modern builders could not rival; walls in which, after three thousand years, there is not a single vertical fissure.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0253" n="art0253">
        <head>PANAMA AND EARTHQUAKES.</head>
        <p>PANAMA AND EARTHQUAKES.</p>
        <p>'When. the canal is completed an earthquake shock might undo in a_few seconds all that the French and American engineers have accom- plished in as many years, although geological experts are agreed that of all parts of the Central American Cordillera, the Panama dis- trict is, less threatened with volcanic or earth- quake disturbance. .Yet there are no geological grounds for believing that the present distribu- tion of land and sea in the Caribbean area should be regarded as permanent, for the forces of upheaval and subsidence are by no means exhausted, as the recent earthquakes in Kingston, Jamaica and Cartago, .Costa Rica, have shown. Some writers appear to think that as the earth's crust cools down and be- comes more rigid these subterranean forces will gradually diminish and ultimately (lie out. But it is by no means certain that the earthquake drama of our planet will have so happy an ending. , It is all too possible that the stresses and strains of the earth's crust, which give rise to earthquakes, may increase as the earth gets cooler and the crust becomes thicker, a view supported by a homely comparison of the late Lord Kelvin, who once pointed out that when oatmeal porridge is cooling in the porridge pot, the escape of the bubbles of steam is effected with much less violence when the porridge is very hot and liquid than when it is cool and viscous. What has happened once may hap- tun again, be the effects what they, may upon the polity of man. These grand impersonal forces are beyond his control. - Empires may rise and fall, but the operations of Nature go on unceasingly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0254" n="art0254">
        <head>REMEDIES FOR SLEEPLESSNESS.</head>
        <p>REMEDIES FOR SLEEPLESSNESS.</p>
        <p>Many, articles pf food are reputed to have the virtue of curing sleeplessness. A writer in the "New York Times" says that when pressed with work and indisposed to sleep, he ate two or three small onions and found their effect magical.</p>
        <p>One or two potatoes baked or boiled in their skins are strongly recommended when the sleeplessness is due to nervous dyspepsia; spinach, asparagus, or oysters for supper have well recdgnised soporific qualities. Probably these remedies act 'chiefly by giving the stomach something to do and thus withdrawing blood from the brain.</p>
        <p>Sleeplessness arises from a great variety of causes, and one has to choose his remedy ac- cording to the nature of his own particular malady. There is the primary 'form of in- somnia, in which the brain remains active and supplied with too much blood because the vessels have lost tone. . Antemia may be the cause, or excessive tobacco smoking. The proper remedy is removal of the pause, but any- thing that will -draw the blood down into the body helps to bring about sleep. A glass of hot milk or a cup of beef tea will often do this and when the feet are cold thesp remedies are assisted by a hot-water bottle. Then there is the secondary 'insomnia, due to some cause outside the brain, such as dyspep- sia, mental worry, cough, .physical pain, &amp;c. Except in the case of dyspepsia, attention to food is of no great value in 'these forms. Overwork is another potent caus6. One often hears a person say, "I'm too tired to sleep." In this case a *arm bath, followed by some stimu- lant, generally proves quite effective. But the appropriate stimulant has to be discovered by experiment. When the brain is tired out a large cup of hot weak tea will often send one to sleep if it is taken after getting into bed. Beef tea is even. a better remedy in this case.</p>
        <p>Then there is elderberry wine of very good repute. Milk punch with hot milk, whisky, and nutmeg is a remedy both in menial and physical exhaustion, and better, in some eases, is "Bristol milk," made with hot milk and sherry. These are most effective When the sleepless person is unaccustomed to the use of stimu- lants. But they have their dangers. Mark Twain tells how, when he suffered much from insomnia, he began the. search for relief by drinking a glass of beer before going to bed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0255" n="art0255">
        <head>Mark Twain's Infallible Remedy.</head>
        <p>Mark Twain's Infallible Remedy.</p>
        <p>• "This gave a little relief for a short time. Then I exchanged my beer for a small pre- scription of whisky. This worked the desired cure "—for a time. But then he found he needed a larger dose, and so "sought another remedy and found it. Yes, sir, an infallible remedy." ' This was a child's German grammar, and he never got 'through a single, page before sleep came along. Dr. Hutchinson says he scarcely ever met with a man who could withstand, the soporific effects of bottled stout. ' AS to late meals, most people have their sleep disturbed unless a couple of hours elapse between a substantial meal and bed-time. But an empty stomach ,is worse than a full one. When the last meal is taken two and a half or three hours before going to bed, something-.--- a few 'biscuits or a sandwich, &amp;c.—should be eaten in case of sleeplessness. Elderly people should not dine late, but they particularly need something light before going to bed, such as a cup of hot mill:, a plate of soup, a glass of ale flip made with warmed ale, nutmeg, sugar, and a well-beaten egg, or some arrowroot, with a little sherry—anything light and nutritious will do. Gouty persons are • very apt to suffer from sleeplessness, and these should avoid the use of tea, fruit, much but- cher's meat, and acid wines. They may eat poultry, game and fish.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0256" n="art0256">
        <p>f CHILDREN'S CORNER. t: Conducted by 'UNCLE JACK.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER PRIZE COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>My dear Children,----</p>
        <p>I suppose you have all read the articles sent ,in for the competition and pub- lished last week. Don't you think all were par- ticularly well written? I must say the letters were very interesting. I have, heard' several people• comment favourably about them, and this is certainly, encouraging to us. Our two prize-winners received their books on Saturday morning, and I am told one got the prize before she saw .the fact in "The Examiner." It is very nice to get a little sur- prise like that now and again, isn't it? Below I am publishing the replies received from our two prize-winners. Both have written me nice little letters of thanks.</p>
        <p>I am now wondering what"sball be the sub- ject for our next competition. I know! We shall have some poetry. Just think what is your favourite•poem, write it down, and then s&amp;id it in to. Uncle 'Jack, "Examiner" Mice, BewseST-street, Warrington, on or before 18th October. Just select short poems, as I would like to publish all, and if they are long that cl,nnot be done._</p>
        <p>Below I am giving you another selection of interesting matter. .</p>
        <p>Your loving UNCLE JACK.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0257" n="art0257">
        <head>LETTERS FROM PRIZE-WINNERS.</head>
        <p>LETTERS FROM PRIZE-WINNERS.</p>
        <p>12, St. Mary-street, Latchford, October 1, 1913. Dear Uncle Jack,—Thank you very much for\ sending me such a lovely book. I have'read some of it, apit,have found it very interesting and ainusing.'"Vhave shown it to all my play- mates and relatiops, and they all have admired it very Much.—Your loving niece,</p>
        <p>VEtIA JONES'.</p>
        <p>29, Folly-lane, Warrington, • 30th September, 1913. Dear Uncle Jack,—l am very pleased to say that I won your competition. I think it is a very exciting book, and full of adventures, because I have read two chapters. I hope some of your other nephews will win your next com- petition.—Your loving nephew,</p>
        <p>G. COOKSON.</p>
        <p>Boy's Kindness.</p>
        <p>Fleeently his mother noticed that a small six- year-old boy at bedtime every Bight laid his little boots together upon the Sides instead of setting them upright. "Please tell me why you always place your boots in that way," she said:</p>
        <p>"Why." answered the little boy, "it's because they must be tired walking so much all .day. I lay them sideways so they can rest.".</p>
        <p>Married in Haste.</p>
        <p>Little Dot (aged the).: Mamma, Dick and I got married this morning."</p>
        <p>Mamma: "You did, did you? Who per- formed the ceremony;?"</p>
        <p>"I don't know what you're talking about." "Well, how did you pretend that '*You were married?".</p>
        <p>."Oh, why, I got my dishes an' set the table, an' then we both sat down, an' he said there wasn't a thing fit to eat, an' I said he was a brute, an' he went out an' slammed the door."</p>
        <p>Conundrums.</p>
        <p>In what respect does a linen draper's domestic architecture differ from that of other trades- people?—The yards are kept in the shop. Why is the executioner like a brief cohe- spondent?—Because he drops a line. Why is a grocer's shop like an almanac?— Because it contains dates.</p>
        <p>Who swalluived a Street, and where ? Henrietta Street, near Cavendish Square.</p>
        <p>Willie and Sunday.</p>
        <p>".010. Willie, you must put your drum away. This is Sunday." "But, mother, I Was goin' to play some sacred music." '</p>
        <p>Down by the Sea'. It is fun to be here when the tide comes in, With a rush, and a roar, and a terrible din; It is fun to jump 'through the biggest billow, And lie at rest on a foamy pillow, And to float on the top of the sea.</p>
        <p>It is fun to be here when the tide goes out, What fun it is to wander about. And hunt for stones with a lucky ring. ' And delicate seaweed, and everything We 'can find oh the shore of the sea! -</p>
        <p>How He Blew.</p>
        <p>In one of the great squares of St. Petersburg' stands a magnificent column 150 ft. in height, erected to commemorate the reign of the Czar Alexander 1., the ally and afterwards the rival • of Napoleon. On the occasion of a public cele- • bration the then reigning. Czar wished to have the great shaft illuminated, and round lamps of an enormous size were ordered from a leading glass manufactory, After two or three eiperiments the workmen discovered to their great consternation that it seemed impossible- to blow bulbs so large by the force of human breath. The blowers blew till they‘vere utterly exhausted, but the bulbs re- mained far below the required size. A handsome prize was offered to the first suc- cessful blower, and the men renewed their efforts, but to no purpose. At last a big fellow, shaped like a barrel, stepped forward and quietly remarked that he was sure he could do the trick. The crowd laughed good hurnouredly, but the man merely said, "1 want to rinse my mouth; it is dry." They gave him a cup of water. He rinsed his mouth, taking'plenty of time, and then applied his lips to the tube. Slowly and steadily the ball of hollow glass grew. Soon it reached the dimensions of its nearest rival. Then it became bigger, bigger, until it approached the required size. Then it attained it. Then it passed it., " Stop I Stop !" cried the crowd. "It's get- ting too big." And the forenlan added, "How did you do it?" "Where's my money?" said the man by way of reply. When he felt the roubles in his palm an ex- pression of genial satisfaction overspread his rough features. "Why, it's easy !" said he. And then he ex- plained how he had retained some of the water In his mouth, how he had gradually blown it Into the molten ball, and how the expanding steam had instantly come to his assistance.</p>
        <p>.L.."'-','.-....''';',-...1:':',.. _.,,i1 -•--,,;..,. '.-',;:.4,.,,-' -`..: .</p>
        <p>Meetings of members of the General Union of Textile Workers were held at Huddersfield and Slaithwaite to consider the refusal of the em- ployers to grant an advance of wages, and reso- lutions were passed which were relegated to a sub-committee to deal with. •</p>
        <p>Miss Kemp, sister of Lord Rochdale, has definitely declined to stand as a candidate at the forthcoming municipal elections at Roch- dale.</p>
