      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0269" n="art0269">
        <head>GLOVES.</head>
        <p>GLOVES.</p>
        <p>The goddess Venus, we are told, was respon- sible for the introduction of the glove, an ar- ticle of attire now o essential that few, even :n the most humble walks of life, care to do without it. According to the old myth. Venus who was in love with young Adonis, accompanied him to the chase, and, when passing through a thicket, had the misfor- tune to scratch her hand on a thorn bush; when she sniveller injury "Venus, vexed with pain Lest any hurt should touch her hand again, Bade all at once her unclad Graces sew A leathern shelter for her hand of snow.</p>
        <p>The lowly Graces, draped in floating hair, No longer left their own hands free and bare, But bound and covered them as Venus did, And now the glove's true origin is hid No longer, thus it is,—fair girls alone Wore on their hands what now is common</p>
        <p>grown. Then came the Emperor and all his court And then at last the folks of every sort."</p>
        <p>ANCIENT WRITERS AND GLOVES.</p>
        <p>Many of the ancient writers make frequent mention of gloves. Thus Homer, writing of Laertes working in his garden, makes him with "gloves on his 'hands to secure them from the thorns," while Arnenoeus mentions them in quite a different capacity in that part of his work wherein he tells of a notori- ous glutton, who invariably placed gloves on his hands before sitting down to meat that he might the more easily handle the joints while hot and so devour more than the rest of the company. From each authorities we gather that the ancients were acquainted with the use of gloves, although their Use was not com- mon. In fact many philosophers and teach- ers were moved to wrath against the custom and Musonuis at the close of the first century of the Christian Era in a burning I denounce. meat of the vicious corruption of the age says, "It is shameful that persons in perfect health should clothe their hands and feet with soft and hairy covering." Their comfort and convenience, howeveri scon made their use so universal, that in the ninth century the Council of Aix considered it expedient to pass a regulation concerning thia part of dress and ordered that the monks should onl7 wear gloves made of sheep akin. In addition to fulfilling their primary duty of warming. and protecting the hands, gloves have in many ages been the symbol of love,' !honour, loyalty or defiance, and their use in some form or other was included in many old ceremonies, such as the ceremony of investi- ture, the conferring of dignities, or bestowal of lands. Bishops were given possession of their 'sees by the bestowal of a glove, and in fact it was so important a part of the episcop- al rule, that all dignitaries of the Church un- der • the rank of bishop were prevented from wearing either gloves, rings, or sandals, these being for the exclusive adornment of bishops, who invariably 'wore them richly adorned with Precious stones.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0270" n="art0270">
        <head>LAWN TENNIS.</head>
        <p>LAWN TENNIS.</p>
        <p>EMMANUEL CLUB'S SEASON.</p>
        <p>The Emmanuel Club's most successful season terminated on Saturday, when the finals for the season's tournaments were played off under ideal conditions.</p>
        <p>Twenty members enjoyed a delightful tea, and Miss E. Jones, of Stockton Heath, iraciously distributed the prizes. The recipients were : Gentlemen's singles, Mr. Hayes; ladies' singles, Miss S. Fish; ladies' and gentlemen's doubles, Mrs. Jaye and Mr. Hayes.</p>
      </div>
