      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0273" n="art0273">
        <head>PROFESSOR GREEN'S THEORY.</head>
        <p>PROFESSOR GREEN'S THEORY.</p>
        <p>What Alderman Bennett termed an earnest, stimulating and helpful address, was given by Professar J. A. Green, of the Uriiversity of She • field, and chairman of the Central Handworkers' Association, in the Cairo-street Schools on Sat- urday morning. The occasion of the lecture was the opening meeting of the Warrington branch of the Educational Handworkers' Asso- ciation. There was a large attendance and the Rev. Canon Morley Stevenson (president) pre- sided. Mr. F. Dainteth is the honorary treasurer, and Miss Bertha Pogh is the honorary secretary. At the outset the CHAIRMAN apologised for the unavoidable absence of Councillor, Henshall. Continuing, he said that the Warrington Com- mittee of the Association were evidently deter- mined that they would give the work of the association for the new season a good semi-off for they had been fortunate to secure Professor Green to give the first lecture. He was known to many of them in various capacities, and he was an expert in everything that had to do with education, science and psychology. He (the Chairman) was sure they would have an interesting and instructive lecture. (Hear, hear.) Professor GREEN said that although he had avoided handwork in the title of his lecture, if he circled round and about it they would under- stand. Continuing with his lecture, he said that as a rule one found that sooner or later schools began to reflect the dominant thought of the time. They always hung a little behind— necessarily so—schools were very conservative institutions; they hung behind the leaders of thought, and by thought he meant the scientific and philosophical especially. Take the edu- cation that characterised .the 13th century. That education was generally directed in pre- paring earthly beings for the life that was to come. That was the dominant note of the time, and it was also the dominant note of school and college of the period.</p>
        <p>To Make Life Happy._</p>
        <p>They might compare that with the view dur- ing the Renaissance, especially as it took form in Italy. The glory and importance of this life were then emphasised. All education went to improve the life of the moment. All that went to make this life joyous and happy was worth while. That idea was ,to turn out lads who would distinguish themselves in society and in the courts—amongst the learned of the day. Such was the dominant thought of the world, and the school followed. Luther harked back to the 13th century. He urged the necessity for compulsory and universal education. Every parent must then be compelled to send his child to school, not in order that be might be happier in this world, but that lie might learn to spell his religion. They might compare those three historical periods in the history of education with the 20th century. What was the dominant thought now P What was leading scientific conception ?surely it was the biological conception; surely it was the biologists who were trying more than ever to make our social and educational thought. It was from the biological standpoint that school reform was corning. It was a very human thought that the schools should hang behind the prevailing philosophy of the time, and to-day the prevailing thought was biological. It was from that standpoint that he was going to talk of the neglect of motive in our schools. Psychology was only a branch of biology, and the psychologist who was not penetrated with biological principles was apt to see things in false proportions. First of all they wanted purposeful action, and the second central doctrine of the biologist was the capacity to learn by experience. The last doctrine was the capacity of adaptation. The mind was the supreme organ for adapting the individual to his environment, and in adapting the environment to the individual. That was a line of doctrine we in these days were apt to forget. Man was not merely a creature of circumstance; he was the master of circumstance. Man made his own environment. Man and environment were corollary terms. Man made his own world and his environment. That was what the biologist emphasised. Man was the maker and commander of circumstance. "I am the master of my thoughts, I am the captain of my soul." Those three doctrines constituted the central teaching of the biologist as applied to psychology. How far had those doctrines touched school attendance? He was simply going to raise problems, and if they could go out of that room thinking that things were not yet quite satisfactory, that although they had got their professional certificates, they had not yet solved the problem of education, it would give them cause for reflection. There was much to learn yet.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0274" n="art0274">
        <head>Revolution.</head>
        <p>A Revolution.</p>
        <p>There was going to be something like a revo- lution in school procedure. If he had made them attempt to solve the problems he would have done his duty. The procedure of the schools generally was governed by the time table andY formal. arrange- ments. He did not think there was anything which was more a waste of time than the ordinary reading lesson. If anything was learned of that lesson it was "How hateful these reading books are." They were books that had no lesson for the scholars. They simply had it because it was on the time table. There might be only one or two in the class who would like to read, but the others had to have it whether they wanted it or not. To-day we were sacri- ficing the present in the supposed interests of the future, which the biologist said was a bad thing; it was bad psychology; contrary to he laws of the ordinary development of the in d. They did not train minds in the vacuum or by artifice, but by thinking towards a practical end. Thought for thought's sake was a tremendous blunder. They insisted upon thinking in the schools. They, severed thinking from doing, and by doing that they were really killing effec- tive thinking. The difficulties of the evening schools came very largely, because they had not got hold of the fundamental biological truth. It was the same in the primary schools, They ,got formality in handwork. They had many schools manned by extremely zealous teachers, who had somehow not got the right meanings. They asked a boy learning manual work what he was doing, and he said making a perch. 'That boy did not make a perch because he wanted one—he had no cage—but because it was rule five. (Laughter.) All school reform that was worth anything would come about through biologising the school. They shou:zl think of life as life itself.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0275" n="art0275">
        <head>Monstrous Fetters.</head>
        <p>Monstrous Fetters.</p>
        <p>If they could get the teachers and the class. rooms penetrated with that doctrine—that the, child was living—and have everything purpose- ful, being taught by experience and capable of adapting circumstances to the boy himself and adapting himself to circumstance, they would alter the curriculum of the school. The school furniture would be changed, They had imposed fetters on themselves by 'the magnificence of 'their fittings. They \Were too costly to be scrapped, but they were proving unbiologicaL Even the time tables were often great, mon- strous fetters for education. The problems would have to be solved. There would be no revolution, but he hoped things would move more rapidly. They should stimulate thinking. The old things were done away with and they should now put the lads to do things which had a meaning for them at the time. He would rather have a man acting without thinking than a man who thought without acting. In the future furniture sometimes, time tables often, and prejudices always, all would have to' go before the wove of the new ideas. School would then be a different thing. Collective discipline in the old sense would be gone, and militance amongst the teachers would die out, and the atmosphere of the school would be living. Every- one would be doing anid learning from doing. There would be more individualism, and the boy would be able to have his thoughts fully developed in whichever direction they lay. The ehildren would all be taught to improve life; they were going to bring motive into the school. (Applause.) Questions were then invited.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0276" n="art0276">
        <head>Series of Questions.</head>
        <p>A Series of Questions.</p>
        <p>Mrs. STANSFIELD said that they, as' practical teachers, knew that until the schools were halvecl il that Profcssor Grocn ha l said' wa3</p>
        <p>(Continued in next column.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0277" n="art0277">
        <head>W. Phillips (committee). Front row (sitting): T. Knight., S. Carter, A. Bramhall (captain), E. Sanders and F. tlorrochs</head>
        <p>W. Phillips (committee). Front row (sitting): T. Knight., S. Carter, A. Bramhall (captain), E. Sanders and F. tlorrochs</p>
        <p>The above club have had a very successful season, having won the League Championship for the first time. They were second in 1902 and again last year. They have played 10 League matches, winning 8, and losing 2, scoring 634 runs for the loss of 84 wickets, against their opponents 374 runs for the loss of 98 wickets. They have also played three friendly matches, winning two and losing one, scoring 177 runs against 140. The second eleven have also had a successful season, winning seven and losing three matches. They scored 612 runs, against their opponents 333. _ .</p>
        <p>The following are the averages for the First Eleven League matches</p>
