      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0278" n="art0278">
        <head>QUEBEC'S SURPRISING SURPLUS.</head>
        <p>QUEBEC'S SURPRISING SURPLUS.</p>
        <p>There is great rejoicing in the :Province of Quebec at the excellence of the financial out- look disclosed by the annual statement recently made by the Hon. Mr. Mackenzie, the Provincial Treasurer.</p>
        <p>"Times have changed for the better in our good Province of Quebec" (les temps sont bien changOs dans notre bonne Province de Quebec), writes one enthusiastic supporter of the Gouin Administration, in commenting upon the sub- stantial surplus that has been announced. There is good ground for rejoicing in view of the fact that, in spite of acute, money stringency, the balance of the loan of 1882, amounting to over two million dollars, has been redeemed, while the ordinary revenue receipts have exceeded ordinary and extraordinary expenditure by 428,752 dollars. In pursuance of the great scheme of road improvement already authorised by the Quebec Legislature, and involving an expendi- ture of ten million dollars, a loan of £400.000 has been raised. It is interesting to note that the biggest item in the list of expenditure for the past year is "Public Instruction, including 'high Schools.'" This speaks volumes for the future of progressive Quebec.</p>
        <p>OAT PLANT WITH TWO HUNDRED STEMS. A wonderful oat plant was exhibited at a recent meeting of the New Zealand Farmers' Union. None of the 40 persons present had ever before seen an oat plant with 210 strong stems growing from it. The owner said two seasons ago he sowed some peas, and when hoeing them he noticed an oat plant which had stooled out 105 stems. This he endeavoured to save till the grain was ripe, but a neighbour's cow broke in and he only saved one grain. This single, grain he planted in his garden, and it produced the plant referred to, with no less than 210 stems. It grew very strongly, and the corns formed well, but being away when the grain ripened, Mr. Higgins was only able to save 12 oats, and these he intends to plant and watch this season, in the hope of raising a new variety of a very prolific kind.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0279" n="art0279">
        <head>No Appetite fore, Food.</head>
        <p>No Appetite fore, Food.</p>
        <p>When you have no appetite for food, or when food is distasteful to you, no matter how attractive or wholesome it may be, then you can take it for granted that something is amiss with your digestion. What you need is a good tonic for the stomach, liver, and bowels. And there is nothing better than Mother Seigel's Syrup. It soon sharpens up the appetite, and regulates the entire digestive system. Then you relish your food, and derive the proper amount of nourishment from, it, without Which you caianot hope to enjoy really; good health. After a few doses of Mother Seigel's Syrap you can eat heartily, and eat what is prepared for you without fear of pains to follow. Mother Seigel's Syrup really does tone up and strengthen the digestive organs and sharpens the appetite.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0280" n="art0280">
        <head>SCHOOL REVOLUTlON—continued.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL REVOLUTlON—continued.</p>
        <p>impossible. Could Professor Green tell them when the time was likely to come? Professor GREEN said he could not tell them when the time would come, but urged them to get hold of the general idea of motive, and, as far as they could, let it penetrate into their works.</p>
        <p>Mr. MANN said that in Warrington and round about things were moving rapidly. They were getting better quickly. Professor GREEN said he was rather socialistic- ally inclined so far as the schools were con- cerned. They should get them all Working to- gether in one common cause. An individual was a meaningless atom. Alderman BENNErr proposed a vote of thanks to the lecturer. The marvel ,to him was that in view of the splendid privileges they enjoyed they were so different from the town they would like to be. One section of the Warrington population were keenly interested in the sub- ject, as he could see by the large attendance. The teachers could mould the future, and the sooner they got the right ideals they would be on the right road to making the future genera- tion of Warrington the hotter. His hope was in the ladies and gentlemen he saw before him. (Applause.) Enthusiastic Teachers.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. MOORE MURRAY (Director of Education) seconded. They had -an enthusiastic set of teachers, whose desire it was to do their best for the children. They shad brought the classes in the schools down from 80 members to 50, and that was a step in the right direction. They must realise that they were making some little progress. (Hear, heat.) He had no fear for the revolution that was coming inside the schools; 'de feared the revo- lution that was coming from outside—from the expert who knew everything about school and how everything should be carried- on and had never' been inside a school since he was 13 years of age- (Laughter and applause.) They wanted the "all round" boy to-day, the one who could think, not the particularised boy. (Ap- plause.) • The vote was heartily supported. On the proposition of Mr. MANN. seconded by Mr. C. JACKSON MUTES, the Chairman was alsrs warmly thanked.</p>
      </div>
