        <head>RURAL COUNCIL "SCENE."</head>
        <p>RURAL COUNCIL "SCENE."</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell and Councillor Knowles.</p>
        <p>A QUESTIONABLE BROOK.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Warrington Rural District Council, held in the Bewsey Chambers, Warring- ton,- on Wednesday, under the chairmanship of councillor G. Mather, Mr. Sutton, the Clerk, reported upon the rate required for general pur- poses. In view, he said, of probable special ex- penditure, it would be well to keep to the rate of sd. in the X for this purpose during the next half-Year'</p>
        <p>The Chairman: We have, same time ago, taken that rate down from 6d. to sd. Councillor Robertson.: It cannot be made less? The Clerk said it depended upon the work they were going to do under the head of general ex- penditure. Calls to meet special expenditure would have to be made in some parishes, but it was awkward to fix that special rate before they knew what the cost was to be during the half- year.</p>
        <p>In regard to the complaints about the odours of Burtonwood Brook, a letter was read from the County Medical Officer at Preston, regretting that owing to pressure of other work, and the fact that he was starting for his holidays, the matter had not received attention, but he had requested the County Sanitary Officer to visit the brook and report. Dr. Sephton- reported that the death-rate for the past month had been 11.7. There had been two deaths from diarrhcca at Burtonwood, and one case of scarlet fever and one of diphtheria had. been reported. Councillor Rey. A. M. Mitchell: Yes, we have a very serious death-rate in the district. In regard to the Burtonwood Brook, I must pro- test against the very casual way in which this matter is treated by the Preston people. Dr. Sephton : The County Doctor has been much engaged with his tuberculosis cases and— Councillor Mitchell: And his holidays. Chil- dren may die off like flies in Burtonwood. The Chairman: Flies don't die?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0284" n="art0284">
        <head>Lively Episode.</head>
        <p>A Lively Episode.</p>
        <p>Councillor Knowles. I do not' think you should make remarks like that about the County Doctor.</p>
        <p>Councillor Mitchell: It's time something was said.</p>
        <p>Councillor Knowles: Yes; you like to get it in the newspapers so that you might make a big show.</p>
        <p>Councillor Mitchell: No, no; I'm not talking to get in the papers. • Councillor Knowles: What j say is The doctor's excuse for not attending is that he was going for a holiday, and that is a very ordinary reason for non-attendance. Councillor Mitchell: That's no reason at all. Councillor Knowles: A hard-worked man like the County Medical Officer must have a holiday. I do not think it is gentlemanly, courteous, nor plite fair for any member to make use of expres- sions like those made by Mr. Mitchell. Councillor Mitchell: I repudiate that. It is a very wrong thing that the parish should be left in the state it is when the children are being practically killed off.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0285" n="art0285">
        <head>From One Man Only.</head>
        <p>From One Man Only.</p>
        <p>Councillor Knowles: I hope it goes forth from this meeting that such remarks as have been made have not come from the Council, and I hope that the public will note the remarks have come from one man in the Council and that they have not endorsed anything about" failing" on the part of the doctor.</p>
        <p>Councillor Mitchell : I used the word "casual." The Chairman: We have, had about enough of this.</p>
        <p>Councillor Mitchell: It's a shame. I think it's scandalous.</p>
        <p>Sankey Brook Troubles.</p>
        <p>- - Mr. Kerfoot, the Sanitary Inspector, presented a further report on the alleged pollution of Sankey Brook, and said that certain local authorities were sending sewage down the brook. Councillor. Furness said it was a serious mat- ter and they must take immediate steps to keep the brook sweet and clean. Councillor Longton said he understood that St. Helens and Newton Councils were sending sewage down the brook twelve out of every 24 hours.</p>
        <p>After some further discussion it was left to a sub-committee to make a full investigation.</p>
        <p>HOCKEY. _ _- STOCKTON HEATH 2nd v. BEBINGTON 2nd. At Spital. Stockton Heath were the first to score and made two. Kilner (Bebington) re- duced the lead. Ker equalised from a pass by Howard, and Howard put Bebington ahead before half-time. Stockton Heath managed to equalise. Good defence kept further scoring down, but Ker put Bebington ahead. Result: Bebington 2nd, four goals; Stockton Heath 2nd, three.</p>
      </div>
