      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0291" n="art0291">
        <head>LATCHFORD v. STOCKPORT.</head>
        <p>LATCHFORD v. STOCKPORT.</p>
        <p>Latchford entertained Stockport and were de- feated by the narrow margin of three goals to two, after a very strenuous game. The score fairly well represents the run of the play. Col- ton opened the scoring for Latchford after a quarter of an hour, but the lead was not main- tained for long, as the visitors scored twice before the interval. In the second half each side scored once, the Latchford point being a nice shot from Pearson. The Stockport halves played well, with Hull most prominent at left- half. Goodwin also did well at centre-forward. For Latchford. Bruhl kept a good goal. The second eleven were at Stockport, and after a keen and rather vigorous game were beaten by 2-1, Stockport scoring within half a minute of time. Furness, at centre-forward, scored Latch- ford's goal, and Wilkinson, at centre-half, did much towards keeping the home forwards in check.</p>
        <p>The following teams will turn out to-day : First Eleven v. Weaste, at Latchford.—J. Bruhl; W. Fletcher, C. Billington; P. W. Isher- wood, J. B. Wilkinson, A. Knight; It A. Dale, J. E. Pearson, W. 11. Minards, A. G: Marsden, H. It. Colton. Bully-off 3.30. Second Eleven, at Bowdon.—T. Dixon; W. T. Eccles, C. Newbold; A. E. Bradshaw. F. C. New- bold, T. Banks; A. 13. Starkey, V. H. Fletcher, W. S. Furness. J. J. McCawley, D. Holt. Train leaves Central Station 1.35.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0292" n="art0292">
        <head>RIFLE SHOOTING.</head>
        <p>RIFLE SHOOTING.</p>
        <p>THE APPLETON CLUB.</p>
        <p>The final competitions took place on Thursday and Saturday. The weather was ull that could be desired, and an attendance far in excess of the general average thoroughly enjoyed the Saturday afternoon's sport. Iu all, 111 entries were made.</p>
        <p>The Honorary Secretary, Mr. A. Shepherd, was ably assisted by the Honorary Match Secretary, Mr. C. P. Day, and the members of the Range Committee. The friendly rivalry extended until darkness closed; in fact, all the entries could not be completed. The results are as follows:</p>
        <p>Ten Shots, Deliberate, at 25 Yards. Score. H'cap. Total F. Tole (Ist prize) H. Baines (2nd prize) 9B 2 100 F. C. Bennion (3rd) 9B 2 100 Ten Shots, Deliberate, at 50 Yards. F. Black (Ist prize) 95 6 101 J. Hartley (2nd prize) 97 3 100 C. Shepley (3rd prize) 93 6 99 Ten Shots, Deliberate, at 100 Yards. F. Black (Ist prize) 96 6 102 P. Harrison (2nd prize) 94 7 101 H. Baines (3rd prize) 97 2 99 Ten Shots, Rapid (in 90 sec.) at 25 Yards. KC. Bennion (Ist prize) 94 2 96 J. Towers (2nd prize) 92 3 95 F. Tole (3rd prize) 92 3 95</p>
        <p>A Service Rifle Competition, which had run through the season, was also completed. The prizes for this competition had been kindly given by Mr. Arthur Down. The conditions were: Seven shots, and a sighting shot, at 100 yards; no entrance fee; no handicap; the best four out of five attempts to count. Possible score for each attempt, 70 : J. Allardice (let prize) P. C. Bennimi (2nd prize and Done- gall badge) C. P. Day (3rd prize) ........ 250</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0293" n="art0293">
        <head>CROSS COUNTRY RUNNING.</head>
        <p>CROSS COUNTRY RUNNING.</p>
        <p>To-day the local season opens with an ordin- ary run from the, pavilion at three o'clock, and all members are particularly requested to turn up, with club costume, in time for a photograph to be taken before the turn out. Beginners and novices (a novice is one who has never won a prize in open competition for running) should meet at the Pavilion on Monday and Tuesday next at 7.45 p.m.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON ATHLETIC CLUB. This club held their annual meeting at the pavilion on Tuesday, when a good muster of members accorded their hearty thanks to the retiring Honorary Secretary, Mr. Charles Berry, for his splendid work in connection with the erection of the pavilion. , The balance sheet showed that the club's efforts to provide com- petition for the lads locally had not proved financially successful, but the Treasurer has still ..t5 towards carrying on the good work. The officers were elected as follow :—President, Sir Gilbert Greenall ; honorary secretary and treasurer, Mr. R. Bennett; assistant honorary secretary, Mr. W. W. Taylor; captain, Mr. James Parsonage; vice-captains, Messrs. W. Bolton and H. Holbrook; committee, Messrs. G. H. 'Ander- ton, A. Bailie, T. Battersby, C. Berry, C. Dag- nall, H. K. Jepson, Fred MumbY, W. Trice, J. Tavlin- and T. Whalley. The opening run was fixed for October nth. .The retiring Secretary was the recipient Of a handsome umbrella as a mark of his co-workers' appreciation of Mr. Berry's untiring efforts on the club's; behalf.</p>
        <p>GOLF. MERSEY CLUB.</p>
        <p>The final round for the Captain's prize, which took place on Saturday by medal play, resulted in a win for Mr. J. Whitehouse. The prize is offered by Mr. C. E. Parker. The details are : Gross. H'cap. Net. Mr. I. Whitehouse .... 97 25 72 Mr. F. Gidman - Mr. W. S. Haddock 96 16 80 Mr. j. Bowen ~ 94 12 82 Mr. F. Collinge No return. Mr. E. Foster No return.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0294" n="art0294">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>The Palgate United team are at home to-day to 'Higher Walton. The following will repre- sent Padgate:—Norke; Warburton and Hold- croft ; Hankinson, Wadsworth and Williams; James Houghton, Darbysnire, Jack Houghton, J. Rigby and Ilardnum Kick off 3.30.</p>
        <p>Grappenhall V. Widnes St. Marie's.</p>
        <p>Grappenhall play at home this afternoon, Widnes St. Marie's being the visitors. The ground is situated on the Knutsford-road, close to the Dog and Dart Hotel. The team .is :—R. Shakeshaft ; A. E. Evans and W. Massey; W. Cameron, T. Woodward and LLeigh ; F. Wood- cock, F. Booth, J. Richards, F. Parr and J. P6ters. Reserves: J. Thomas, T. Fairhurst and H. Parr.</p>
        <p>hi'. HELENS AND DISTRICT THURSDAY LEAGUE. Warrington Co-operative v. Prescot.</p>
        <p>An, interesting game took place between the above teams on the King and Queen ground, Padgale-lane, on Thursday afternoon. In the first half Rutter "drew first blood" for the " Co-opers," but before the interval was reached Prescot had made the scores level.</p>
        <p>The second half proved very interesting, and Presca soon added two more goals, but Taylor reduced the lead with a clever shot, and a good exhibition of football ended: Preseot, three goals; Warrington Co-operative, two goals.</p>
      </div>
