        <head>, – . ~.,""k•A•i,•., • •,-, \ ...4 . #,•,-,•.',.• .$4:,,_, ....,4.5.'-. : 1,,•;&lt;:--,,Z4 •-,41, .r. I.is . ~...., i-e4.',..:.•- . ' A . ''''. , .:. f'..---... – I'll, .17- Z – ~ ~, t5.:..,,i ..i.xx tii.,:1i.).\,..7' ' '•7. , – la '3 4 . . . . – – :.", ~,, :L. . . – : .r. y .. ' ,':, ... ~. . j ..:-.. • F I .... t..,..., _ ~.,... ._. _ ..„?.,.....,„,..„, ~.,..: A"c . ;2.0.- AV • • ii', 4., ~....! --,....,14-.= • , ft... 1 . ,:: , • . , -.. f . – – '4; . ' ...g,l • ... __ .</head>
        <p>, - . ~.,""k•A•i,•., • •,-, \ ...4 . #,•,-,•.',.• .$4:,,_, ....,4.5.'-. : 1,,•;&lt;:--,,Z4 •-,41, .r. I.is . ~...., i-e4.',..:.•- . ' A . ''''. , .:. f'..---... - I'll, .17- Z - ~ ~, t5.:..,,i ..i.xx tii.,:1i.).\,..7' ' '•7. , - la '3 4 . . . . - - :.", ~,, :L. . . - : .r. y .. ' ,':, ... ~. . j ..:-.. • F I .... t..,..., _ ~.,... ._. _ ..„?.,.....,„,..„, ~.,..: A"c . ;2.0.- AV • • ii', 4., ~....! --,....,14-.= • , ft- ... 1 . ,:: , • . , -.. f . - - '4; . ' ...g,l • ... __ .</p>
        <p>pack, and made one or two 'attempts to get through, but could not break down the defence. After a spell of midfield play, A. Brown got nicely away, and from a clever round of pass- ing, in which he, Dainteth and Waywell took part, the latter was sent over at the corner for a fine try. Critchley, however, found the angle too difficult to convert. Swinton once more had a turn on the attack, and some clever pass- ing movements were indulged in, but their " threes " could not break down 1. Brown's defence.</p>
        <p>Warrington Forward's Leg Broken.</p>
        <p>-- - - As the result of some very keen tackling in this half, Clark, who was making his first ap- pearance this season, was brought heavily to the ground, and had to receive assistance from the ambulance men. Judging that a leg had been broken they called far a doctor, and Dr. Bennett, who was on the stand, superintended the removal of the man from the ground. This was indeed unfortunate, Clark having been playing a good game. Warrington now resumed with only five for- wards, but Swinton failed to take advantage of this, and the first half ended in Warrington leading by one try (three points) to Swinton, nil. _</p>
        <p>Much-Improved Second Half. On resuming, play was much better in the second half, Warrington doing most of the at- tacking. Dickenson and Baker made much progress on the left wing, .and on cme occasion Dickenson intercepted 'a Swinton pass very smartly, and had exceedingly hard lines in not scoring. Douglas and A. Brown were next seen to advantage, and Waywell eluded a host' of opponents, but Flynn brought about a fine tackle. Exchange, kicking was then indulged in between the backs, after , which Blundell made a superb dash, and was almost over, being brought down a few inches from the line. Swinton hereabouts made one or two spasmodic' bursts, and on one occasion a score seemed cer- tain, but faulty passing spoiled a chance. Dickenson took a pass very smartly, and made much progress, Dainteth being sent over for a nice try. Critchley was not successful with the goal kick. Waywell was the next scorer, he getting over at the corner. Critchley this time landed a beautiful goal from a very difficult angle. From a forward rush, Critchley got over, but was unsuccessful with the shot at goal. A fine run by Baker on the right ended in that player planting the ball behind the posts, for Dainteth to add the extra two points. Soon after this the whistle blew, and the game ended: Warrington "A," two goals five tries 1(19 points) ; Swinton "Ar nil.</p>
        <p>" Georgie " at His Best. Of the players, J. Brown played well at full- back. In the threequarter line, Geoi•ge Dicken- son was a tower of strength, and gave one the idea that he is still a force to be reckoned with. Some of his dashes and the way he intercepted many a Swinton pass reminded one of the old days when he partnered J. Fish.. He proved himself worthy of , a place in the team to do duty against St. Helens on the occasion of his benefit match this afternoon. 'Baker, who part- nered Dickenson, also made many a fine run. He is a very " brainy" player, and a few games with an experienced centre like Dicken- son ought to develop him into a class wing man, and no doubt we shall soon see him amongst' first-class company. On the other wing, Dain- teth and Waywell played well together, and did many smart things, the latter showing a rare turn of speed. A. Brown and Douglas played well at half-back, both players "feeding" their threequarters well, and making many good openings. In the forward line there was not much to choose between the players,. Harmer, Critchley and Blundell being most prominent. For Swinton, Flynn was very safe at full- back, and was little- inferior 'to his rival, his fielding and laking being very good. Their threequarters at times got nicely away, but lacked cohesion, and were not so smart as their opponents. The half-backs could not make I much, headway against Brown and Douglas, and were well watched. The forwards all did well.</p>
        <p>St. Helens "A" v. Warrington "A." Warrington "A" have a stiff task orf hand at St. Helens, but they should win with the -fol- lowing:— J. Brown; Waywell, C. Brown, Tranter and Baker; A. Brown and Ruddick; Naylor, Green, Boardman, Blundell, Critchley and Bevan.</p>
        <p>New Player Signed On. The Warrington 1...0111111i11ee 011 WelllleSllay nignt completed tne transier of C. brown, tile Leigh player, anu lie is due to, turn OUL With the -A" team to-day. He received Ins training wan the lAuncorn ‘v into star and went later to Leign. He is reported to be a speedy man at threeqUarter.</p>
        <p>young Jolley, who greatly enhanced an already nne reputation. He is a full-back of many parts, and is sure to attain high honours. He takes a ball finely, can kick well, and tackles with accuracy."—" Daily Dispatch." "1 single out Jolley, because, to my mind, he was the most notable player on the field. His feeding, kicking, tackling and running were the features of the game."—" Centre," in the "Daily News."</p>
        <p>"Behind these players was a distinctly clever full-back in young Jolley, of Warrington, whose tackling and kicking and judgment generally served Lancashire in very good stead."—" Man- cunian," in the "Manchester Courier." "'Jolley was a pillar of strength, and dealt with every attack in a masterly fashion."— "Daily Post," Liverpool. These tributes confirm everything I' have hitherto said about our popular back, and the other experts thus confound those who were apt to allege that I was over-rating the capabili- ties of Jolley. Ruddick played a good game for Cumber- land, but as that side was chiefly on the defen- sive, he did not get an opportunity to bring off much of his dashing spurts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0302" n="art0302">
        <head>Offside and Obstruction Leads to the Downfall of Warrington at Swinton.</head>
        <p>Offside and Obstruction Leads to the Downfall of Warrington at Swinton.</p>
        <p>THE THREEQUARTERS PROVE A FAILURE.</p>
        <p>w.ll was a nasty defeat at Swinton.. The sting tttus found in the fact that the Swintonians 0f4,11Y won on the strength of penalty points. t-si ofde and obstruction were the chief features Ivll' fairly fast game, and the Swinton men V.e even more at fault in this respect than or,ringion, but the home team got full share 'Lle free kick's, made the most of them, and won by nine points to two, six points Of were acembulated through penalty goals. 'lllls/the fair result on the play would have a win for Swinton by one try, and that bc°,111(1- More justly have marked the difference t,;:ween the two sides. Warrington were .cer- ,.;',* not so smart as Swinton, but they were c 19% deficient to the extent the 'score would in- Every time Warrington got a ,penalty it was at an awkward position. The home 41ta always found the free kick right in front v, goal, and the worst (loud kicker possible r.alild have been able to add the points. In this 4Pect the home Side found plenty of luck.</p>
        <p>By 44 Scrum."</p>
        <p>worked hard, although most of his efforts did not produce, results. Cox accomplished some fine work, and if anything he was the most in evidence amongst the front rankers in the Warrington team.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0303" n="art0303">
        <head>"SIMPLY PLAYED ROTTEN."</head>
        <p>"SIMPLY PLAYED ROTTEN."</p>
        <p>L There was plenty of incident in the game, I,ll,tit waS more frequently supplied by the, side. Warrington did not play the game tileY have been following since the start of the t?,48°11. They seemed to 'be disorganised, and aie,.Y gave even a worse display than they did it Huddersfield, where from the start they had GhoPeless task. Had they repeated the Hud- esfield play the result at Swinton 'on r Satur- li''Y would have been different. Swinton did tic't Prove a brilliant team by any means; War- • wtigton proved, in comparison, on the play, a °rse team. 1:11eY simply played rotten from' back to was the terge way an enthusiastic sup- ter of the Warrington team expressed his vv;lr of the game. With that I am sure every f:ttirrington supporter present at Swinton will eee• Warrington could do everything but right thins; they were out of form in every artmene— back, threequarters, half-backs forwards had a (147 off, and the score tells at happened 15ecause of that.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0304" n="art0304">
        <head>PROMISE NOT FULFILLED.</head>
        <p>PROMISE NOT FULFILLED.</p>
        <p>titlllo visitors gave promise when the game (4rted, but it was never fulfilled. Buddick to„saed up a nice passage, and he handed on tLnavis. Howard had a clear field if he had a path near the touch, but for some 415;4450n he ran into the ruck of is opponents, flcit Weight of numbers pulled him down. Then co I."°wed a eries of free kicks for off-side.. a eeided Weakness with the "Lions," but War- di-gton reaped no advantage. Once more Bud- got on the run, and he stuc,k to the, leather. 111 trying to swerve past an opponent he slipped 1:11 the bare, greasy pitch that is called a Ifoot- ground at Swinton, and another. Chance Was lost. , , If anything, the " Wirepullers" were at this gtage the better side, and they looked for the oment, certain wifiners Just when a Chance :irne, Ruddick was pulled up for off-side; and Opportunity went. Bradshaw next covered bad slip by Jolley, and Renwick mulled ir chances. Bradshaw put in a fine run, and lot,t\lS got the ball, only to hang on to it tog. (ligig and spoil the chance. Moores and Leah 14 lied through Warrington's defence. and eor t(iii es scored .a simple try. -Anderson's kick ed, and so the game stood at half-time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0305" n="art0305">
        <head>ONLY WARRINGTON SCORE.</head>
        <p>THE ONLY WARRINGTON SCORE.</p>
        <p>r ;ere was some better work done by War- Liiit'tgaut)ll at the opening of the second portion, 'tree times was it marred by persistent off-, re-: an obstruction On the part of the "Lions" tifghard. At last this became so glaring that ki-,.referee had to allow Warrington a free 11`:N, and Jolley kickeg a pretty goal. Tranter juttl,:le a slip that nearly let in Moores, and Jolley- Ito"' managed. to get the ball away. Renwick w;illiged next in' the best run of the day. He A 11° support and was pulled down. • `trlother spurt by the visitors was marred by 4311sPictious bit of off-side by Swinton. The gg,e,ree blew his whistle, Wharton protested .is`tinist a free kick, but the 'referee rightly per- in his decision, whereat the -spectators loudly. Jolley's kick just missed the ;I right. Fickle was the crowd. For • Moment or so .they booed" every- the ling toy, referee did. Then he gave SWin- 10,;",,a free "kick, as Nicholas was .off-side, and wo'ulY did they cheer him. Three penalty goals rl"(ire scored by Swinton in rapid succession, fget aided by these the "Lions" won an unsatis- Dui °rY game by three goals one try (nine 'rlis) to one goal (two points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0306" n="art0306">
        <head>WEAK ALL ROITND.</head>
        <p>WEAK ALL ROITND.</p>
        <p>• itallie greasy ground and the slippery ball may had something to do with the upsetting thr„ e Narrington game, but it was not entirely tin,"' Which led to the defeat. Warrington were lele y out of form. Jolley, who has been in- -1,0 lib id in past games, was often fallible. He Teali'" not bring off his usual tricks, and one m°,ll was the wet .ground, wliich cut up, 'lltr4ii;"Y• He often slipped and. fell at um for-fehal moment. Still, after making allowance Ittcli at, he was not up ,to past form, and made Dre-'°io slips in this match than lie did in the four v 'ph ns ones combined. kori e threequarter line was weak in compari- lvvi,, to the Swinton quartette. Only • once or lArGe did they embark in combined work, and 'N-811,,they did so the play was good, but such jl-14(ics-it'dic efforts do not bring results. Davis seine bad slips, and seemed to forget that r;gs a wing man. Tranter appeared scarcely dition, but he will doubtless regain his 114111,7'i, Ilenwick effected a few brilliant bits of work. He was not nearly up to last form. Bradshaw seemed to have lost sPeed, and rarely got away.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0307" n="art0307">
        <head>bISAPPOINTING HALF-BACKS.</head>
        <p>bISAPPOINTING HALF-BACKS.</p>
        <p>The 45 half-back play by the Warringhton duo verY disappointing. It was niuc below tile Cl of past games. Ruddick did not work ttrti,serinns as well as usual, but he made -rlas for his defects with some tricky, run Nvit Witii Jolley, so with Ruddick, the ground tveS,, against his style of .play. His tricky siv.es Were marred by side-slips. On at tsilree occasions he would have scored had NiQh-a; sliPPed and fell just at the vital moment. r\Ded-4„as, especially in the second half, desei. "angerous liking for getting off-side. Ile played fairly well, and was the 'C'64lbSi starting the threequarters on the. few Thelled efforts they made. 'P lay was indifferent. The War- Wer'ehad the weight, yet' the " Lions " 81011.. aNe Jo' push them ,about. They were also 4h 4i 'bre,:tki rig Up. Clare never got a chance C'Whis,,speed. 'Chester, the county man,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0308" n="art0308">
        <head>NOT A SUCCESS.</head>
        <p>NOT A SUCCESS.</p>
        <p>Writing candidly, I do not think the re- organised threequarter line was a succesS. Ren- wick and Tranter were palpably 'not in form, and were not an improvement upon what Dick- enson and Brookes were in the earlier matches. Dickenson, on such a tricky surface as that met with at Swinton, would have been a most ser- viceable man, and he would have\ been better able to tackle ,men like Moores and Leah than either Tranter or Renwick. , Had Warrington laid themselves out to bottle up the speedy Swinton pair, a different com- plexion might have been put on the game. As it was, they gave both Leah and Moores too much grve, while there was also far too much off-side. It was evident that the threequarters will require a lot more practice before they can become really effective, but the making of a ;good quartette is there. How long We may have to wait for its development is, however, another question.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0309" n="art0309">
        <head>N.U. LEAGUE TABLE.</head>
        <p>THE N.U. LEAGUE TABLE.</p>
        <p>RESULTS TO DATE. Per- For. Apt. cen-</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. G. T. Pts. G. T. Pte. tags. Hull 5 6 0 0 16 26 110 2 3 13 100.00 Huddersfield 5 5 0 :0 90 35 145 8 1 37 100.00 Rochdale H 4 3 1 0 8 14 58 4 4 20 75.00 Salford 4 a 1 o 2 10 46 5 3 19 15.00 Swinton 4 3 1 0 9 9 45 5 3 19 15.00 Hull K. R 4 3 1 01114 64 8 531 15.J0 Wigan 4 3 1 0 10 17 71 7 1 35 15.09 Barrow ... 4 3 1 0 51349 ' 3 T2l 15.00 Oldham 4 3 1 0 19 11 59 5 10 40 15.00 Widnes 4 3 1 0 6 835 4 8 39 15.00 Huns!et 4 3 1 0 9 8 49 4 11 41 15.C.0 Bramley 8. 2106115 5 6 23 66.66 Warrington 5 3 9 0 8 8 40 10 12 56 60.00 Leeds 4 '.2 9 0 8 13 55 6 13 51 50.00 Wakefield T,4 2 9 0 T 5 29 6 5 27 50.80 St. Helens 4 2 9 0 8 943 14 955 60.00 Keighley 31904214 10 9 41 33.83 Dewsbury 4 1 3 0 6 1331010 50 25.00 Bradford 6 1 4 0 9 5 33 13 16 14 20.00 Halifax 4 0 4 0 4 6 96 11 11 ,55 00.00 Leigh 4 0 4 0 T 3 23 19 15 69 00.00 Runcorn 4 0 4 0 6 9 18 11 11 55 00.00 York 4 0 4 0 10 li 35 96 95110 00.00 Batle) 4 0 4 0 8 2 19 12 39 63 moo Broughton it 4 0 4 0 1 0 2 11 9 49 00.00</p>
        <p>GEORGE DICKENSON'S BENEFIT. Some of the Contributors.</p>
        <p>To-day will be a memorable one in the history of the Warrington Club. It is the date of the benefit allocated to George Dickenson, a player who has done much for the club and much for the fostening of the N.U. game generally. Every follower of_the pastime, should make certain of witnessing the game, and I hope the veteran will get a ,burnper benefit: Donations are.coming in nicely, and I hope by to-day these will reach a creditable sum. George deserves a great recognition. Many of the N.U. League clubs have contri- buted, and at the moment of writing Hudders- field, Halifax, Hull, Swinton, Wigan, Widnes, had sent in donations. In every case tile secre- taries wrote in congratulatory terms of the Warrington Man.. They spoke of his gentle- manly, conduct on the .field, what he had done in the past to encourage the game, and wishing the benefit a great success.</p>
        <p>Mayor-Elect's Donatios. • councillor Peter Peacock, who is Mayor- elect of Warrington, writes the following inter- esting letter to the Secretary.:-H . , Dear Mr: Bradshaw.—.</p>
        <p>Will you kindly accept the enclosed cheque for £1 Is. as a subscription to the G. Dickenson Benefit Fund. Ttie beneflciare has been known to me for many years, and I, sin- cerely hope that on Saturday he may receive the " bumper " gate that he so richly deserves. 'Yours faithfully, • (Signed) PETEK PEACOCK.</p>
        <p>• COuncillor Peacock the Mayor-elect, and Mrs. Peacock; the future Mayoress,, will be present at the match. Mr. W. Bainbridge, acting on behalf of Sir Gilbert Greenall, has also forwarded a cheque for '.21 is.</p>
        <p>DRASTIC CHANGES IN WARRINGTON Dainteth Comes Into Half-back _Line.</p>
        <p>_ The Warrington Committee, on Wednesday evening, gave careful consideration to the com- position of the Warrington team„in \view of their disappointing performance at Swinton on Saturday, and also in regard to the stiff match they have to face in the game with St. Helens at Wilderspool to-day, and some drastic changes have been made.</p>
        <p>Ruddick, Tranter, Green and Howard Davis are absentees. The first three are lking given a short spell with the ,"A" team. H. Davis has not been enjoying good health and he is being given a rek. Thus Brookes and Dickenson re-appear in the first string at threequarter, and Harmer, who has been accomplishing useful work all this season with the reserves, is given a place in the front rank. .</p>
        <p>Dainteth is to Partner Nicholas at half. This is his first appearance with the seniors this season, but' he was in that Position in the cup final last season. Dainteth has played well with the reserves during the past month. The absence of Ruddick has come as a surprise, but his absence is likely to be temporary. The teams will line" up as follows</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON. Jolley. Brookes, Dickenson, Renwick, Bradshaw. • Nicholas, Dainteth. Thomas, Chester, Cox, Clare, Harmer, Fearniey</p>
        <p>Prescott, Tellyn, Myers, Shallcross, Heaton, Pope. Trenwith, Cheshire. Roberts, Fla,naghan, Barker, West. Turtill. ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON "A" AGAIN VICTORIOUS.</p>
        <p>The "Lions" Second String Well reaten. Our Combination team had Swinton "A" as their opponents at Wilderspool, and the game was played in a summer atmosphere. , The first half was rather tame from It spectators' point of view, and neither side could claim much advantage: Swinton kicked off, and Warrington were soon on the aggressive, Dick- enson leading the' way with one of his char- acteristic bursts, but Flynn cleared. Swinton were much smarter in getting the ball from the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0310" n="art0310">
        <head>Northern Union Items.</head>
        <p>Northern Union Items.</p>
        <p>Clarke, the arrington -A" man, who had his leg broken in tile match with Swinton -A" on Saturday., was last night reported. to be making favourable progress in the Warrington Infirm- ary, where tie was taken after the accident.</p>
        <p>For the Lancashire Senior Cup tie beiween. Warrington and Barrow, at Wilderspool on the 16th October, the referee will be Mr. J.C. Lum- ley, Leeds, and the touch-judges, Messrs. J. 11. Lawton and R. Heath. Rylands' Recreation have been drawn against Swinley Hornets in the second round 'of the Lancashire junior Cup. Playing at home on November Ist, they should win this round.</p>
        <p>JOLLEY, THE POPULAR HERO. Warrington Men in County Match.</p>
        <p>The two Warringtonians in the match between Lancashire and Cumberland at the Broughton Rangers' ground, did excellently, and showed that the committee had acted right in making choice of Jolley for the full-back position,. and Chester for the pack. Chester- wt 4 an unknown quantity to most of the spectators, but he played so sound a game that they were loud in their apprAa- tion. Chester undoubtedly did the best he ,has done sO far this season. It must have been particularly satisfactory to Warrington people that he should figure amongst the scorers. In hict, the best try of the match was that scored by Chester, and it was particularly appropriate that it was his clubmate, Jolley, who let him away. A critic, describes the stir- ring incident thus : "One of the best tries of the match came just before the interval when Jolley made a fine run from full-back and handM to Chester close on the line. Naturally the, Warrington forward was pleased with his clubmate's performance, arid after scoring the try walked across and filipok hands with him."</p>
        <p>The game resulted in a win for Lancashire by three goals six tries (24 points) to one try (three points). The fourth try was got by Ches- ter. Jolley kicked the three goals. FULL-BACK TRANSFORMATION.</p>
        <p>If Jolley lost his art on Saturda3( on the Swin- ton pitch, he refound it on tii.c; adjoining field at the Cliff on Monday. He played a remark- able, game, and one which was in vivid con- trast to ,kliat of Saturday. He tackled, kicked, fed and ran in a manner which stamped him as the best full-back in Lancashire, and one of the very best in the N.U. League. His excel- lent display on Monday made ample amends for the disappointing exhibition on Saturday.. The critics could not praise him too •.much, and certainly his • display merited all that has been written. The following Are a few views of other expert writers on the game about our little, but alert, back</p>
        <p>"'One of the greatest figures on the field was</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0311" n="art0311">
        <head>PICKS OUT OF TIIE SCRUM.</head>
        <p>PICKS OUT OF TIIE SCRUM.</p>
        <p>Pithy Points About the Games.</p>
        <p>"Mr. Penalty" was the scorer. He was prominent at Swinton. . Four goals he accounted for. Twice that number was missed. It was A funny game all through. The defeat was nasty to Warrington. Swinton could not get much credit. Their win was not a brilliant one. The heat was against good Play- A greasy ground upset calculations. Warrington suffered, most in this respect Swinton are born mud-larkers.</p>
        <p>* 0 0 * 0 * What was wrong with Jolley? He seemed out of, form. His tricks proved futile. When he swerved he 'fell. It wt § due to the slippery ground. He did not: gather the ball well. This was due to a Wet leather. At times he was brilliant. At others he was not up to pitch. Warrington supporters were not so jo/ly. They were disappointed, that they were. And came home a saddened throng.</p>
        <p>* * * * * Can anyone give the reason Why the heavy Warrington forwards Allowed themselves to be pushed About by the lightweight " Lions " ? Were the " Lions " stronger than they looked? Certainly they were not cubs. They know the forward game. Clare got no chance to run. Rarely did he get the ball at all. Chester did well at Swinton. He, however, was scarcely tp to The form expected of a county man.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0312" n="art0312">
        <p>•0* . •</p>
        <p>Oh, for one hour of Dickenson! He would have been the man to check "Lions'" dashing threequarters. he was dropped. Tith,te va "sLa p New Zealand did not shine. Renwick was brisk at times. ; Then again he was indifferent. The Colonial needs ,some more training, Perhaps then he will,liven up. Tranter also wants tuning up. At concert pitch he will be dangerous • In the Warrington threequarter line.</p>
        <p>• • * -* * • • r; Nicholas fumbled ',a go,od deal. It is a bad habit to get into Is that of getting so much off-side. Ruddick did not shine as usual. The ground upset the "little 'un." His side steps produced side slips. Side slips produced' nasty falls. Then the "Lions" got the ball. It was hard lines on Ruddick That he should slip at the moments When he seemed on the eve of scoring. Dancing tricks are no good on a mud pitch</p>
        <p>* *. * * * - Do not forget the big event. To-day is George Dickenson's benefit. Roll up in your thousands. Roll in your cash in piles. Give the veteran a bumper "gate." George was in good form in the "A" team He delighted with his dashing runs. The team will give good value For the money the public will spend. If they beat St. Helens it.will be The crowning event of what is A truly memorable day for Wilderspool.</p>
        <p>* * * The Mayor-elect has set a good example. He has "donated" one guinea to Diekenson's benefit fund.</p>
        <p>Now, then, let hundreds follow the future Mayor's lead. Huddersfield and other clubs have sent a donation. The secretaries have also written very nice letters. They write of George as a very gentlemanly player. ~ They also say he has.done much to foster the N.U. game.</p>
        <p>All they say everybody will agree is perfectly true. George, let us have a try to-day ; one of the grand old sort. What a cheer the veteran will get when he appears on the pitch. Si. Helens are hot stuff, but • Warrington ought to be warmer. . . If they put all in they should pull off a fine victory.</p>
        <p>* .</p>
        <p>* *</p>
        <p>* *</p>
        <p>It was very hard luck on Clark To have his leg broken early on. And this his first appearance this year. He was promising a great game When the 'misfortune overtook him. ' J. Brown was great at back. Baker plays with his head as well as his feet. Dickenson thinks Baker a coming man. The veteran is a good judge of talent. Dainteth accomplished some dainty work. He is another coming man for first team honours.</p>
        <p>• a</p>
        <p>The young "Lions' were wired in. Warrington secured a fine victory over the "A's." Waywell displays his fine speed. He is a dashing man when he gets the chance. Douglas is a tricky half-back. A. Brown plays well with him. In fact, the pair get the ball away wcll. And when Warilell gets the ball he finishes their work. Will our second men win at St. Helens' There is a good sporting chance that tiny will do so.</p>
        <p>* * * * * '* Jolley was the hero of the county match. He played a remarkably fine game. Oh, what a difference from that at -Swinton. The critics sing his praise unanimously. They have made a very 'important discovery We have known it a long time now. Will Jolley be picked against Yorkshire? He is about the only man for the position. A fine try that Chester, wasn't it? -It was very satisfactory to see you score. Let us have More similar exhibitions. The more tries you get the merrier.</p>
        <p>rlylands' match and tern, page 8.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0313" n="art0313">
        <head>'EAMS FOR TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>'EAMS FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>The following players have been selected to represent the Adult School in their League match against Crosfield's at Folly-lane : Welsby ; Hunter and Garnett; Leigh, Ridyard and Clarke.; A N. .Other or F. Jackson, Webb, Dykes, RoweAmd-Banner. The club have a few opqp-dateswd. woutd -like to-arrange with any clubs having pilvate grounds.</p>
        <p>MATCHES FOR TO-DAY. The Cheshire Senior Cup Tie.</p>
        <p>Latchford v. Brooklands, at Sale; B. Phillips. Warrington and District League. _ _</p>
        <p>• Daresbury v. Great Sankey; J. Smith.</p>
        <p>Crosfleld Rec. v. Adult School; S. Breeze. Fairfield v. St. BarnabA.; R. Rule.</p>
        <p>Bank Quay Recs. v. Orford St. John's; J. T. Hadkiss.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0314" n="art0314">
        <head>REFEREE'S EXAMINATIONS.</head>
        <p>REFEREE'S EXAMINATIONS.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Rule, Sherlock, Rushton and Pittam having passed their examinations, have ,been initiated into the order of the knights of the whistle for the Warrington and District League.</p>
        <p>Grappenhall St. Wilfred's v. Excelsior. Grappenhall opened the season on Saturday with a mattch with Warrington Excelsior at Bewsey. Good combination' on the part of the Grappenhall forwards ended in Cameron scor- ing. Eventually the home team pressed and equalised through the medium of Crozier. , It - was not long before the game player gave Ex- celsior the lead. The leaders attacked, .and Crozier again scored. Resuming -play, the Saints monopolised the attack, and scored twice, Woodcock and Thomas being the contributors. The Excelsior rallied, but could not penetrate the Grappenhall defence. This well-contested game ended in a draw of three goals each, Richards, Evans and Massey playing well for Grappenhall, and Turner, Williams and Stringer for the Excel- sior.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0315" n="art0315">
        <head>SHOTS AT GOAL.</head>
        <p>SHOTS AT GOAL.</p>
        <p>During September there has been six matches played in the Warrington and Dis- ilia League (First Division) and 20 goals scored. Ditchtield heads the list with four. Dykes comes second with three. Booth and Leach have scored two each. While' eight other players have scored once. Garnet has. scored against his own side. In the Second Division 55 goals have. 'been scored. Meachin, of the John's, comes first with 10. Leah, of Excelsior, second, with seven. Shuttleworth, of Barnabas', has got five. Bradshaw, of Bank Quay Becs., has got four. Sudell, Hankinson and H. Walker, three each. Tickle, A. Hurries, Hunt and CI. Hughes, 'two each. Quite a lot of them have got singles. Meachin has done the "hat trick" in every match. Bradshaw, Shuttleworth vitt Leah have also obliged in the same way. W. Hunter, better known as "Billy," is ren- dering splendid service tio the Adult School team.</p>
        <p>Houghton was in fine trim for the Sankey boys. • They have not got any sharpshooters now. They have missed Foster in this direction. Saville, the Lymm Amateur's goalkeeper, at Stockton Heath kicked the ball clean over the houses last Saturday.</p>
        <p>The Spectatots shouted "lost ball," thinking bout cricket.</p>
        <p>The ball ended in breaking a window. Magill and Denny were the best half-backs on the field.</p>
        <p>Jones and Lockhart also played-well together. The Soldiers made a good staii in the League- in gathering a point on opponents ground. Great Sankey came near their first defeat against Adult Schpol. Daresbury are going to ,try their luck on Saturday. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0316" n="art0316">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0317" n="art0317">
        <p>A Remarkable Cup-tie Ends in a Draw at Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>A GOALKEEPER'S DESTRUCTIVE KICK.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0318" n="art0318">
        <head>By "Linesman."</head>
        <p>By "Linesman."</p>
        <p>Saturday was a blank day for our local clubs in the North Cheshire League, the date being set apart for the playing of the first round of the Cheshire Amateur Cup ties, and the Latch- ford Club Were fortunate for unfortunate, as</p>
        <p>the case may be in obtaining a bye lor the first round, while Crosfield's 'Recreation Club are not taking part in the competition, so' arrangement was made for a friendly fixture to be played on th:!. Thelwall-lane ground. The match served a good purpose to both clubs in giving trials to several junior players, with a view to strengthening their ranks. The game was very interesting and .keenly played by both teams, and, as the ,score' indicates, the .teams were very evenly Matched. Both scored three times before half-time, and it was only a few minutes before the close that the home players secured the winning goal, Birchall, the visitors' goalkeeper, giving an easy chance to his opponents by carrying the ball above the prescribed allowance in his own urea. Webb, Smith, Downs and Robinson, for the home team, and Foster, Whitfield, Birchall and Worr rall, for the visitors, all played well, the latter player showing up very prominently at CenTle- half.</p>
        <p>WHAT lIAPPENED AT STOCKTON HEATH. The. most attractive fixture in the district was the Cheshire 'Amateur Cup tie between Stock- ton Heath and Lymm Amateurs, which was played on thu ground of the former club, and a very interesting and pleasant game was wit- nessed in which the visitors did very well. The home team were successful in scoring twice in the first twenty minutes through Lockhart and Jones. The visitors replied through Smith before the interval, and Leigh equalised during the second portion. Saville, the visitors' cus- todian, did the lion's share, his keeping being a treat, and it was largely due to him that the Amateurs will be able to take part in a replay, which will take -place at Lymm on Saturday, the 11th inst.</p>
        <p>Since Saturday I have had reports given to me of an incident which took place at the close of the match between Stockton Heath and Lymni Amateurs. At present, it is not advis- able for me to comment further upon them, but it is the -duty of the committee to try and put a stop to such occurrences taking place, or they will soon have the reputation of the club damaged beyond repair.</p>
        <p>NOTES ON WARRINGTON LEAGUE GAMES. The fixttire between the Fairfield and Fair- field United teams produced a keen match, as neither club had previously won a match., but had, met With defeat in similar manner to each other. The result was in favour of the Fair- field club by three goals to one. St. Barnabas' entertained the Excelsior team 'at Lovely-lane, where an evenly-contested game was played. , During the first half only one goal was scared, but during the second moiety the Excelsiors, so:red qn. two more. occasions, and won by three goals 'to nil: ' The -Orford 'Barracks team made the journey to Weston, Where they met with a good recep- tion from the villagers, and, after a keen and fast game', the Points were divided. It was no mean performance on the Soldiers' part to make 'a draw, for the Weston team are hard to beat on their own ground. Their dis- play was very good. There is no doubt that when the Soldiers have had a little more practice together we shall hear of them doing better still. Another very interesting match was that played in Victoria Park between the Adult School and Great Sankey. As the home club have been showing very good form lately by taking a point from the Weaver team the pre- , vious \ week, the Sankey boys knew they Could not afford to treat them too lightly.</p>
        <p>Although theh team is not up to last, year's standard, they have several good players, in- eluding Houghton, Ditehfield and Kelley, while Hunter, Dykes and Welsby did very well for the School team.</p>
        <p>Woodward; Magill,- Denny and Marsh4:Lloyd, Lockhart, Jones, Taylor and Parr. Lynn Amateurs: Saville; Harrison and Hudson; Blane, Booth and R. G. Smith; Har- rison, Lees, Hewitt, Richardson and Leigh. Referee, W. Barnett, Altrincham. The replay will probably take place at Lymm on the 11th inst.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0319" n="art0319">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. First Division. Weston v. Orford Barracks.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. First Division. Weston v. Orford Barracks.</p>
        <p>This match was played at Weston, and the following players lined out under Referee C. S. Essenhigh—Weston Nancollis ; Wood and Barnes, Askey, Talbot and Hope; Molyneux, Smith, Flanagan, Conway and Hulse. Orford Barracks: Moodie; Burley and Loman ; Press- land, Ross and Stevens; Campbell, Hicks, For- shaw, Roberts and Trafford. The visitors won the toss, Flanagan kicked off, and the Soldiers, getting possession, were the first to make an attack, the ball going to Hicks on the extreme right. He beat the backs, but his final shot went over' the bar. After his the home team took up tfie attack on the right, and Molyneux centring finely, Flanagan beat, Moodie and opened the score. Even play I followed Tor a while, until the Soldiers gradu- ally got into the danger zone of the libme quar- ters, when one of the defending players handled ne ball in the penalty area, and from the re- sultant kick the Soldiers missed their chance of drawing level. At half-time tile home team were leading by one goal to nil. On resuming it was soon evident the visit- :ors were lanxious to avoid defeat, and the game was fast and exeiting, the supporters calling on their favourites to play up. The stamina of the visitors was in their favour, and the visiting forwards all took part in a fine passing move- ment. One of thethome backs mis-kicked at the critical moment, and Hicks seized trie oppor- tunity and equalised tile score. From the kick ! off both teams put more energy into their efforts, and each goal was 'visited, but to no purpose. Nancollis and Moodie respectively cleared their charge Under heavy pressure, and the- game concluded in a draw: Weston, one goal; Orford Barracks, one goal.</p>
        <p>Adult School v. Great Sankey.</p>
        <p>This match was played at Victoria Park, Latchford, and 'owing to the good form shown by the Adults the previous week, and. the repu- tation which the Sankey team have gained, a pod crowd Of spectators assembled to witness the match. The teams were as follows :—Adult School: • Welsby, Hunter and Garnett; Leigh, Bidyard, and Clarke; Williams, Dykes, Webb, Bowe and lianner. Great Sankey: Coughoon ; ' Glover and Kelley; Glover, Stephens and Payne; Darlington, Ditchfield, Booth, Poulton and Houghton. The visitors won the toss, and Wei% kicked off for the School. The ball was taken on the left -into the "visitors' quarters, and play was transferred to the other end by a good return by Glover, and the visitors, combining very well together, kept the home defenders very busy for some time. Welsby distinguished himself by some good saves, and gaining relief from the goal kick, Dykes and Webb tried hard to get through the visitors' defence. Kelley, however, was playing a fine game at back. ['he Sankey boys now had a turn at the other end, where Houghton put in several fine 'centres, whieh the inside men allowed' to go a- begging. Near the interval Ditchfield got pos- sessiOn, and beat Welsby with a fast low shot, and at half-time Sankey were .leading by one goal to nil. ' The second half opened with a spirited attack !py the home team, who were now playing a far better game, and the visitors' backs had a very warm time, a shot from Webb going just wide Of the post. Another raid 'was made on the Sankey goal. Banner and Rowe, on the left, led the . attack, and from their centre, Dykes made the scores equal. From the centre the visitors played hard to gain the lead, a fine shot from Darlington hitting the cross-bar. Hunter was now very conspicuous, clearing his lines again and again. The visitors continued to have the better of the game, and Welsby dashed out ig his goal and took the ball from the feet of Booth, when, to all appearances, he was beaten. The visitors kept up the attack to the finish, but they failed to improve upon the , chances which came their way, and the game Colitchided in a draw, the score being: Adult School, one goal; Great Sankey, one goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0320" n="art0320">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. Second Division. Fairfield v. Fairfield United.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. Second Division. Fairfield v. Fairfield United.</p>
        <p>These teams met at Orford-road in the above competition, and owing. to the rivalry betwev the clubs, both teams were well supportea. The matbh was considered a " Derby " fixture by both teams. The following players turned out :—Fairfleld United P. Gleave ; Rowlands and Woodhouse; Dooley', Bebbington and Easters; Shelmardine, Duckworth, Challinor, Sinicock and Appleton. Fairfield: Dainteth : Byram and Jackson; Webb, Titley and Hol- lows; Broadhurst, Taylor, Hughes, Osborne and Bates. Referee, Mr. J. Kite. The United won the toss and Hughes started the game for Fairfield. The opening exchanges were very tame, neither side gaining much ad- vantage, and play was confined to midfield. From a breakaway the Fairfield lads rushed on to the United goal, and Osborne opened the score, after 35 minutes' play, and no further scoring took place up to the interval. On resuming, the United were the first to become aggressive, and was awarded a penalty against Byram. Woodhouse, taking the kick, equalised. The game Was now hotly contested for a while, when the Fairfield lads got the upper hand, and gong were added by Hughes and Taylor. No further score was made, and the game ended:. Fairfield, three goals; Fair- field United, one goal.</p>
        <p>St. Barnabas' V. Excelsior.</p>
        <p>This match was played at Lovely-lane,- and the following players lined up under Referee Barnabas': Shaw;. Forster and Spruce; Appleton, Long and Shuttleworth ; He..lsby, Vernon, Bevan, Brown and Ryan. Ex- celsior : Rigby; Warham and ; Spencer, Hunt and Walker; Leah, Pritchard, Holmes, Hughes and Cox. The visitors won tbe toss. The game became very fast, both teams playing very well. From a fine combined effort on the part of the Excelsior forwards, Walker, with a low drive, sent the ball past Shaw, and, thus opened the scoring. Shortly after Cox repeated the ,feat, and Excelsior led at half-time by two goals to nil. On resuming, the game was not quite so fast, and. \for a while,play was not very interesting. The Excelsior did most of the pressing, and gained a number of corners. Walker put his</p>
        <p>side further ahead, and when the final whistle blew, Excelsior ran out winners by three goals to none.</p>
        <p>FRIENDLY ENCOUNTERS.</p>
        <p>Latchford v. Crosfield's.</p>
        <p>Owing to the former club having a bye in the cup ties, and the latter not taking part in the Cup Competition, a friendly game was arranged and played on the Thelwall-lane ground, neither team being fully represented: The fol- lowing players turned out :—Latchford: Kite; Robinson and Povey ; Downs, Webb and Car- ter; Smith, Siddall, W. Taylor, Stubbs and Taylor. Crosfield's: Birchall ; Cletho and Dol- phin; Walsh, Worrall and Bretherton ; Bell- field, Whitfield, Munn, Foster and Beddall. From the kick-off play was very even, and the players entered into the game in 'the heartiest</p>
        <p>fashion. The home team was the first to be- come dangerous, and Smith opened the score, and shortly after Taylor gained a second point. he visitors, not to be outdone, made an attack on the home goal, and Foster scored. Play continued very even up to half-time, when the score was three goals each.</p>
        <p>On resuming, each goal was attacked in turn, Worrall, of Crosfield's, Playing a fine game at centre-half. As the game proceeded the players on both sides worked hard for the winning ad- vantage. Birchall committed the offence of carrying the ball within his own area, and from the free kick Webb scored a doubtful goal. The home team won by four goals against Cros- field's three goals.</p>
        <p>Beamont United v. Ist Warrington Scouts.</p>
        <p>Beamont won the toss, and the Scouts kicked off facing the sun. Beamont soon became ag- gressive, and Heesom opened the scoring, which was quickly augmented by .a second from Bes- wick. Then Muliery twice scored, only to be ruled off-side. The interval arrived with the score: Beamont, two goals; Scouts, nil. Beamont re-opened with a dash, and Beswick scored. Then the Scouts rallied and scored, Conley, the Beamont goalkeeper, being hurt. The Scouts quickly added two more goals. Then Witton raced down the Beamont right and centred, for Heesom to score. Five min- utes from time Beswick added his third goal, and Mullery quickly added the sixth and last goal. Beamont eventually winning 6-3. _ _</p>
        <p>_ Conley, for Beamont, kept goal splendidly, and he forwards were in fine form, especially Beswick, who scored three goals. The Scouts played well, but wel4 weak at back and goal.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE SENIOR AMATEUR CUP TIE. Latchford's Big Engagement.</p>
        <p>In the above competition the Latchford Club have to-day to meet the Brooklands Club in the first round, on the ground of the Brooklands Club. •</p>
        <p>The Latchford Committee have selected their strongest team to do duty. Mr. .1. C. Bardsley, th-iarnOeur internationgl, is turning out for the first time, and Challinor has been dropped in favour of Haspell. •</p>
        <p>The following players are making the jour- ney, leaving Latchford at 1.18 p.M. : P. A. Kite; J, C. Bardsley and W. G. Robin- son; E. Downs, E. Webb (capt.) and .1. Carter; T. Smith, T. flaspell, J. Lees, P. Fogg:and W. Taylor. Reserves: H. Stubbs and &amp; Siddall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0321" n="art0321">
        <head>CHESHIRE AMATEUR CUP-TIE. First .Round. Stockton Heath v. L 111111 Amateurs.</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE AMATEUR CUP-TIE. First .Round. Stockton Heath v. L 111111 Amateurs.</p>
        <p>These teams mest at \Vest Avenue ground, Stockton Heath in the first round of the'Ama- teur Cup, and a good crowd of supporters wit- nessed a fast and pleasant game. The visitors won the toss, Jones, he ,home centre, kicking off. An attack was immediately made on the visitors' &amp;al, but the final' shot went wide of the mark. From the .goal kick the visitors' left carried the ball into the home quarters, only to be forced back by Gosling, wno mage 'a fine return.</p>
        <p>The ball was passed to Parr, who beat the opposing half-back and centred finely. Then Lockhart got possession, worked for position, and he beat Saville with a beauty, thus open- ing the score for the home team, after 15 min- utes' play. From the centre kick the visiting forwards met ,with a sound defence. Denny, robbing the visitors' centre, passed to Magill,- and he fed Lloyd admirably. The latter placed to Jones in a good position-. He fastened on to the ball and beat Saville with a grand shot from 30 yards-range. Nettled by these reverses, the visitors now played more determinedly, and caused Sim- cock to saye, on several occasions. The visit- ors would not be driven back, and the home half-back § fattered in their play, and allowed , Lees to break through. He made a grand pass to Smith, Who made no mistake with a shot which beat Simcock all the way. He had no chance with the Shot. After some good ex- changes in midfield,. the ball was carried into the visitors' quarters, and Lockhart scored from an off-side position, a feat which was repeated lby 'Jones shortly after. At half-time the home team was leading by two goals to one. On resuming, the home forwards became dangerous, and Lockhart caused „Saville to handle. From his clearance the visitors drib- bled the ball up the field, and they caught the home half-back line napping. Woodward, in- stead of kicking into touch, back heeled, and Richardson, getting the ball passed on to Leigh, who equalised with a rasping shpt. From the centre kick the ball went out to Lloyd, ' who beat the visitors' bb.cks, and Saville made a'splendid save. Off-side against the home team gave the visitors relief, and they took the ball to the opposite end, where the home goal had a lucky escape, the ball travelling across the goal mouth. Then the visiting forwards missed the opportunity Of gaining the lead. From the goal kick Denny did some fine work and pass- ing to Parr, he made a drive at goal, which shot Saville saved. With a long punt he set his own forwards going, when ,Magill robbed Harrison in neat fa§hion, and lie passed to Jones, who sent in a stinging shot, causing Saville to con- cede a ,corncr, which was well got away. Mid- field play followed up to the finish, and the score was two goals each.. Stockton Heath: Simeock ; Gosling and</p>
      </div>
