      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0333" n="art0333">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 4, 1913.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 4, 1913.</p>
        <p>Coast &amp; Country.</p>
        <p>WHERE TO STAY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0334" n="art0334">
        <head>VICTIMS OF THE ROAD.</head>
        <p>VICTIMS OF THE ROAD.</p>
        <p>Warrington Children Killed by</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0335" n="art0335">
        <head>Motor 'Bus and Tram.</head>
        <p>Motor 'Bus and Tram.</p>
        <p>FIRST FATAL ACCIDENTS.</p>
        <p>It. is not long ago that Alderman Smethurst (the chairman of the Tramways Committee) said, with evident pride, ili the Coun- cil Chamber, taat there had never been a fatal' accident since the trams were introduced in Warrington. Last week-end was a sad one, for on Friday a child was killed by one of the new motor 'buses, and on Monday a little boy met his death by running into a Win- wick-road tramcar. These first two fatalities in connection with the tramway and motor 'bus system have caused much grief in the town. Coroner S. Brighouse conducted the inquests at his Warrington -Court on Tuesday, the Mayor (Dr. Joseph) and Alderman Smethurst. attending. The first inquiry was on the bodY of Mabel Hall (7), who lived with her grandmother, Mary Wright, 43, Bewsey-street, and was knbeked down by a Bewsey circular 'bus on Friday. Mary Wright, the grandmother, said, she had been separated from her husband, Job Wright for some years. The deceased was the daughter of William and Eliza Hall, but she (witness) had not seen or heard of the parents for four years. She had brought the child up since it was a month old. About 4.50 on Friday the child went out to play with Some more children. At five o'clock witness was told that the child had met with an accident and. had been taken to the Infirmary. She was afterwards informed that the child had died.</p>
        <p>"No Chance to Pull Up."</p>
        <p>William Packwood, horse driver, in the employ of the London and North-Western Railway Co., and residing at 12, Bissell-street, said that on Friday afternoon, about five o'clock, he was driving a horse and lurry out of Brown-street, into Bewsey-street, when the Corporation 'bus passed, going in the direction of Horsemarket- street. He noticed several children playing on the pavement at the near side of the road, oppo- site Cheshyres-lane end. Suddenly the deceased ran practically underneath the 'bus, giving the driver no chance to pull up. The rear wheel passed over the upper part of the body'. The 'bus was about three yards from the pavement at the time of the accident, and was not travel- ling at more than five miles per hou.E. • The driver sounded his horn after passing him (wit- ness). He was 20 yards to the rear at the time of the accident, hut had a clear view. The Coroner: Do you think the driver could have done any niore than he did? Witness: No. The Foreman:. I was there and can confirm that.</p>
        <p>harry Mason, 8. Parr-street, the driver of the 'bus, said that when he reached the corner of Brown-street he almost stopped the 'bus to allow a milk cart to pass across in front of him. After proceeding about 15 yards the child walked on the left-hand side of the footpath. about four to five yards in front of him.</p>
        <p>. Suddenly Stepped Forward. The 'bus would be 'about one and a half yards from the kerbstone. The girl suddenly turned off the pavement, 'right in front of the 'bus. lie tried to swerve to the right, but the wheel passed over her back. Ile pulled up in about eight yards. He was travelling at about four miles per hour. _ The Foreman: The driver did his utmost. _</p>
        <p>The Coroner: Children of this age are full of vivacity. They don't move slowly. I pass a school every morning and the children run into me right and left as they come out. They can- not help it, for they are made that way. The Foreman: They don't seem to realise danger. The Coroner: No, they don't. The jury returned a *erdict of "Accident death," and exonerated the driver from all blame. Alderman Smethurst said: On behalf of the Tramways Committee and myself, as chairmau, I am sure we express our deep sympathy with those who should be so unfortunate as to lose this little girl. ram sure they have the sym- pathy of everyone in the town. Ido not want to distress you, because I feel almost as much as if it were my own child. I hope and trust God will bless you and give you strength to bear this deep affliction. As the Coroner has said, children will be children, and it is very difficult indeed to guide them. Only to-day I myself was coming home to my dinner when the school was loosing, and I saw a boy about ten years old swinging and climbing on the back of a cart, along with other boys. . This boy gof his foot fast in the middle of the spring, and it was only just in time that a gentleman stopped the cart and got the boy out. Giving another instance of the anxiety which the tramway men, and more particularly the motor men, had, he said: The trams are confined to the lines, but the motor 'buses went here and there and every- where. Only this morning my wife, who was riding in the motor 'bus, said that there might have been another fatal accident. As it hap- pened the driver pulled. up in time, • and the child of three years was given a good smacking, which someone else should do. I era sure that a few words from the head teachers to the boys and girls, telling them not to run after tram- cars and lurries, would do good.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0336" n="art0336">
        <head>" The Last Post."</head>
        <p>" The Last Post."</p>
        <p>To show the difficulties there are. In War- rington, unfortunately, children have got a game they call "Last Post.' I have not seen it, but a gentleman, who is a manager of one of our large works, has seen it done many a time. Children will sit on the kerb-stone and when they see a 'bus or a motor car coming they will set themselves the task who shall run the most times across the road before the 'bus or car gets past. The last boy to pass gets a cigarette card. I hope the people of the town will try to help us in this matter. This is the first fatal acci- dent We have had since the commencement of the tramway system, ten or eleven years ago. 'The Mayor (Dr. Joseph) expressed his Per- sonal regret and that of the Corporation. No more words could come from him at present. The Coroner: We all appreciate the fact that Alderman Smethurst and the Mayor have pub- licly expressed their sympathy. It is very nice of them to do so, and if I was in the place of the grandmother I should appreciate it very much. rn the extraordinary circumstances she must be intensely grieved. Referring to what Alderman Smethurst had said, the Coroner remarked: I think a remon- strance from the teachers will not have much effect upon boys from what I can remember when I was a boy. Boys will still climb walls and ladders, run behind carts and cabs so long as human nature is human nature. After all, we don't like to see children who are milksops. I like to see lads with. a bit of devil in them, for those Were the boys who grew up to be soldiers and sailors. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0337" n="art0337">
        <head>TRAM FATALITY.</head>
        <p>THE TRAM FATALITY.</p>
        <p>How An Accident Occurred.</p>
        <p>The second inquest was on the body of George Henry Dalglish (21), 11, Clare-stret, who was killed by the tramcar. The mother, Martha Dalglish, said that about four o'clock on Monday she was standing at the corner of Buckley-street and 'Winwick-road, talking to Mrs. Stokes (her sister) and another woman. Witness's child. was playing close by with her sister's child .and another one. The other two children crossed the road, and her boy followed and almost got to the footpath, when witness noticed a lurry coming down Win- wick-road towards Warrington. Witness's sister put the child on the footpath when the lurry ,had passed, and the next thing witness knew was that her boy had been knocked down by a tramcar. He was taken to the Infirmary. She thought that her sister, after nutting the boy on the footpath, went to see her own child, and that witness's boy followed her and was knocked down. The lurry ob- scured witness's view of the accident.</p>
        <p>Catherine Stokes, the dead child's aunt, cor- roborated.</p>
        <p>Found Beneath Car.</p>
        <p>The driver of the Car, William Crozier Baxen- 'dale, 7, Rosewood-avenue, Padgate-lane, said he was driving the four o'clock car from the Cen- tral Station, and when opposite Messrs. Waring's Tannery two lurries passed him. When passing (Continued in next colvmn.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0338" n="art0338">
        <head>MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>Little Interest so far Aroused in the Coming Contests.</p>
        <p>THE WHITECROSS WARD.</p>
        <p>So far little interest has been aroused in the forthcoming municipal elections, but next week may witness sonic developments. , The retiring candidates are :—Town Hall Ward, Councillor Lindley (L); Whitecross Ward, Councillor Hawthorn (L); Bewsey Ward, Councillor Skelland (L); Orford Ward, Council- lor Wilkinson (L); St. John's Ward, Councillor Dr. McKee (C); Fairfield Ward, Councillor Locker, (L); Howley Ward, Councillor Archer (C); St. Austin's Ward, Councillor Broadhurst (C); Latchford Ward, Councillor Henshall (I).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0339" n="art0339">
        <head>COUNCILLOR HAWTHORN INVITED TO</head>
        <p>COUNCILLOR HAWTHORN INVITED TO</p>
        <p>STAND.</p>
        <p>Popular Whiteeross Member.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the 'Whitecross Liberal Ward ,Committee ,was held on Wednesday evening. Here was a large attendance, presided over by Mr. T. Dale. Councillor J. Hawthorn said three years ago he advocated better means of communication in the ward, and now they had a splendid motor 'bus service. When the .two additional 'buses arrived, an extra service would be prOvided, particularly on market days. Referring to the £16,000 scheme for the ex- tension of the electricity department, he said the works in the tOwn were demanding more and more electric power each year. This brings me to another important point, he added. We hear a good deal just now about the smbke nuisance. Well, I believe one of the remedies for that lies in an extensioh\ of the electricity works. It may be visionary, but I believe the time will come when most of the works in the town will obtain their power 'supply from the Corporation.</p>
        <p>Smoke Stacks Unnecessary.</p>
        <p>. When that time arrives there Will be no neces- sity to have scores of chimney stacks belching forth their columns of , smoke, but the supply will be centralised at our electricity works, where we shall be able to adopt the best appli- ances to mitigate the smoke nuisance.</p>
        <p>Already the committee had done something in this direction, and considering the great power supply we are now maintaining, the smoke from the erec.tricity works is compara- tively small, in fact smaller than at most works in toe town. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>. Slot Meter Question.</p>
        <p>On general questions affecting the town, I think you will agree with me that the time has come when we ought to make some concessions to consumers of gas by slot meter. Consider- ing the profits made by the Gas Department and the price of gas to ordinary gas consumers, I think the Corporation would be doing a right and a fair thing if they gave a larger supply of gas for a penny than they do at present. I admit that the question is one of some diffi- culty, for there are certain expenses in regard to slot meters, such as collecting the money and providing the meters, which do not obtain in regard to ordinary meters, but notwithstanding %Ills, I think the slot meter users have been very patient, and are entitled to the sympathetic and generous 'treatment of the Gas Committee.</p>
        <p>The Ne‘.v .Bridge.</p>
        <p>The completion of •dne-half of the new bridge across the Mersey marks a new era in the his- tory of Warrington Bridge. There have been considerable delays—longer than many of us liked, or ever anticipated—but there is reason to believe that within the next twelve months we shall see the completion of a structure which will be a credit to the town and one that will meet the needs of the public for many years to come. - -</p>
        <p>Another matter which looms ahead of us is the provision of new municipal offices. Some of the officials of the Corporation are accom- modated at (1) Town Hall; (2) Bank House, San- key-street ; (3) Education Offices; (4) Gas Offices, Mersei-Street.</p>
        <p>The Town Hall Question.</p>
        <p>The time is coming when we shall be obliged to face the question of whether the time has not arrived when the Corporation should centralise all these offices in one building, to be erected on land, in Sankey-street. We have land at the rear of Bank House and ale Education Office in Sankey-street, and I believe the proposal to erect a commodious building there ha § advan- tages which cannot be claimed for another pro- posal %which has been made, to enlarge the Cown Hall.</p>
        <p>_ This historic building has arcliitectural features which ought not to be destroyed by ltering it, or transforming it into something a for which it was never intended, and is ill- suited for.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Councillor Roberts, seconded by Councillor Peacock, Mr. lwthorn was in- vited to stand as candidate, and the meeting pledged itself to secure his return by a triumph- ant majority.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0340" n="art0340">
        <head>This was carried unanimously.</head>
        <p>This was carried unanimously.</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR SKELLIND INVITED.</p>
        <p>The flewsey Ward Vacancy. A meeting of the Liberal Executive Committee of the Bewsey • Ward was held on Thursday evening. Councillor Dickenson presided. The Chairman proposed, and Mr. C. E. W. Nurse seconded, the following resolution, which was carried unanimously : "That we desire to express our sincere thanks to Councillor Skelland for the most efficient way in which he discharged the duties devolved upon him as our representative on the Warrington Town Council, and this meeting again invites him to contest the ward at the forthcoming election, and .pledges itself to use every legitim- ate means to -secure his return by a triumphant majority."</p>
        <p>This resolution, as in the case of the other two wards above, will be submitted to a later meet- ing of the general committees for approval.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0341" n="art0341">
        <head>TOWN HALL WARD.</head>
        <p>TOWN HALL WARD.</p>
        <p>At a largely attended meeting of the Town Hall Ward Liberal Committee last evening, Mr. O'Brien proposed that Councillor Lindley be invited to contest the ward. He paid a high tribute to Mr. Lindley's work on the Council. The resolution was carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0342" n="art0342">
        <head>VICTIMS OF THE ROAD—cont.</head>
        <p>VICTIMS OF THE ROAD—cont.</p>
        <p>the end of the second lurry he heard a scream, which he thought came from a neighbouring entry. Thinking, however, that something was wrong, he applield the emergency brake. On looking round hd saw a little boy underneath the car. He picked him up and stopped a motor car, which conveyed him to the Infirmary. He did not see the child come in the way, but thought it came from behind the second lurry. He believed the pilot board crushed it. The Coroner said he thought there was a " catcher " underneath the car.</p>
        <p>Witness said he thought the car itself must have struck the boy, and that he approached, not from the• front, but the side. A child of that age was very small and would soon be knocked down.</p>
        <p>Detective-Sergeant Holland said from inquiries he made there was nothing to indicate the driver was in any way to blame. He was driv- ing carefully, and was a careful man. The jury found that death was accidental and that the driver was in no way to blame.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-10-04_WidnesExaminer_art0343" n="art0343">
